      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>PLEASANT PiI.R,AGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PiI.R,AGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A GENTLEMAN said to a dilatory waiter: "Aro you the lad who took my plate for this beef!" "Yea, sir." "Bless me, how you have grown." FED: "What do you think of my argument!" Will: "Sound—most certainly sound." : " Fred : "And what else!" Will : "Nothing else—merely</p>
        <p>sound." KINDLY OLD GRNT : "Well, my little man, what would you like to be when you grow up?" Little Man: "I'd like to be a nice gentleman like you, with nothin' to do but walk around and ask ques- tions." A YOUNG woman sent to a newspaper a poem entitled "I Cannot Make Him Smile," and was ranch displeased when the editor sent it back with &amp; line saying that she would probably succeed if &amp;he showed him the poem. ELLA : "How could you tell that your husband " lost money at the races to-day, when he said nothing about it?" Stella: "I know he did, because when he came home he began talking about the advantages of saving money." COLD COMFORT —Unsuccesful Suitor: "Am 180 very obnoxious to you?" Miss Freezem : " N-o ; I can imagine circumstances under which I might six-p niy• arms about your neck very gladly." "Thank Heaven ! Under what circumstances?" "If I were drowning, and you were near." MOST Dsclnantr.—Jinks : "I don't believe that &amp; critic leads half of the author's book he crit- kites." B.nks "Tue author is more considerate. He reads every word of the critic's criticism. GRNErINE v"YMPATIIY.— "Oh, Oi'm sorry t' hear ye've !prose yure leg, Bor. Which leg was it?" Mr. Home: "The right one, Pat." Pat: "Phwhat &amp; pity it wasn't the lift, !" TAKING THE AGENT DOWN.—" This book," said the went, " will cost you one dollar." "I'll bet you it won't," said Dixon. "You can't get it for less" "That •may' be; but it won't cost me a because I'm not going to buy it. A YOUNG lady said to her dressmaker: "If women are ever allowed to vote, what do you sup- pose will lie the fas ion for voting dresses ? " Tun ell ii.or wrote: "The showers last week, though copious. were not sufficient to meet the wants of the milhuen." The compositor set it up "milkmen." MANAGER : "That young nephew of yours is a sleepy sort, of fellow. 'What shall Ido with him?" Merchant : " Sne if you can't find room for him in the night-shirt department." Titling is nothing more aggravating to a woman with a secret than to meet people with no curiosit y. KNowt coon IS WEALTH. —Druggist : "You :night have charged that young man five shillings for tilling that prescription. Why did you put the price at one shillieg ' Clerk: "He understands</p>
        <p>Ts.roux : "Hang it all, Marie, that confounded poodle of yours ha.s bitten a piece clean out of my leg." Mrs. Tangle: "Ott, how very annoying, Henry, when poor Fib o is ill, and the doctor said that he mustn't have a bit a meat for at least two weeks." "What a lovely morning,'' he said. "It is a perfect morning," she replied. " Troe," he said. "I haven't seen anything this morning that isn't perfect." And he looked her straight in the face. Then she blushed. Gas de Smith: "Do you know my father, Miss Birdie ? " Birdie: "I never met him, but I believe he is a very modest, unassuming sort of man." Gus de Smith: "Right you are. You can get some kind of an idea of how unostentatious he is when I tell you he does not brag about having me for a son." SOLDIERS IN RED.—It is stated that the Spartan troops were uniformly clothed in red, by the direc- tions of Lyeurgus, in order to prevent the soldiers from perceiving their loss of blood, or the enemy from discovering their wounds. Query: Is that the reason why the British soldiers dress in red? MR. LORENZO DAY has been lately married to Miss Martha Week: _</p>
        <p>A Day is made, a Week is lost, But time should not complain : There'll soon be little Days enough To make the Week again. A TEACHER had been explaining to his class the points of the compass, and all were drawn up in front towards the north. "Now, what is before you, John?" "The north, sir." "And what behind you, Tommy?" "My coat tail, sir," said he, trying at the some time to get a glimpse. "Tun death of your husband was a great loss to you," said Mrs. Bunting to Mrs. Gasket, by way of condolence. "Oh, the loss was fully covered by insurance," repled the widow. PRATTLE : "You don't seem to have the cour- age of your convictions." Mrs Prattle: "I should like to know how to get at that conclusion?" Prattle : "You say there's no use talking, and then you talk for hours." ONE ON HICKS. —" Some people are much more fortunate in their marriages than others," sneered flicks. "That's the only reason why I don't con- sider matrimony a failure." "You are very right there," said Mrs. Hicks. "Here in our own family you got me, but I—l only got you." FATHER-IN-LW: "I am ruined; all is lost ! " Son-in-Law: " Ahem ! Then I married for love, after all !" Hoxvd. CLERK (to new by) : "Did you wake up No. 44!" Boy: "No, sir, cuddent wake him up, sir; but I did the neareat I could, sir." Hotel Clerk "What was that?' Boy: "I waked up No. 45, sir." OLD GENTLEMAN: "What would you like to be when you grow up?" Boy: "I'd like to be a .bricklayer.' "That's a commendable ambition. Why would you like to be a bricklayer ? " •"'Cause there's so many days when bricklayers -can't work." "WHERE if! the island of Java situated?" asked a school-teacher of a small, rather forlorn-looking boy. "I dunno, sir." "Don't you know where coffee comes from?" "Yes, sir; we borrows it from the next-door neighbour." A RECENT writer asserts that the less a man knows the wider he carries his mouth open. He says it is as impossible for an ignoramus to keep his jaw closed as it is for a sick oyster to keep his shell shut. The following story of a suit for breach of pro- mise, tried some years ago, was recently told by a relative of the defendant: The case began to look very much as if it could be won by the fair plain- tiff. when one morning, as the friend came down the street, he observed the attorney for the defence waving his hat and wearing a happy expression of countenance. As he came near, the attorney ex- claimed. "We have beaten them! We've beaten them ! "How so ?" queried the relative, wondering what new phase the case could have assumed. "She died last night," joyfully replied the attorney. SMITH: "I once possessed a splendid dog, who could always distinguish between a vagabond and a respectable person." Jones: "Well, what's be- come of him? " Smith; "Oh, I was obliged to give him away He bit me.'' A SYMPATHETIC FRIEND.—George: "Jack, old boy, I'm so glad I ran across you. Never needed your friendship more. I'm in love with the brlle of the season, and I promised her a sail to—lay, but I had a run of bad luck last night, and haven't a cent left." Jack : "Too bad. "Yes. I don't care for myself, you know; but it's such a pity that a charming creature like that should be dis- appointed. You have a little money to spare, haven't you?" "Oh, plenty. Make yourself easy my dear boy. She shan't be disappointed. take her myself." "CHARLEY, dear," said the rustic young girl, 4' will you love me as now and c ing to me always ?" "Yes, darling," replied the matter-of-fact lover; "I'll stick to you as tight as those freckles on your nose." DON'T TRUST TO APPEARANCE. —Do not mistake a man for a fool because he looks simple. If you do, you may find yourself the fool at the end. WEAR AND TEAR.—Wife: "T at last set of china is the best we ever had " Husband: " U'm ! It's all gone, isn't it?" Wife : Yes; but just think, dear. It lasted four girls and a half." " CHARLEY belongs to a splendid club," said young Mrs. Tooker. 'lndeed?" said the visitor. "Yes. They have some arrangement by which a member has to pay a certain sum into the treasury if he doesn't mind his grammar. I'm not sure, but I think that's the way it • Did your husband fell you about it?" Not all shout it, but he said that while he was playing billiards last night his bad English cost him five shillings.</p>
