      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Concerning the projected visit of the Earl of Rosebery to Canada, I hear that the ex-Premier has written to tne Earl of Aberdeen postponing his trip until after the Canadian general elections, which are expected to be held in November or December. Lord Rosebery will probably leave England about Christmas, and after a tour of some length in the Dominion will return about the middle of March.</p>
        <p>It is useless to abuse the the Liberal officials in London for not attending to registration as care- fully as their Conservative opponents. It is entirely a question of funds. Every London Conservative member and candidate pays from £lOO to £.500 a year for registration work. Mr. Ritchie lort his seat for St. George's because he would not comply with this rt-quirement, and in fact, no Conservative candidate is now even recommended for accept- ance unless he consents to incur the liability. A house-to-house canvass for registration purposes cannot be done for less than £l5O in any division The City members get off best, for they pay lest than £lOO each, the Association finding the rest.</p>
        <p>Now that the railway race is over, the competing companies are counting up the csst of their record runs. A writer in a railway paper says—referring to the London and Nnrth-Western Company—that every link in the vast machinery regulating the traffic was put out of joint. The working time- table was suspended. Orders were issued to every official, to every man concerned, that no train what- ever, no matter what its urgency, must be allowed to interfere in the smallest degree with the flight of the racing express. As a result ot all this the line from end to end was simply choked with i traffic. Live stock, fruit, vegetables, and other \urgent goods were kept waiting in all directions, and it is computed that the company will have to pay thousands of pounds for every day of the race as compensation for delay and damage to goods.</p>
        <p>Perhaps if a peer or two lost their lives by the dangers resulting from the use of cheap petroleum lamps, the Home Secretary would be less ready to defer much-needed legislation on this subject until there has been the further delay of, inquiry before a Select Parliamentary Committee. A London coroner puts the number of accidents in a year from the use of one particularly vicious lamp at three hundred. A roll of death and injury thus caused rivals that recorded in many occupations scheduled at the Home Office as so dangerous as to need prompt in- terference. Why is zeal for the preservation of human life confined to factories and not extended to domestic interiors?</p>
        <p>Here is a somewhat hard nut to be cracked by those who protest against the Indian cotton duties as protective in essence and destructive of panca- shire trade in effect. The first result of the impo- Qition of these duties naturally enough caused a temporary falling off in Lancashire exports to India; but it is now evident that the shrinkage was transitory, not permanent, for the official figures show that there has since been a recovery to more than the normal volume—a fact which "makes hay" of the contention that the duties are protective.</p>
        <p>The fiasco of the America Cup race having passed out of the bounds of sober criticism, is exercising the ingenuity of those who are anxious to have an international race in the home waters. The latest proposal, born of recent success in another depart- ment of sport, is that a shilling subscription should be started for the purchase of El Dunraven Cup. It would certainly he a well-merited compliment to the proprietor of Valkyrie if the idea were taken up. as the contests in New York Bay have shown that nearly everybody in the empire takes an interest in yacht racing. It is one of the few sports that have not been corrupted, and anything that would foster the spirit of emulation upon what we nave been taught to regard as our own ep,cial preserve ought to be welcomed. The shilling subscription is a trifl,. bizarre, and some less sensational method may be fund for giving effect to the idea. Many prizes are already offered, but one that originated out of the America controversy would have irresistible attractions.</p>
        <p>-- There are rumours that the Home Secretary may have to make an important appointment at Scot- land Yard. Although the stal ement that there are serious disaffections in all ranks of the police is an exaggeration of the facts, it is, I hear on excellent authority, quite true that the heads •of some of the departments are at loggerheads, and there is much talk in the service of the probably resigna- tion of a high official,whose place Sir Matthew Rid- ley would have to fill.</p>
        <p>It is assumed that the testimonial to Mr. Middle- ton, the wire-puller in chief to the Tory party, will assume the form of a cheque, that being the most acceptable gift. But the accompanying address will be presented at a dinner, the date of which will probably coincide as nearly as possible with the reassembling of Parliament. The dinner is likely to be a very big affair, and it is anticipated that Mr. Arthur Balfour will consent to preside.</p>
        <p>For a prince reputed to be rich, Kbama is " do- ing" this country on strictly economical lines. He is staying at a cheap hotel, and since he has been in London he has walked about armed with an um- brella to guard against the risk of being compelled to seek refuge from a shower in a cab. He has even patronised the penny 'bus. When the party of African rulers and their English interpreters and guides went to Brighton they drove to the station,not in regal state, but in cabs, and occupied a second-class compartment in the train. At Brigh- t,n they appeared upon the sea-front in all the Western glories of frock coats and well-polished silk hats. Needless to say they were the objects of much attention. They seemed, however, to care littlb for the curious glances of the crowd, but marched along with an air of stolid indifference.</p>
        <p>When our American visitors come next year.those of them who have not yet seen us, they will find in all probability yet another Dickens landmark gone. Within the past few days the lower windows of the Old Ship in Chichester Rents, on the western side of Chancery-lane, have been boarded up. The oh] place is the Old Sols Arms mentioned in " Bleak House" as the scene ot the inquest. On the first floor, with the windows giving on to the "Rents," may be seen the long low room in which the coroner presided, and opposite is poor Miss Flite's dwelling, where from the windows of her attic she could see the roof of the old hall of Lincoln's Inn.</p>
        <p>The " New York Mail and Express," in mention- ing that the "Century Magazine" has purchased the serial rights of Mrs. Humphrey Ward's new story adds :—" Those unsucccessful aspirants for literary honours who constantly complain of the poverty ' of authorship may be interested to know that a sum almost fabulous is said to have been paid by the magazine. It is reported that for magazine and book rights combined Mrs. Ward will receive anywhere from 50,000d01a to 75,000d015. for this one novel "—that is to say, from £lO,OOO to 215.000.</p>
        <p>An illustration of the literary tendencies of miners is given by a competition that has been run- ming in the " New Age.' Prizes were awarded for the beat essay on "My Working Day," and more papers were received from miners than from any other people. The prize-winner mentions in his essay that more certificates for science and art are taken by Northumberland and Durham than by the whole of London, It is quite common to work side by side with a man who has a certificate for a man- ager's situation and also for surveying.</p>
        <p>I hear that colonists and others wishing to join the Collectivist colony ofJopotobampo, blexico,may do so under present conditions of paying ten dollars for a share in the Company, ten dollars for lot or land use, and thirty dollars for the commissary fund. The last is levied to enable persons to draw supplies for credit until the labour of the colonist produces articles or farm products for exchange. An intending colonist must equip himself with wearing apparel, bedding, and household goods,and must give an undertaking that he will abide by the regulations made in conformity with the principles of association, and, though he has a special trade. I will engage in any other occupation for which he may be fitted and in which the colony may require work done. If, moreover, he would get food he roue work and both geod standing in the colony.</p>
        <p>To MuTIIEft9.—ISIrs.Wi slow s Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immedlitely. It is pleasant to taste; ft produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the tile cherub awakes "as bright as a button." Of all hemiate,lll. ltd. per bottle.-4dol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY Dasursr Nar-raz,</p>
        <p>Dear me ! Summer is nearly over, and very soon our flimsy blouses and summer materials will have to be discarded in favour of warmer costumes. So I will tell you to-day a little about the coming autumn costumes. Tweeds are very popular, while the smartest tailor-made gowns are of beaver cloth. The best colours are Lincoln and water- cress green, the rich reddish tint known as "sail- brown," from the fact that it is the exact colour of a fishing-boat sail, a new darker brown, termed "rosewood," and Guards' blue, the latter being chiefly used in combination with cream, light tan, or Guards' red cloth. Coats will be short during the coming season. Tailor-made skirts are being made of a sensible length; that is to say, they clear the ground by about three inches all round. Here is a very smart and servicable costume, and its advantage is that the coat is adapted to almost any degree of temperature. On a hot day it may be worn over a cool piquo; waistcoat, or, if un- buttoned, over a silk or cambric vest, while as the autumn advances, the proper cloth waistcoat will be stylish and comfortable, and. when winter, bleak and hoary, makes his appearance, the vest may be a warm long-sleeved one, with front of velvet, plush or sealskin. My model was of Lincoln-green cloth, with a waistcoat of bright tan cloth, though a little later in the year this might alternate with a waistcoat of bright red velvet, with white silk spots. The shape of the coat is very like that of last year. The sleeves are, of course, full above the elbow, and the coat reaches just below the waist. The basque must be quite flat in front and full on the hips, while at the back it is fashioned in two box-pleats.</p>
        <p>Turning to indoor garments I may remind you that black crepon makes the most useful gowns imaginable for smart occasions. It is as well to have the bodice and skirt made separately, so that the latter can be worn with a coloured Mum occasionally to effect a change. Deep lace collars, or yokes, with epaulettes attached, make a pretty trimming for black bodices. Braces of coloured satin ribbon, with a waistband and collar to match, relieve the sombre appearance of the black crepon. White silks with narrow stripes, or cross lines of black, are considered very chic. I must tell you of a stylish double-breasted long wrap. It has revers turned back with 4cru guipure work, which is carried round the back of the neck. A godet cape is apparently thrown low on the shoulders, and finished off with a shaped collar, open at the centre of the back, and outlined with a thickly pleated niche of pinked-out silk, corres- ponding with the binding and the large buttons. The sailor hat is in yellow straw, encircled with a</p>
        <p>band of white moire ribbon, which is secured at the side under folds and clasp in paste diamonds. There is an aigrette of a single large black quill. Gradually, if slowly, the patchwork style of dress is departing and we are coming back to the idea of wearing complete dresses of a single fabric. There has lately been given a fresh impetus—if one were wanted—to the popularity of black and white, and a sweet dress is exhibited by an old London firm made entirely of narrow black and white striped glace silk with small black chine figures printed all over it. The cape was white silk, embroidered with black in a very delicate design and edged with the silk of the dress, and rather a wide piece of this black embroidery on white silk was let into each sleeve. The neckband and shell-like arrangement at the throat were pale pink Esse, as were also the wristlets, and the bonnet or toque was trimmed with tufts of double pink-shaded daisies with a black aigrette and black pleated lisse. As to hats, very fashionable ones are being worn now, as I think I told you, of velvet edged with fancy straw. However, many stylish ones are made entirely of straw. Here is one of shot</p>
        <p>C straw, with black tips, citron igiand tinted osprey. Your loving Jincuts.</p>
        <p>:R'S LITTLE Tica :(7.;a101 - PILLS. I IFUL TEETH L'oxe. for all who use daily on the tooth brush few drape oi</p>
