      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 21. 1895. GLENALPINE'S OATH;</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 21. 1895. GLENALPINE'S OATH;</p>
        <p>The widows of Paris, when riding bicycles, wear crape " bloomers." The nerves of warm-blooded animals teleuraph infor- mation to their brains at the rate of about one hundred and fifty feet per second.</p>
        <p>To remove the strong flavour from poultry, the fowl should be washed first in soda, rinsed in cold water, and wiped dry. Five barbers in Paris make a livelihood by shaving dogs. Some of the dogs have the forward part of the body shaved, some the rear,while others are ornamented in six or seven stripes.</p>
        <p>There is a craze -amongst ladies in America just now for whist, and several professional players are earning a good deal of money by going round and giving series of lessons in the game.</p>
        <p>In China the minuter pearls are often used, ground into dust, as a medicine. It is believed to be a specific for sore eyes, and, indeed, in the higher pharmacopce a, powdered pearls is a constant item of the prescrip- tion.</p>
        <p>Out of the thirteen million pounds worth of foreign manufactured silks purchased by England last year, eleven million pounds worth were for home consump- tion, and only a little less than two million pounds worth for exportation. The starfish kills the oyster by enveloping it closely in its arms, then,placing his mouth to the crevice of t ho shell,he injects a very acrid and venomous juice wit' The poor oyster, disgusted by the poison, opens his shell to admit water and so rid himself of it, and thus falls a prey to the destroyer. The growing demand for small dogs is said to have developed a new industry in Paris. The pups, when a few hours old, are said to be placed largely on alcohol diet, which is gradually increased. This prevents their growth, and after a generation the Liliputian animal is more easily obtained. The Empress of Austria Is apparently making a col- lection of cows. From every foreign place at which she stays for any length of time she brings one home. All these cows are sent to the Royal farmyard, and the Empress orders milk from this favourite or that, as the fancy takes her. " Should there be more than the usual'grain of truth," writes a correspondent, "in the reports of the engage- ment of Princess Maud of Wales to Prince Chri flan of Denmark, the not unosnal union of a very tall husband and a very short wife will be seen in the distinguished couple. The young Prince is six feet two irwhes in height ; Princess Maud is very little over five feet. There is no European country in which women clerks are more employed than in France. Indeed, it is rare to enter a French shop and find a man serving as an ac- countant. Book keepers are paid from £4O to £l2O year, and accountants much the same. In the commer- cial houses, where women-clerks are also employed, they often have an interest in the business.</p>
        <p>Berlin has a shorthand writer with a unique speciality. He attends all funerals of prominent persons and takes flown verbatim the addresses of the officiating clergy- men. Then he prepares highly ornamented copies of addresses, and sells them to the friends of the eulogised dead. His businesa ia so good that he has taken one as- sistant, and has advertised for another.</p>
        <p>Rocking chairs of the styles prevailing nowadays are believed to have been invented in the seventh century. They are mentioned by Vener able Bede. "The women are so luxurious that they do have chairs with wooden circles on the legs and which sway back and t orth in such sort that it maketh one sick to behold them.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Mr. Gladatone's Grandaughter.</head>
        <p>Mr. Gladatone's Grandaughter.</p>
        <p>Opinion is very much at variance as to hew far the human system may be hardened by extreme hygienic methods, without jeopardy to health. But in Mr. Glad- stone's little granddaughter, Dorothy Drew, we have a good example of desirable results follwaing on drastic treatment. The little maid always goes barefoot,indoors end out, except in very muddy or bitterly cold weather. Her dress and fare are of the simplest, and she is one of the healthiest, happiest, and most irrepressible of chil- dren. If Mrs. aew leads in educating her child in a more natural way why should not others follow ? There are multitudes of children of wealthy parents who would I be benefited by a similar treatment.</p>
        <p>Eyebrows for the Million. An ingenious ladies' barber has discovered a method of beautifying eyelashes and eyebrows where these raeeful features are deficient. Instead of painting them is the vulgar style of old, he puts the genuine article there, and professional and amateur beauties of London are flocking to him to be made lovely. The operat- ing is said to be extremely delicate,though by no means painful. He takes a hair from the head of the beauty, or, if she does not like precisely the colour of her own hair he takes one of any other colour that she likes, threads an extremely fine needle with the bair, runs it along inside the skin of the eyelid,sewing sailmaker's f as- hion,but leaving the loops long sufficiently to enable him to cut them afterwards,tio that they will form a range of beautiful fringe and look perfectly natural. For eye- brows he does the same thing ; but the eyebrow opera- tion is, of course,less delicate. Arched eyebrows, bushy eyebrows, straight eyebrows, crooked eyebrows, in any colour or shade or form, this wonderful perfumer makes for the ladies of London ; and it is said that his success is something astonishing. We are of opinion, however, that such a barbarous operation would be conducted with mere safety by a skilled surgeon. Aseptic tools are an essential, and we do not usually trust barbers in this direction.</p>
        <p>English and French. In a recent number of the " itevue de Paris," M. Filon, a wrll-known authority on all that concerns England and English literature, devotee a long article to a com- parison between English women and French—in favour of the latter. Mr. Filon has examined the English divorce and suicide registers, but he found that though Englishwomen often kill themselves for love, they very rarely put an end to their existence throueh re-verty. "In England, divorce is a luxury—an access •Ty to the existence only of the wealthy; that is why there is only one divorce for every five hundred and seventy-seven marriages, and in France, one divorce to seventy-eight marriages, and in Paris, one divorce to every thirteen marriages! And yet," says the French writer, "nowhere is marriage as an institution more attacked, both from the inside and outside, than in Englond," and to prove this he quotes Sydney Grundy's "New Woman."" The Notorious Mrs. Ebbsmith," and Grant Allen's " Woman who Did," which hook he describes as being " noble and sad, treating chastely a delicate ques- tion." M. Filon does not approve of long engagements. "I have often seen two young people united at the altar who had already become mortally sick of one another." He also criticises severely our large families : " When the children are young there is a nurse and under-nurse, as they grow older they are sent off to a distant school, only coming back for the holidays. As for the children of the people, they grow up as they can, in the gutter and the street. With us (that is in France) not unfrequently a man and his wife, who have begun by being indifferent to each other, will become truly united owing to their love for their children; " and he proceeds to give a roseate ac- count of what continental matrimony really implies. "In France, marriages are I think seldom made through love. Once married, with what energy the young couple work with a view of building a nest for their little ones. The wife is as often as not an excellent business woman ; she is frequently superior to her husband, and ends by becoming master. During the marriage ceremony the man promises to protect and the woman promises to obey, but we all know bow many husbands obey their wives and how many wives pro- tect their husbands 1" And then he continues : "I have never seen anything of the kind among the middle- class Englishwomen ; they know rather how to spend than to save or make money; few of them know how to sew, still fewer how to cook ; their rooms are un- tidy, and they are at the mercy of their servants ;rarely are they to be fouad in their' home,sweet home.' They spend their days shopping, or seeing their friends, and walking in the park."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WORLD'S WEALTH OF DIAMONDS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD'S WEALTH OF DIAMONDS.</p>
        <p>There is always something fascinating about the sub- ject of diamonds, and rich and 'poor like to read about precious stones. There are 4,000 manufacturers in Eur- ope, and about 200 in the United States, who employ be- tween 7,000 ana 8,000 as cutters and polishers. Perhaps 28,000 people are employed in the diamond mines throughout the world. We read that in past centuries 60,000 people were working in some single Indian mines at one time, and perhaps that statement is not exagger- ated, since by the aid of modern machinery one miner can now accomplish as much as twenty who used the primitive methods. The total value of all the diamonds in the world undoubtedly exceeds £200,000,000. There are perhaps 8,000 dealers in diamonds in the world who carry in their stock stones worth perhaps £70,000,000. The remainder are in the hands of private individuals. To compare present con- ditions with those of the past, it is in- structive to note the enormous increase in the production of diamonds, and the important changes wrought thereby,which have resulted from the discovery and working of the great South African mines. During the past quarter century, ten tons of diamonds, selling for more than £80,000,000 uncut, and £120,000,000 after cutting, have been added to the world's wealth—an amount more than twice as great as the value of diamonds known to exist before. This vast value is in the most concentrated, portable, and ornamental form, and more convertible than any- thing except gold and silver. Its accumulation has built up cities like Kimberley, and maintained important industries in Amsterdam and other centres. The De Beers Company, Limited, a single corporation, with stock having a market value quoted at over £20,000,000, controls more than nine-tenths of the entire output and regulates and maintains the price. Asa result, diamond cutting industries have been established such as were not thought of before, employing hundreds øf people in immense mills, where the cutters used to hire only the lbenches at which they did their work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>OR, THE MARK OF THE BLACK CROSS.</head>
        <p>OR, THE MARK OF THE BLACK CROSS.</p>
        <p>BY SYLVANUS COBB, Ju.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
        <p>THE CONFLICT IN THE JUNGLE-AN UNEXPECTED</p>
        <p>TURN IN AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>Lord Birchmear was instinctively a brave man. His first thought, when he found himself sur- rounded, was to estimate the number and character of the enemy. There were a full score of them— stout, wild-looking villains—and he saw from their garbs, as well as from .their Tartar features, that they were of the Nepaulhorde. He quickly saw, too, that they had no fire:quiz., yet they were armed most thoroughly. Their spears were broad and sharp; their swords Avail tempered, as could be told by the colour of the blades; and, moreover, each hound of them had a savage-looking knife in his girdle. Their leader, who wore a bunch of peacock feathers in his turban, spoke an abomin- able lingua franca, which the baron well under- stood ; and for the thira time he commanded the travellers to alight. He had two objects in view in this, as Sir John could plainly see: His victims would be more readily dispatched if they were on foot; and the horses would not be so liable to be injured. Those European beasts were valuable, and the northern Jemma.dars would purchase them at goodly prices. "Dismount—quick I" ordered the Ghurka chief. "What will you do with us ?" asked the baron. He had his answer in the very next movement that was made. The twcf Afghans, who had at- tempted to break through the cordon, were dragged from their seats and slain on the spot. There was no need to ask any more questions. Sir John knew that the Ghurkas had learned their lesson of the Thugs, and that they would leave no living witnesses of their evil deeds if..they could help it. My men," said he, turning to his followers, "Remember Birchmear, and fight for your lives We will out our way through them if we can !" . Two men had grasped Sir John's bridle-rein, but with a well-directed blow of his sword he struck one of them down, and the other was hurled backward as the noble horse reared and plunged under the influence of the spur. And the stout beast would have borne his master through the obstructing rank had he been left to overcome only the foemen that stood before him. But the Ghurkas had been on the watch for such a move- ment, and no sooner had the baron gathered his rein for the dash, than one of the villains' who had been stationed for the purpose, and who knew well bow to do his work, smote his keen blade across the ham-string of the horse, and the poor beast faltered and staggered. " Down 1 down!" ordered Sir John, who now saw that he must fight on foot if he would fight at all. "Come hither and stand by me 1" He had slipped from his saddle towards a point *here the line of the robbers were broken, and where he could place his back against the thick hedge of tangled wildwood ; and his attendants— all of them brave men—quickly followed his ex- ample; and they were enabled to gain the position the more easily because the enemy were for the moment eagerly bent upon securing the horses. And now approached the tug of battle. There were twenty against four. At first the Ghurkas set on with their swords. Sir John and his men were good swordsmen, and while they could keep the enemy before them, and only swords were used, they had a chance at least of prolonging the conflict. The murderous rascals howled and screamed and brandished their swords furiously; and had their skill been commensurate with their . zeal, they might have made quick work of the slaughter. . Three of their number had fallen—three besides the one whom Sir John had slain at his horse's head—and in return they had inflicted but one or two insignificant scratches. At this point the chieftain, who was bleeding from a scalp-wound, and whose tuft of peacock feathers had been shorn away, gave a quick, shrieking order in his own tongue, and on the instant his men jumped back to the opposite side of the way, where they dropped their swords and poised their spears. Sir John's heart sank. Against this new attack they could make no defence. He knew the deadly precision with which the Ghurkas could hurl their sharp javelins, and he was upen the point of raising his hands to implore mercy, when a new sound broke the air; and upon turning his gaze in the direction whence the sound pioceeded, he beheld a horseman dashing towards the spot from the northward. The new-corner had evidently taken in the situa- tion upon the first instant of beholding it, for he spurred on without wavering, with his bright blade flashing in the air, and was just in time to strike down the Ghurka chief, who had drawn back his spear for a thrust at the baron. For a brief space the robbers were seemingly paralysed by this ur expected appearance, and not until three of their number had'gone down beneath the furious blows of the strange champion did they discover that only a single man had thus interfered. But they made the discovery too late to take the benefit of it, for just as the fourth man of their number went down with his head split, and while Sir John and his attendants were advancing, half a dozen more horsemen came rushing upon the scene; and the robbers, without a leader to guide them, and completely overpowered, sought safety in flight, darting into the jungle like so many jackals. Ten of them escaped, and ten of them lay dead in the road. Sir John, without waiting to see who were these last that had ridden up, advanced to greet the one man whom he regarded as his saviour. The gallant champion had dismounted, and was wiping his sword with a tuft of flossy grass. He looked up as the baron approached, and started back with an exclamation of surprise. "Lord Birchmear I" "Arthur 0, my dear, dear boy! God bless you." Arthur Rainsford took the outstretched hand. For a moment a bright joy-beam illumined his handsome face; but directly a cloud came and drove It away, "My lord," he said, with sadness in his tones, "you say that because I have saved your life. Had we met otherwise you would not have found a blessing in your heart for me." "As God is my judge, Arthur, you do me wrong," exclaimed the old baron, still holding the youth by the hand. "You have had the love of my heart always. Ask Peter, and he will tell you that I came all this wild, deaolate way on purpose to meet you. 0, my boy! I knew not how much I loved you until I lost you. Come back to this poor heart, Arthur." • The noble minded youth could not resist the fond appeal, and when he flew to the old man's bosom, and wound his strong arms about him, he but obeyed the dictates of his deepest, holiest in- stinct.</p>
        <p>"Bless my soul t—cau I believe my eyes ?—Sir John Berwick ?" _ . .</p>
        <p>The baron, as he arose from Arthur's embrace, -•- -•- „</p>
        <p>met the wondering gaze of Sir Jasper Wallace. "Aye, my dear Sir Jasper," he cried, at the same time *rasping the out stretched hand; "it is your old friend, as you see; and—thanks to your timely arrival—alive to greet you." "No thanks to me, my lord. Captain Rainsford lithe man who seems deteritined to hold all his friends in his debt for their lives."</p>
        <p>-.•-- "Rainefordr repeated Sir John, with a flurried, painful look. "Our gallant Arthur, I mean," explained Wal- lace.</p>
        <p>"No, no 1" cried the baron, eagerly. "Don't rob poor Berwick of the only hope he hath on earth of seeing his name upheld and honoured in the coming years. My dear boy, you will be Arthur Berwick once again, and for all time, will you not ?" Our hero met the gaze of his step-father, and when he saw how much affection there was in it, a great joy leaped to his heart and thrilled every nerve, as he exclaimed: "My lord 1" "Hush, Arthur! No more now. I came to find you. You are mine. There shall be no more mis- understanding." "Hold where you are !" cried Sir Jasper, who had beard the words. "You would not rob the service of so gallant a soldier ?" "Birohmear needs an heir to its lordship, Sir jasper; and. moreover, you will remember that proper candidates for that honour are not so plenty as are those from whom you can select infantry captains." " Are you there, my lord ?" exclaimed the old general, with eager enthusiasm. "That is my firm and steadfast purpose, Sir Jasper." "Amen with all my heart !" ejaculated Wallace, and then, turning to Arthur, he added: "Come, come, my dear boy, you are yet a sol- dier and attached to my staff. Make ready for moving as quickly as you can."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>Arthur started like one awakened from a dream. He gazed first upon Sir Jasper and then upon Sir John. He saw the warm beaming of their tacos, and he knew that the blessing was a reality. By some means the barrier of evil had been broken, and the old love had been opened to him anew. He caught the hand of the baron and pressed it to his lips, and then he turned away to make pre- parations for setting forward; but he was not per- mitted to go far until he had received the embrace and the blessing of Peter Brun, and he prized the offering of the good old keeper, for he knew that It was pure coin. Three of those who accompanied Gen. Wallace were army officers, and as they had heard Arthur's story they Were at no loss to translate the scene which they had not witnessed, and a remark dropped by Co!. Harry Seaton to a companion gave evidence that they knew the whole secret. "Jack," said the colonel to Major Jack Hogarth "you can wager your life that it's all up with Harold Travers." . .</p>
        <p>"I hope so, Colonel. He was a villain when he was in the army, and I doubt if he has mended." "He conies of the wrong stock for mending. His father before him was a treacherous rascal, and I heard that his mother—" "Hold up, Colonel. She is the present Lady Birch mear." "Right, Major; but I'll hold to my opinion nevertheless." "And," added Hogarth, "knowing that it must be far from a favourable one, I would not have you speak it." The bodies of the dead Ghurkas were gathered by the wayside, after which the ' party were mounted ready for the start, ,Sir Jahh's poor beast bad been put out of misery y a pistol-ball, and he took the horse of Mark Vining, who found a seat upon one of the sumpters. "If I have not misunderstood you, my lord," said Sir Jasper, when they had gained their sad- dles, "you were on your way to meet us ?" "Yes," replied Sir John."l heard you were at Jungeepoor, stopping for a tiger hunt; and I thought I would join you." "We saw two tigers brought in, and might have waited Linger, had not Seaton and Hogarth received orders to move. We wished to keep them company, so we concluded to move with them But I am at your service, Sir John, if you wish to go on." "No, no," interrupted the baron, "I have found ell I sought. Let us back to Calcutta." An Jaen. tionate glance towards Arthur told his meaning vex)? plainly. After they had started on their way, Lord Birch- mear asked if it had been mere chance that had brought his friends to his rescue.</p>
        <p>"Rot entirely chance," replied Sir Jasper. "We heard at Burhampoor that a gang of Ghurka rob- bers on the road; and we also learned from a Darca courier who came through during the night, that a small party of Europeans, on their way northward, had engaged a bungalow at Nuddya. This led us to hurry, as you might imagine. But, after all, you owe your life to the quick ear and brave heart of your step—" A quick motion of the hand on the part of Lord Birchmear arrested Sir Jasper's speech. "Hush!" said the old baron, with a flush upon his face and an earnest light in his eye. "Never speak that word again to me on Arthur's account. Call him, as I hold him—my sox. "Believe me, Sir John," returned the old sol- dier, with visible emotion, "I never was called upon to speak a word that gave me more pleasure in the utterance. And now, as I was telling you, the quick ear of your noble boy caught the sound of mischief long before the lest of us heard it; and with a call to us to follow him, he put spurs to his horse and plunged forward. The rest you know."</p>
        <p>Yes, Sir John knew the rest; and for the first time in long years he felt truly proud and happy. That night in their bungalow, Lord Birch mear sat apart with Arthur, and talked with him of the past. "I can see now," the old man said, "how I suffered designing persons to magnify your faults in my estimation. But I knew not how I loved you until you were gone from me. It is no sin for me to confess, Arthur, that the love which I gave to your mother I have never given to another. You, my dear boy, come nearest into the void which she left in my heart. When you had gone—when I knew you had fled from the country—l would have called you back, even though I then believed you had robbed me." "Robbed you I" exclaimed Arthur, with a start,</p>
        <p>"Aye. I wits led to believe it." " I—l rob my—" "Hush, my boy. I know better now. But you carried money away with you ?" "Yes," said the youth. "I had a thousand pounds. I found it in my pocket-book on that fatal morning. It surprised me, for I knew not whence it came. Harold was with me when I found it. I had told him that I had no money. He bade me look and see. He declared that I had won heavily at play on the night before. I found the bank-notes—a thousand pounds—and he swore I had won it fairly. Fairly! 0! what a miser- able use for so fair a word! Even then I would have restored the money to those from whom I was led to believe I had won it, but Harold laughed at me; and, moreover, my own situation forced me to keep it." "I understand it all, my son." "And I did not win that money at play ?" "No. You lost." "And those notes were stolen from you?" "From my cabinet—yes. But ask me no more now, my boy. You are free. Let that suffice. We will try and live a happier life in the time to come." "God helping me," cried Arthur, resting his head upon the old man's bosom, "1 will live a life worthy of the priceless love you have given me 1" "Bless you! Bless you, my son I" On the following morning they set forth again, and reached Calcutta without further adventure. Before crossing the river from Bishop's College the patty separted, it having been arranged that Arthur should, for the present, remain at his post of duty. So Lord Birchmear and his servants went their way alone, the latter having been strictly enjoined to keep secret the principal incidents of their journey. His lordship desired that his re- union with Arthur might not at present be known to Lady Birchmear or to Harold. And he had most potent reasons for the wish. But a secret known to so many was not to be so easily kept from those who, above all others were most directly and deeply interested, and who would surely be on the watch for every sign. (To be continued).</p>
        <p>WAGAWAY: "60 you heard my .Lectures on 'Miracles.' Do you know what a miracle is, my little girl?" Bessie : "Oh, yes. My sister said it would be a miracle if you didn't stay for dinner to-day." As William bent over her fair face he whis- pered : "Darling, if I should ask you in French if I might kiss you, what would you answer ?" She, summoning her scanty knowledge of French, replied : "Billet doux ' "I USED to find time hanging on my hands, but I don't now. The days seem to fly." "Then you are happy?" "No, I'm not. I've a,. bill coming due, and don't know how to meet it."</p>
        <p>LITTLE Boy: "Isn't fathers queer?" Auntie: "In what way?" Little Boy: "When a little boy does anything for his papa lab, doesn't get anything; but if another man's boy- does it he get's a ' . "MN.. GINGHAM," said Taper, "I would like a fortnight's absence to attend the wedding of a very dear friend." "It must be a very dear friend, indeed, to make you want that much time. Who is it?" " Well, sir, after the ceremony she will be my wife." SOLD AGAIN 1— A : "What is Meyer doing now I" B: "I saw him a little while ago with his head in a noose, a knife at his throat, and foaming at the mouth." A "Horrible I Where? Where ?" B : "At the barber's I"</p>
        <p>AMONG THE " BOHEMIANS."—" Just my luck! I have gone and taken a room, and now I find there isn't a fireplace in it." "That's easily remedied. Come and warm yourself in mine. All you've got to do is to get a man to bring your coke here. In that way you will save the expense of a stove."</p>
        <p>- _ GZNTLEI!RIAN (savagely, to hairdresser) : "You villao! That stuff you warranted to do away with the bald patches I had has taken every hair off my head." Hairdresser: "Well, it has done away with the bald patches, sir. Your head's bald all we^ now."</p>
        <p>Youra 808 (enthusiastically): "Matrimony h the thitg for you! Why, you bachelors don't know anything about comfort, companionship, and the long-tried years of comradeship—" "How long have you been married I" "Nearly threa "mkt!</p>
