      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>OPENING OF PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING OF PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>DESCRIPTION AND HISTORY OF THE NEW ORGAN.</p>
        <p>MAILS.</p>
        <p>I. Siii-rnp,</p>
        <p>In view of he opening of Parr Hall, on Septem- 4er 2tltk, when there will be two performances by the Warrington Musical Society, a few details of the past history of the enlarged and up-to-date instrument which has been placed in the Hall by the &amp;piety will be of interest.</p>
        <p>In the year 1833 s ist;d-1-8.34, the Musical Society appears to have been growing in impartahce, so much so that it wee-decided at a meeting held on February 4th, 1834, to purchase an organ. Mr. Hall, Mr. R. R. Pickmere, and Mr. Kerfoot were deputed by the meeting to complete a contract with Mr. 4enn, organ builder, Manchester, for the purpose of supplying the organ: The contract entered into by Mr. Reim for the organ was as follows : " I, the un- dersigned. Samuel Rena, of Manchester, do hereby agree to built and to complete an organ tor the Musical Society at Warrington, according to the following plan and scale for the sum of .£ll5, and to fix up 'he same, thee Society to pay for the paint- ing an i carriage from Manchester, the boxes for the the pipes to be lent by me." In this original organ, there was no choir organ, but only a great organ, and a swell organ. In the great organ, there were 58 pipes, compass 0 G toil' in alt. The stops were,l, open diapason,metai; 2,stop diapason, wood; 3, flute, metal and wood; 4, principal, metal; 5, fifteenth, metal ; sesquialtra, metal. In the swell organ, the stops were, 1, stop diapaeorr, wood; I open diapason, metal; 3, principal, metal; and 4 hautboy. metal. A subscription list was issued at the same time with a ready response. It was headed by Mr. J. Wilson-Patten (afterwards Lord Winmarleigh), and the Hon. Horace Powys who each gave ten guineas. In the list of subscribers were many well-kn iwn names. Messrs. Jo4eph Stubs and William Hall each con- tributed .a.; Mr. John Guest and Mr. R. R. Pick- mere. £4; Messrs. W. Hadfield, George Furnival, jun., John Edelsten and Sons, T. W. Hardy, Thomas Kerfoot (Greenall and Buckton), Edmund Malley, William Heath, William Stubs, Thomas Hall,Thoma&amp; Vete Bayne.: Caldwell, Parker, and Company T Hurst, Samuel Hassid's, James Haddock,and Broad- bent, each £2. A large number contributed XI, among them being Messrs. J. R. Pickmere, James Matson, Beamont, sad J. Pickmere. The treasurer was Mr. sohn Rylauds, Jun., who is still connected with the Society. The organ was vested in trustee in the name of the Society, and the trust deed which was drawn up by Messrs. Greenall and Buckton,is dated April 30th, 183.5. The whole of the cost ot the organ was not raised at the time, and a lean of £6O was required to complete the pur- chase. In 'lB4O, further subscriptions were soli- cited, and the debt was then paid off. The organ was erected in the old Music Hall, which had previously been a Roman 'Catholic Chanel. and which stood behind a row of buildings ii Beweey-street near the Liberal Club. This rooms had been engaged for the purposes of the Society a short time previously. The opening ceremony of the organ was celebrated by a grand cencert which was held on Tuesday evening, March 17th, 1835, and at which some prominent singers were engaged. On Noveniber 13, 1840, it was resolved to remeve the organ to the old Town Hall, in the Market-place, and shortly afterwards it was removed by Mr. Renn, the builder, at a cost of £l4 8. It remained in that place until the erection ot the Public Hall in Rylands-street. As there was now a place in Warrington worthy of a good organ, the Musical Society decided to remove their organ thither, and also to add to it a choir organ, and a gamba stop. Meaars..Gray and Davison, Liverpool, who are en- gaged in the present enlargement, secured the con- tract,and the choir organ was built at a cost of .£ll5 and the gamba stop £25. The whole of this amount, and also the cost of removing the organ reaching the fiaure of £4Bo,were generously defrayed by the late Mr. Joseph Litton, and in return, the Musical Society presented him with a key of the organ with liberty to use it any time except during per- formances. The new and enlarged organ was opened amid great enthusiasm by Mr. Best, of Liverpool, in May.23.1865.A few alterations were made,and the organ was thoroughly cleaned in 1872 at a cost of about £lOO. After the Hall was sold to the present theatrical company in 1892, the organ was taken to pieces and stored away until recently, when it has been erected in the new Parr Hall. Fur this purpose, it has been extensively repaired, re- newed and enlarged by Messrs. Gray and Davison, organ builders, Liverpool, at a further cost of about £350. In the trust deed, there were originally 38 trustees, all of whom are now de- ceased. .0n January 16, 1892, Lord Winmarleigh, who was the last of the original trustees,appointed a fresh batch of trustees. The Musical Society have been able since their decision to erect this new organ, to set en foot a subscription list. They have realised close upon £2OO up to the present time. They hope within the next month or two to almost if not entirely cover the whole of the .£350 expended on the organ.</p>
        <p>STEM/CATION OF INN ORGAN. The organ has been greatly improved and extended since its erection in the Public Hall, and the appended specification will show the extent of its resources. The instrument may be considered new, excepting portions of the old sound boards and pipes, which have been in- corporated in the present instrument. Much of the old work was found to be sound and good thus testifying the excellent quality of the work done by Gray and Davison in 1872. The present front is designed and made to meet modern requirements. The various tiers of pipes are supplied with direct wind by means of tubu- lar pneumatics, which greatly increases the volume of tone and dispenses with many complications. The manuals and couplers are built upon an improved prin- ciple, each separate pipe having sufficient speaking space and ample room is provided for the combined full organ. The Musical Society deserve all credit for their enterprise in thus providing an efficient organ in time for the open- ing of the new Hall.</p>
        <p>Specll lextion of three-manual concert orgaa erected in Parr Hall, Warrington. GREAT ORGAN UO TO F. 64 Notes. Tone. Pipes. 1 Large Open Diapason BM. ... 54 2 Gamba Bll. ... 54 3 Stopped Diapason ....... 4 Principal 4ft. ... 54 5 Fifteenth 2ft. ... 54 6 Mixture 3 ranks various... 162 7 Trumpet 8 Clarion 4it. ... 54</p>
        <p>SWELL ORGAN CC TO P. Tone. Pipes. 1 Double Diapason 2 Open Diapason Bf t. ... 54 3 Dulciana 4 Stopped Diapason Btt. ... 54 5 Pr! cipal 6 Swabe Flute 7 Fifteenth 2ft. ... 54 8 Mixture (2 ranks) various... 108 9 Cornopean 10 Oboe CHOIR ORGAN CC TO P. Tone. Pipes. 1 Dulelana Bft. 2 Keranlophon 3 Clarabella 4 Harmonic Flute 5 Harmonic Piccolo 6 Clarionet PEDAL ORGAN CCO TO P. Tone. Pipes. 1 Large Open Diapason lSt. 30 2 Vioione 3 Violoncello 14 Super Octave 4fl. ... 30 5 Trombone COUPLERS. Great Organ to Pedals. Swell Organ to Pedals. Swell Organ to Choir. Choir Organ to Pedals. Swell to Great Unison. Swell to Great Sub Octave. Trernulant to Swell Organ. 3 COMPOSITIONS TO SWELL ORGAN. 4 COMPOSITIONS to GREAT ORGAN and PEDALS combined. SUMMARY.</p>
        <p>Pipes. Great Organ 540 Swell Organ 682 Choir Organ 3OO Pedal Organ l5O</p>
        <p>Total number of Pipes 1571 A perusal of the above specification will show that although not having a large array of stops, these are well arranged and selected for variety ot tone and effects. The portions of the organ incorporated in the present instrument were buil; inl 872 by Gray and Davison. The organ was used for the first time at a rehearsal by the Musical Society on Tuesday evening, and its mellow tone and variety of stops was much admired. The organ will be completed in ample time for the opening en the 28th inst., a section of the reeds only now remaining to be placed in position.</p>
        <p>'ME OPENING CEREMONY. I The opening ceremony in connection with Parr Hall will be held on the 26th of the present month. Mr. J. Charlton Parr will formally open the Hall, after which there will be two grand musical performances given by the members of the Warrington Musical Society. In the afternoon performance. Handel's grand oratorio" Judas Maccabees," will be given. Sir Arthur Sullivan's can- tata, a 4 On shore and sea," and various other high class pieces will also be rendered in the evenisg.</p>
        <p>_ AL</p>
