      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>l'H E E KAMI F,13, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 21 1895.</head>
        <p>l'H E E KAMI F,13, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 21 1895.</p>
        <p>Some of the leading stars in the musical world have been engaged, including Miss Anna Williams, soprano ; Miss Clara Butt, contralto ; Mr. Edward Lloyd, tenor ; and Mr. Andrew Black,hass, In addition to whom there will be a chorus and orchestra of upwards of 230 performers under the conductorship of Mr. Frank H. Crossley. The Mayor and Corporation will be present at the evening ceremony.</p>
        <p>CIEMNIURT. BED LION. .1 Hewitt W Love, l5 W Coppock .......... ........... 2 Ti Rothwell W (Peeve HWilliams .......—. ........... 15 W Rathbone 3 R `NW°. l5 Ir Broady — 14 R Farrington l5 W Mclntyre (:apt) ......... 8 T Rolton l5 P Hamm ....... 3 3Br melow (eapt) -..... ..... 15 H Bro wly .... 7 W Woo 4 15 T Bowers TRPienn l5 T Heesom 1 G Ball, jun.</p>
        <p>Total.—. ...... 179</p>
        <p>Totl.l _ 80</p>
        <p>The following table shows the positions of the respec- tive clubs in the above competition which has now come to a terinimation. First teams. (Tab</p>
        <p>Mr. Scott as their candidate and really the ground bad been so well traversed by them that very little had been left for him to Bay. But when they remembered the cir- cumstances under which Mr. Scott came amongst them —a perfect stranger, and within nine days of One of the most crucial and important contests Warrington ever saw, and remembering also the amount of popularity which immediately followed his visit to the town, well, he could say honestly that in all his experience, now extending over a considerable number of years, he bad not been prepared to find that Mr. Scott would get hold of the heart of the Liberal portion of the constituency to one tithe the extent he did in the short time he was amongst them. (Hear, hear aid applause.) He thought that perhaps the very fact of his coming at such a late hour and tackling what seemed to be to , him and to them,a forlorn hope, so impressed them with I his courage and pluck that he was at once endeared to all the workers of the party. They found also during the conduct of that election,as had been referred to pre- viously, that Mr. Scott was a gentleman, not only in name and social position, but in fact. (Hear, hear.) They had been in the habit, perhaps rather too much in Warrington, of imbuing their elections with a consider- able amount of strong feeling, and perhaps to some ex- tent that had been provoked by the strong treatment they had received from their friends on the other side. (Hear, hear.) All of them were not blessed with the patience of Job and some of them perhaps were a little bit more peppery than othersond if they got pinched,or kicked, or cuffed, they were very ready and apt to try and give the same thing back again with a certain amount of interest in addition—(laughter),—but latterly they had learnt that it was possible to conduct a cam- paign without a single bitter word being said or dis- creditable thing being done. He (Mr. Roberts) was quite sure that the party was stronger to-day because of the manner in which the last fight was con- ducted than it had been for many years. He agreed with Councillor Plumpton that in Mr. Scott they had in a very large degree a reproduction of those traits of character so prominent in their late dear friend Mr. McMinnies. (Applause.) Mr. Scott had a great amount of tact, largely the result he had no doubt of the training he received from one of the most successful 'business fathers in Manchester, and largely from the fact of his own large business experience, and, the speaker was glad to say,—successful business experience—(hear, bear)—but also from a natural gift which was not always given in an equal degree to every man. Mr. Scott was naturally a man of very great tact and he (Mr. Roberts) did not think he would make many mistakes. They were certain that Mr. Scott had made up his mind to do his duty to the Liberal party of War- ringtan,and they in return must make up their minds, if they had not done so,to do their duty by Mr. Scott. To do that would require a considerable amount of sacrifice from all of them, for during the past ye.irs they had been deprived by death, by removal, and change of opinion, of many leaders whom they were proud to have am- ongst them, and unfortunately men of like social posi- tion had not succeeded them to take their places se well, and, therefore, the responsibility upon those who re- mained was thrown further down in the ranks of the party and upon the shoulders of every man in it. He could say to Mr. Scott on behalf of the Liberals in the room and those outside, that they would not be wanting in their duty whenever the next contest came, and be- tween the present and then. Ihe bitter results of ex- perience were, be thought, rubbing his ewn angles eff and he was prepared to take anything and everything so long as they could get what end they wanted.(flear,hear). It was thought that it might be some years yet before they would have to try to wrest the borough from the hands of the Tory party, but none could tell what the future had in acre for them end there might be circum- stances to give them an opportunity through a b,°- election of trying their strength with the other side. He thought that to-day they were in a better position as a result of the revision last week, acid with the addi- tional assistance they had been able to give to Mr. Clay- ton they would have a much better register for 1898 than they had ever had before. He expected it would be fonnd to be very pure, and the Liberal strength upon it would be confederablv greater than it had been for a good while past. "Many hands make light work," and that applied in a special degree be the work of registra- tion. He hoped during the winter they would set ep as many parts of Liberal work as they could. They must have their organisation perfect, and it was his opinion that the committees were net as perfect as they ought to be. Those committees would have to be overhauled and the best men put in the important positions, and they ' ought to have one or two men responsible for every street in the town when an election occurred. It was 1 their duty to keep the town posted up with the opinions of the day, end show up the actious of the Conservative party as against the policy of the Liberal party in the same direction. (Applause.) Mr. T. BiecKazz said it gave him very great pleasure to say a few words in support of the toast then before the meeting, and he was sure it was a great pleasure to all of thereto see so many young men there, as they were, after all, the hope of the party. With regard to Mr. Scott, he might say that his own experience had ex- ,' tended, as they knew, over a large number of years but his hopes had never been so high as to the success of the Liberal party in Warrington since 1880, when they carried their excellent friend Mr. McMinnies. It ought to animate every member of the party to do his very best to carry their cause to a successful issue by electing Mr. Scott as the representative for Warrington. (Ap- , pla use. The toast was then enthusiastically honoured to the accompaniment of" For he's a jolly good fellow." I Mr. MORRIS, returning from a short absence from the room, said he had been to meet a deputation who asked him to interest himself in getting Mr. Scott to take the chair at the Sunday school anniversery sermons on October eth at the Buckley-street Sunday school. It was a place at which he himself had taken the chair for many years and the meetings had been successful. but I he felt certain that if Mr. Scott would come among those workingmen the Success would be far greater than be- fore. He would tell the deputation that Mr. Scott had cenaented. (Laughter.) Mr. Scorr, again applauded on rising, thanked the I speakers for the kind way in which they had referred to I him and his father. He assured the meeting he was deeply grateful for the kind feeling they had displayed that night and upon former occasions. With regard to the 6th of October he would be only too pleased to be present if he was able. On the evening of the 18th he was presiding at a concert in aid of the Bible Society at Bold-street. In October he believed there was a bazaar to be held which he was to open. He was also going to be present at the opening of Parr Hall. (Applause.) Ile would like, before sitting down, to propose a further toast and that was " Their absent friends." (Hear, hear.) He asked them to remember him kindly to their better-halves and children. (Hear, hear and applause.) He would like to meet all, and he purposed before long making a tour round the town and calling upon both Liberal and Tory. (Applause.) He had in Manchester a number of friends, not only musical but possessing other talents, and if possible he would like to arrange for a dramatic performance or two in the winter months. (Applause.) Ile, however, did not want it to be done altogether by Manchester people, but be would find one or two and he wished others to assist by finding the rest. These entertainments would be devoted towards reliev- ing the poor or to some such institution as the Infirmary or Hospital. (Applause.) On October sth two lady friends of his were coming over to Warrington toeing at some gathering, at which he hoped as many as possible would be present to ensure success. Councillor Roaenrs mentioned that they bad in th- club a portrait of their former president and late mem her, Mr. McMinnies, and he thought it would be a grand acquisition to the club if they could have a portrait of their friend, Mr. Scott. (applause.) He happened to know that an artist in Manchester had almost completed such a picture and he thought if they went about it the right way to ask Mr. Scott to present it to toe club— (laughter)—he would do so. (Applause) They would give it a most honoured place—(laughter),—and while there was no need for the counterfeit presentment of Mr. Scott they would like to have the picture where for many years they could look at it and remember the time when lie came amongst them, and point bun out as the man who came to fight the forlorn hope for Warrington and succeeded in becoming its member and retaining that position for many years. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Mr. Moneta : If Mr. Scott doesn't care to present this picture I .will pay for it myself. (Loud applause.) Councillor Putsfeeurr, amid much laughter, proposed that they as members of the Liberal Party there that night, request Mr. I'. B. Scott, at the earliest possible moment his portrait be finished,that he shall take means to present it to the Club,and that they join together and tender their very best thanks to Mr. Scott for the vary handsome present he had made. (Loud laughter and applause.) Mr. Scorr said Mr. Morris would not be called upon to spend his money in placing them in possession of his (Mr. Scott's) portrait—he himself was responsible for Mr. Roberts having said what he had done. If they wished it he would have pleasure in sending the portrait down there shortly. (Applause.) During the evening songs were rendered by Messrs. Greenhalgh, Matthew Knowles. and Finch, the latter gentleman also acting as accompanist during the even- ing. Recitations were given by Mr. Moses Butter- field,who chose as his subjects "The Country Bumpkin" and "The Bashful Man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WORLD'S EXHIBITION IN 1900.</head>
        <p>A WORLD'S EXHIBITION IN 1900.</p>
        <p>The " New York Mail and Express" proposes World's Exhibition in 1900. Let the entire civilized Globe unite, and success would certainly be stowed. Not America's Exhibition not Great Britain's Exhibition : not an exhibition for Franco,G Irminy,Beigium,Ssritzsr- land, or India: but an exhibition representing the scien- tc advances of the world at large as obtained at the close of the 19th century, and as compared with those obtained at the end of the 18th century. Some have sug- gested that such a unison of thought, idea, feeling and interest would be difficult to bring about; but as this de- sirable nnani city already prevails among tire masses of these very countries on questions of importance, where they have united as in one voice, it is reasonable to pre- sume that a united effort for aGeneral Exhibit would be of easy accomplishment.Of course to obtain a unanimous verdict from all classes representing so many countries, the object of thle popularity must be based upon genuine merit. As instanced the verdict endorsed by thousands in all civilized countries, with no dissen- sion or exception, which has been given for the value of Warner's Safe Cure in oases where the liver is out of order, or where the kidneys are weak or diseased. This unanimous verdict is due to uniform irenuineness. Experiences like the followieg give an idea of the value of this medicine and the importance of prosaptly re- sorting to its use when danger threatens.</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Thomas. Llanwydelelan Rectory, Newtown, Mont„ says :—" For four years I stiffen I from indigestion. GREAT BRITAIN. &lt;liminess. and frequent headaches. Fin- ally became so low I actually bade fare• well to my 'family, but 4 bottles of Warner's Safe Cure corapletely cured me." R. A. Gunn, M.D., Prof. New York Medical Col ege attests :—" In severe cases of Bright's Disease, where all other remedies and treatments felled, I have effected perms eat cures with Warner's Safe Cure. Furthermore. I am con- vinced that in all ailments where the blood is on an unhealthy condition, and the general health impaired, the adven- t/we gained by the use of Warner's date Cure is remarkable." Dr. Fiseher, Govt. Physician at Reline- buerg. Wuertemburg. attests :—" I have pleisure in saying, one of my patient. who was suffering from Bright's Disease of the kidneys and who, in consultation with eminent colleagues, was treated with all other known therapeutic re- medies without avail, was completely cured through the use of Warner's Safe</p>
        <p>AIIHRICIA.</p>
        <p>OBRM&amp;NY.</p>
        <p>Cure." - Prof. J. Potraud„ B.C. B.Sc.. Paris attests :—"After weeks of suffering from foflammation of the bladder, durhug -which time, although under the most noted specialists, the disease sinadily developed and I rapidly grew weaker. I then resorted to the use of Warner's Safe Cure. It promptly relievA the painful symptoms and my convalescence to a complete cure was uninterrupted and rapid." I. joaneaux, Rue de la Longue Hale 31, Brussels, says :—" Warner's Safe Cure cured my daughter (aged 19 years) of Pulmonary oisease following typhus fever, although doctors and their medi- cines failed."</p>
        <p>PRANCE.</p>
