      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BOWLING LEAGUE. Gamma v. RED Liox —These teams met on the green of the Cemetery Club on Saturday last and brought the season, an far as the League is concerned, to a close. The Cemetery players in the end won by a majority of 99 points and by their victory secured the first place in this season's competition. Below are the individual</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>senres</head>
        <p>senres</p>
        <p>MR. P. B. SCOTT AT THE</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB:</p>
        <p>ANNUAL BOWLING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>INTEREQTING SPEECHES AND ANNOUNCEMENTS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the annual main of bowls was played off between the members of the Warrington Liberal Club Bowling Club, the players competing for a number of prizes which bad been presented by friends and members of the club. Some excel- lent bowling was witnessed. Following the game, the competitors, accom- panied by Mr. P. B. Scott, Liberal candidate for Warrington at the recent election, were provided with an excellent tea in the reading-room of the club. After tea, at 8 p.m. the party, together with a large number of friends, adjourned to the com- mittee room, which was soon full of ardent Liberals. Here Mr. J. H. Fleming took the chair and amongst others present were Mr. P. B. Scott, Councillors Shaw Green, Henry Roberts, J.P., Wm. Pierpoint, J P., G. Plumpton, and Messrs. T. Morris. I T. Birchall, J.P., D. Tinnion, A. Brown, and C. J. Holmes. .</p>
        <p>Th. emEIRMEN announced that in the absence of their president (Councillor Pierpeint bad not then arrived) he formally took the chair, but he thought it would be out of place for him to occupy it long and he therefore called upon Mr. Scott, their candidate—or rather future repro- sentative—(cheers)—to occupy his place. Mr. SCOTT then took the chair amidst loud applause, and said that before going further he would announce that they had received letters regretting absence from Captain Rogers and Mr.J.W.Hayward J.P. The toast he had first to propose was the " Health of Her Majesty the Queen," about which it was unneces4ary to say one word, as they were all as loyal as he was. After this toast had been honoured, Mr. Scorr said that when me received an invitation from the Prince of Wales to dine with him at Marlborough House, it was usually received in the shape of a command; he had received their kind invitation te come that evening, but he did not take it so much as a command, although be felt it was his duty to be there, and he also felt it would be a rteil pleasure to come from the place where he was ! —Scarborough. That night he Was to have dined with the member for Scarborough and officers of the ieet,had he remained. Mr. Reckitt was more successful than be (Mr. Scott) had been in Warrington, having turned out the late member, Sir George Sitwell, who, as Mr.Birchall ! had just reminded him, didn't sit well and he did not think he would again. (Laughter and hear,hear.) The other day he Mr Scott drove from Scarborough to 'Bridlington, and in speaking to the driver, who was a Tory, he asked him how it was they did not return Sir George, and he said " Well, the best man's in ; he (Sir George) was a wooden-headed old Tory and never spoke a word in the House and did not do a thing for us : (Laughter and applause.) There were few Tory members who did. (Hear, bear.) He did not known whether they had been watching the member for Warrington. lie had, and he saw that Mr. Pierpoint was on his favourite topic—Cyprus--which was about all he could say anything on. They would remember to keep their eye on Mr. Pierpeint, who was no use where he was. Let them rub it into the Tories wherever they could and let it be well-known what he was doing. (Hear, hear.) They had lately had an op- portunity of claiming a vote and he trusted that not one man in Warrington had missed the opportunity. It was not.the blame of their party if they did not get their votes; it was their own blame. If they did not get them they were not only damaging themselves but any- body who came to fight their battle. (Hear, hear.) This was not the first time he had appeared in Warrington since the election and during the next two or three weeks he would be here pretty often, and he hoped during the winter to have further opportunities of forming some kind of a Debating Society for all. (Applause.) That was the proper and only way of getting to know his opinions and the only way of educating themselves and coming to a right conclusion. A man would be a poor man if he simply voted Liberal because he thought the Liberal was the man to vote for, or vote for a Tory for a similar reason. They should vote because they conscientiously believed the man they voted for would represent the right cause, and if they did not they would never get what they anticipated for themselves. (Hear, hear.) Referring to the reason for his presence. Mr. Scott said he was afraid they could not have called upon a worse man to give away the prizes for the bawling main that afternoon. Bowling was a game he had never played but he had watched it before and with more in- ! terest that day than at any other time. Probably next year he would jein the Bowling Club there, and no doubt come in a bad 17th—there were 17 prizes to dispose of. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. Scott then distributed the prizes as follows :—First, Mr. Jennings, carving knife and fork and set of six knives and prongs; second, Mr. Hooper, dinner service of 58 pieces. This gentleman was going to be married and they would all join with the speaker in wishing him their very best congratula- tions ; third, Mr. James Locker, cruet stand; fourth, Mr. Peter Langley, set of bowls ; fifth, Mr. Haynes, I umbrella ; sixth,Mr. B. Shearer,pipe and ease; seventh. ! Mr. Cross, two brushes ; eighth, Mr. Parr,a hat ; ninth, ! Mi. alyneks, me, flower pot ; tenth, Mr. Taylor, pair of slippers ; eleventh, Mr. C. Roberts, large pie ; twelvth, Mr. Sankey, dolly tub ; thirteenth, Mr. LI. Williams, ' seed, and currant bread ; fourteenth, Mr. Bradburn, meat pie ; fifteenth, Mr. Craddock, pair of gloves ; sixteenth, Mr. F. Jones, tobacco pouch ; seventeenth, Mr. Joseph Bennion, another meat pie. ! Mr. A. Baowee had great pleasure in proposing the ! health of the donors of the prizes, coupled with the names ot Mr. William Pierpoint, Mr. J. B. Fleming, and Mr. T. Morris. He was sure they were all glad to see Mr. Scott there ; he was for one, as it was the first time he had met him and he hoped it would not be the last. lu Mr. Scott they had a man who would work and ! make eiinself pleasant all round, and agreeable not only to his own party but also to the opposite party. The election was conducted by him on most gentlemanly lines and Mr. Browne hoped that at the neat election Mr. Scott would be returned at the head of the poll. (Applause.) The toast was then honoured with the sing- ing of " For they are jolly good fellows " and " a way we have in Warrington." Councillor PIIMIPOINT said he would he as brief as pos- sible because he wanted to hear Mr. Fleming and Mr. Morns. (Laughter and applause.) As one of the donors of prizes, he had always taken an interest in bowling wherever it had been and more especially at the War- rington Literal Club. Lie was also glad to say that he had in his house a copper kettle which he won sea second prize at one of the mains of that club. (Ap- plause.) While he wanted to do all he could to help bowling in the Liberal party in Warrington that was only sufeidiary to the main object in view. They should take an interest in bowling, in the club itself,and also in that great work in which they were all engaged, more especially on behalf of their friend, Mr. Scott. They should all be forward in making a resolution on the day of the Licit election that they wuuld go to the com- mittee rooms and offer their cervices. He had no hesitation in saying that as they had been successful in their bowl- ing mains they would be successful in bowling over their friends the Conservatives. (Hear hear, and applause.) Mr. J. H. FLEMING, also responding to the toast, said he had taken a warm interest in the club and also in the brwling green. As the last speaker had pointed out they should be interested not only in the club nut also in politics generally, and it behoved every man in the room to buckle on his armour for the light to come about some day or other. It was a flue thing for him to see this meeting as it as the best he had seep since he was cOnnecb.d wadi the club. (Applause.) Mr. SCOTT was sorry he had not had the opportunity of meeting with bitc. Morris sooner as he was sure they would have had better results than they had, as they knew the work he had done in the past. A few words from him we Led be listened to, he was sure, with the utmost pleasure.</p>
        <p>Mr. f. Moulin said he would say" ditto " to all the other speakers had said with regard to their friend, Mr. Scott, getting in the next time he had an opportunity. He considered Mr. Scott had made a record—(hear,hear) —coming amongst them as he did on a certain Saturday and on the following Monday week polling so heavily. They made a vety good fight, and if they had known Mr. Scott eight days earlier he believed they would have won. (Applause.) He would pray most heartily—as he had done while in Ireland—for Mr. Scott to win nett time, and swear more heartily if he didn't. (Laughter and applause.) They had not had a man before who had had the pluck to come to them and conteez. an election to come off t.n the 9th day after his coming. Mr. Scott was simply a marvel of.pluck and English per- severance. (Loud applause.) Mr. DAINNION proposed the health of the Chairman of the Bowling Green Committee and the manager, Mr. Mitchell. He was sure they would all agree with him that this meeting was one of the most, if not the most. successful they had ever had in connection with the Bowling Club. After the drinking of this toast, Mr. Shearer responded on behalf of the committee, and testified to the excellent condition of the green which was due to Mr. Mitchell's labour.</p>
        <p>Mr. MITCHELL acknowledged the compliment, and at- tributed their successful meeting to the telegram an- nouncing Mr. Scott's corning. Mr. CRITCHLET proposed the health of their visitors, coupled with the names of Messrs. Plumpton and Holmes.</p>
        <p>In responding to this toast, Councillor PLIMPTON said it was his firm opinion that if they had another election they would win it. Mr. Scott he referred to as a young Medionies,and by far the best candidate since the death of that gentleman. Mr. C. JACKSON HOLMES said this was the first time he had been present at one ot the bowling mains, though it was a game which he took a great deal of interest in. He was also of the opinion that Mr. Scott had stirred up considerable enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS proposed a toast which he said was almost last but ceitainly not least, namely " The Health of Mr. Scott." (Applause.) A good deal bad been said bat night by previous speakers about the excellency of</p>
        <p>;-7, t • ,</p>
