      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Mt lA.</head>
        <p>Mt lA.</p>
        <p>played Won. Lost. rtg. Cemetery Inn 8 Bulls Head Horse awl Jockey 8 Re 4 Lion 8 Liberal Club</p>
        <p>The following table shows the nosition of the second teams in the Warrington and District Bowling League Corn petition. . -</p>
        <p>Mltehes Club played. Won. Lost. PM. T.rorsA an. oakey ... Cemetery Inn ............ .- ..... 6 Balls Head . Liberal Club 6 . _ -</p>
        <p>THE SARACEN'S HEAD, WILDERSPOOL.—The member; of the Saracen's Head Bowling Green,played their closing main on Saturday afternoon. Tbe weather was fine and the green was in admira.:lB condition. There were only 20 entries for Saturday's main,and 15 prizes were offered, 10 in the main itself and Sin a consolation main. The nrizes were all good ones, the lowest being valued at 74. 6d. A handsome clock was the first prize, and this was won by J. Jolley. The second prize,a travelling bag fell to W. Whittaker, the third, value £1 18., to J. Boa- cow, the fourth, a pair of bowls to Johnson Clarke, and the fifth, a copper kettle to T. Ince. Five ether winners will get prizes value 76. 6d. each. The competitors had tea together on Thursday night at the Saracen's Head, and the prizes were then distributed,</p>
        <p>MAIN AT THE BIILL'S HEAD.—On Saturday the closing main of the Bull's Head Club was held, when a match was played for prizes which had been kindly con- tributed by Messrs. J. Fowler &lt;president), R. Anderton (vice-president), T. Locker, J. Evans, F. Webster, and othsr gentlemen. The match was warmly contested, and the final result was as follows :-1, J. Gleave ; 2, R. Fairbrother ;3, F Berry ;4, E. Shaw. There were 32 lentries. The prizes will be presented when the club have finished with the county contests,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CONSERVATIVE CLus /Kam—The annual main in connection with the Warrington Conservative Club was concluded on Saturday afternoon. Some ex- cellent bowling was witnessed, the green again being in splendid condition. The six winners were as follows :- Ist prize marble timepiece and ornaments. F. Kerfoot ; 2nd prize, silver watch, John Thornton ; 3rd prize, silver plated teapot, D. Pickton ; 4th prize, cruet, W. Hill ; sth prize, travelling bag, D. Bawling ; an 6th prize, copper kettle, G. Brown. At the close, Mr. John Roberts distributed the prizes, in place ot Mrs. John White, a prior engagement having prevented her being present. Mr. T. Mason, the hon. secretary of the Bowling Green Committee, said be too was sorry Mrs. White was unable to present the prizes that evening. He found that that wag the eth main of bowls in connection with the Club, and it was satisfac- tory to know that bo many of the members enjoyed the pleasure of a game of bowls after their day's work was over. Mr. John Thornton proposed a vote of thanks to the manager, and said Mr. Watkin had done well in keeping the green in order. Mr. John Taylor seconded the vote of thanks, and Mr. Watkin suitably responded. A hearty vote of thanks was passed to the friends who had subscribed to the prizes. A vote of thanks was vassed to the Chairman and Secretary, and Mr. John , Roberts and Mr. T.Mason suitably responded. COUNTY ASSOCIATION Sestl-Frsets.--The officers of the County Association, at a meeting held at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Manchester, Mr. J. J. Meakin in the chair, I resolved to dispose of the semi-finals and finals in the ' senior division prior to the close of the present month, In the draw for " ties" by the four districts yet unde- feated, Warrington South was coupled with Pendleton, and Broughton Cobden with Warrington North. The I half-yearly meeting was deferred until late in October. LANCASHIRE CHAMPION SHIP (FINAL) : WIGAN V.W ITH- INGTON.—The final in the eighth annual competition for the county champitnship took place over the green of the I Leigh organisation on Saturday. The same representa- tive districts have twice previously met in a similar con- test, and the results have gone to Wi,L.,an by a small majority. Wigan played eleven of t/..- team that beat Pendleton in the semi-finals, and Wallington ten ef those who took part In the second match against Levens- !mime. Mr. J. J. Meakin acted as referee, the umpires being Messrs. H. Scott (Wigan) and J. Green (Witting- ' (ton. Withington won by 19.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WEEK IN THE NORTH OF • IRELAND.</head>
        <p>A WEEK IN THE NORTH OF • IRELAND.</p>
        <p>It was to the North of Ireland that I repaired for the purpose of spending a brief holiday. Lame, on the Antrim coast was the centre decided upon—a flourishing and pleasantly situated town which has of late years sprung into prominence as the home of the English, and especially the Lancashire tour- ist. It can be approached by various routes. A two hours' sea passage from Stranraer in Scotland is the shortest, but the most popular is by the steamers plying between Fleetwood and Liverpool to Bel- fast, and thence by rail to Larne. It was the latter route that I selected, and at midnight on Friday week I steamed out of the Prince's Dack,Liverpool, on one of the fine steamers of the Belfast Steamship Company. A ten hours' sea voyage brought me to Belfast, the first city in Ireland in commerce, and the second in population. The visitor is at once struck with the commercial prosperity of this essentially modern city—a prosperity which has been simply marvellous. In 1821 it was but a town with a population of not more than 32,000; to-day its estimated population is 290,000, and its rateable value £791,864. Fine buildings, wide streets, and large warehoases are to be seen on every hand and long before one enters the dock, the clanging of a thousand hammers is to be heard from the now famous Belfast shipbuilding yards. It is the linen and shipbuilding trades which largely account for the city's somewhat phenomenal prosperity. faking a train for Larne, a 25 miles' journey on the Belfast and North ot Ireland railway, brought me to the desired haven. Larne possesses a fine harbour which is well sheltered and commodious, and its depth, even at low water, is sufficient to float the largest transatlantic vessels. The town was at one time an important seaport, and in the middle of the eighteenth century it was the only port in the North of Ireland from which emigrant vessels sailed. In the present day it is best known as the starting point of the mail steamers for &amp;ran- raer. The town is surrounded by beautiful and in- teresting scenery, and its progress has been very marked during the last few years. Its popularity as a salubrious retreat increases year by year, and there are ample facilities for golfing, rowing, sails ing, driving, and fishing. Close to the harbour is Islandmagee where the remains of a Danish fort are to be seen. There are many causes to account for the town's pre-eminence as a tourist resort. Its physical advantages render it by sea and land most accessible. Both road and rail And their natural termini here, and it owes much to the business en- terprise of the railway company just mentioned, and last, but net least, to Mr. Henry McNeill. Of this remarkable man it can. be safely said that he has done more than any other indivi- dual in bringing into the country the English tourist. He has spared no expense in providing accommodation and touring facilities for visitors, and the immense establishments which have grown up under his festering care and enterprise are marvellous developments of business prescience and forethought. The large saloons, dining halls, ex- tensive dormitories, linked hotels and well fur- nished inns, testify that Mr. McNeill well deserves the title of "Prince of Caterers," as well as the " Bianica "of the North for the excellence of his posting establishments. From the middle of June to September, there are to be found in Mr. McNeill's establishments over 300 tourists, and in the height of the season, far more. During the last 40 years he has catered for upwards of 8,000 wedding parties —being an average of 200 parties every year. Be has several hotels in the town. The original one has been se enlarged that over 1,000 persons can dine together, and there is sleeping accommodation ,in this one place alone for 500 persons. His terms are so moderdate that be has the nick-name of "Knock-'em-down "from his competitors. He will provide anyone with excellent board and lodging for .£1 a week,aad this includes a free 40 miles'drive. IThe Lug's Arms Hotel is his best establishment, and here for £2 a week, visitors fare luxuriously. IHe makes a special feature of his noaching tours and parties to the numerous places of interest in Antrim so far N3rth as Giant's Causeway are con- ducted daily, and over one hundred horses are kept continually at work during the seasonit was in one of his hotels I stayed, and the majority of the com- pany were Lancashire people. Mr. McNeill is a thorough business man and his resources seem in- exhaustible. He is ever busy, and yet ever calm. His testimonials give ample evidence, if any were needed, that his endeavours to cater for tourist. are fully appreciated. But I am digressing somewhat, and must hurry on to mention some of the beautiful places around Larne, Mounting one of Mr. McNeill's cars,we pro- ceed along the Antrim coast to Glenaraconsidered to be the most beautiful and enchanting of all the Antrim glens. Tourists on the cars and brakes soon become familiar with the sound of the word " scram "—and with its meaning too I For miles the barefooted children will follow them witb this one exclamation until the much sought-after penny is forthcoming. The drive to Glenariff is round one of the finest coasts in the kingdom. The con- ' struction of the road was commenced at the time of the Irish famine in 1846, and it is a splendid piece of engineering skill. It winds along beneath the shade of great basaltic rocks which make one creep to think of e portion falling. Beyond the other side of the read there lies,like a great leviathan,the broad expanse of the North channel. The beauti- ful scenery on this route is beyond description. Mr. land Mrs. S. C. Ilan in their book on Ireland say of this part of the country. "We have never visited any district which so completely defies description either with pen or pencil." Arriving at Carnlongh a short halt is made at the Londonderry Arms Hotel —a fine hotel much used by visitors, and overlook- ing the magnificent bay of Carnlough. A twenty minutes' drive from here brings us to Garron Tower (the seat of Lord Londonderry). In the background there stands in solemn grandeur, tremen ions chalk cliffs with richly-planted slopes. Advantage is taken of the privilege of visiting the grounds and hall—a favour which is greely appreciated, and a treat not soon forgotten . The mansion is built on an eminence and an extensive sea view is obtainable. It is a castellated, battlemented building of large dimensions. Inside the mansion, there are some fine portraits of the Londonderry family, and one magnifieent picture by Rembrandt is said to be worth .£lO,OOO. There is also a beautiful collection of ancient and modern furniture, and numerous in- teresting relics—one of which is a camp bed used by Lord Londonderry at Waterloo. On a block of limestone rock close to the tower, there is an inscription in connection with the potato famine of 1846, and in which the Marchioness of Londonderry states that she had it engraved "being desirous to hand down to posterity an imperish- able memorial of Ireland's efll,ct ion and England's generosity in the years 1846-7, unparalleled in the annals of human suffering." After pursuing along the coast for a few miles further, the main road strikes inland, and is girt on either side by a moun- tain range. Here and there are to be seen water- falls tumbling headlong over the rocks, and falling Into fine diaphanous spray. At Glenariff there is quite a number of falls, cascades, and rumbling hole.,and the glen abounds in rare and fantastic charms. It has been described in several scno by the Bard of Dunclug, and Mrs. A. M. Minster in her volume of "Waifs and Strays," describes it inverse, four lines of which are as follows:</p>
        <p>"Foaming, flashing, far above us, leaping on from rock to rock, Fell the spray-enshroudeal waters, with a pealing thun- der shock; Ash and rowan, bireh and privet, waved along the river side, And the hazels bent and quivered o'er the dark impetu- ous tide."</p>
        <p>The Giant's Causeway—one of the wonders of the world is always an attraction to visitors to the North of Ireland. From Larne you can approach it either by car or rail. It is a distance of over 150 miles, and the railway is the most popu:ar route. It was this one that I took. You proceed along a narrow gauge railway past mountain, glen, and woodland, as fat as Ballymena—a leading centre of 'the linen trade. Scores of factory girls can here be seen going to their ,labour, the only perceptible difference from what is to be seen in Lancashire, is that they are all barefooted. Again entraining, after making a rapid survey of the town during the hour placed at your disposal, you are taken on the ordinary gauged railway to Portrush on the North Coast. This is a most fashionable watering place, occupying a unique situation on the isthmus of a short peninsula, and possessing two sea frontages. It has been Called the " B ighton of Ireland," and is much frequented by lovers of golf where the finest links in the kingdom are to be found. There is a magnificient sea view, and the waves with their white crests are of great height. Atlantic liners from Liverpool and Glasgow are to be seen passing this point almost every day. About half-a-mile from the coast are the small islands known as the Skerries. Judging by its pe4ition and surroundings there should be a fine future for Portrush.</p>
        <p>TIIROIT IRRITATIoN AND COUGH.—Soreness and dryness, tickling and irritation, inducing cough and affecting the voice. For these symptoms use Epps's Glyc rine Jujubes In contact with the glands at the moment they are excited by the act of suckh,g, the GlycYrine In these agreeable con- fections becomes wctively howling. Sold only in boxes, 7fd., tins Is. lid., labelled •...Twstxs Rept &amp; Co.. Ltd., Homceo- pathlo Ch,.nsists, London." Dr. Moore, In his work on •'Nose and Throat Diseases," says: "The Glycerine Jujubes I prepared by James Rips and C.)., are of undoubted service as a curative or palliative agent" while Dr. Gordon Holmes, Senior Physician to the Municipal Throat ,nd Bar ,nfirmary, writes "After an extended trial, I have found your Glycerine Jujubes of conshiemble benefit in almost all forms of throat disease."—Adet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <p>An eight miles drive on an electric tram from this town, brings you to the world-famed Giant's Causeway, " that celebrated monument of Natura's mighty grandeur." The electric railway skirtsthe 888 coast, and the most important place en route to the Causeway is Bushmills which derives its name from the old watermills on the river Bush. The scenery in this district is one having few equals. Another fine sight to be obtained from the cars is that of the interesting ruins of the famous Dunlnce Castle, standing on an insulated rock and separated from the mainland by a deep and broad chasm. It would be a difficult task to adequately describe the Causeway. It is the result of Nature's handiwork, and is shrouded in mystery. Since public attention was called to it in 1694 by Christopher Cole: and it has been visited by thousands of people from all parts of the globe. This remarkable assemblage of basaltic columns comprises about 40,000 perpendi- cular and symmetrical pillars, principally hepta- gonal and hexagonal in shape. They are of un- equal length, but the most mysterious feature about them is that they fit so closely together u not to admit of the insertion of a sheet of paper, in Ifact there is not the slightest observable interstice. Every pillar is perfect, distinct, and separable from those adjoining, and they are fitted together in a manner which no mason could approach. It WB4 whilst gazing on these wonderful columns, witlx the high and white crested waves lashing over a, portion of them, that a Lancashire farmer, of the robust type, came up to me and suggested in quite a reflective mood, that " those stones could easily be hewn down and made into good paving stones l'</p>
        <p>It would be impossible here to mention all the interesting p'aces which I visited during the week'. visit, but I must pause to mention one or two mere. I have a vivid recollection of a fine drive round Lough Larne and on to Whitehead—a rapidly rising- watering place on the western shore of Belfast Lough. A path, two miles long, has been con- structed along the beach, which gives access to several wonderful caves. A visit to these, well repays all the trouble taken in reaching them. From this point on a clear day may be seen Mina°. Craig, the Ayrshire and Wigtownshire coasts andV., the Isle of Man. f ? Another interesting place which I visited wag' Antrim—the county town. The drive from Larne —a distance of a little over 20 miles—is throrgh a rich tract of agricultural land, well under cultiva- tion. The town is nicely situated on the Six-mile water, and within a quarter of a mile of the well- known Lough Neagh, the largest lake in the 'United Kingdom. It is 15 miles in length, and on entering from Antrim by the Eastern approach you pass through the beautiful demesne of Lord Massereene. In the distance on the north-east of the lake can be seen Shane's Castle, the seat of Lord O'Neill—a name associated with almost every re- markable event that has occurred in Ulster for many centuries. The lake abounds in fish, chiefly pollen, a kind of fresh water herring which is sold in the English markets under the name of "gray- ling." In Antrim itself, there is nothing particu- larly attractive beyond the Castle, a plain square building with turrets at the angles, and built is the reign of Charles 11. These are but a few of the numerous places in the county of Antrim, easily accessible by the tourist. Others include the historical town of Carrickfergus Whiteabbey with its fine abbey or monastery from which it takes its name; the fine watering places of Whitehouse and Greencastle; the beautiful town of Ballycastle with its romantic and charming scenery; and above all the sweet villages of Cush- endall. and Glenarm. In fact right from Belfast Lough to the North coast there are open to the tourist, retreats, well worth visiting. Within that small stretch of country—at our very doors—there are scenes which would give enjoyment and infor- mation to equal, if not surpass, those to be found on the Continent, and where perhaps there is greater need for the English tourist's gold. J. B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday. The Rev. G. F. Roberts occupied the chair, and there were also present Messrs. W. Houghton, J. Ball, J. W. Knowles, J. War- burton„ J. Hou_rhton, J. Johnson, J. Dignum, T. Halton, T. Mather, S. Setton (clerk), W. Owen (surveyor), T. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF NUISANCE INSPECTOR.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF NUISANCE INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board -sent -1; -co-m—m—unication expressing their approval of the Council in re-appoint- ing Mr. Frank Kerfoot at a salary of £llO.</p>
        <p>ESTIMATES OF EXPENDITURE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK in submitting the estimates of general ex- penditure for the next six months, said that he fully expected that a rate of a penny in the £ would be re- quired, but he found that they could meet the estimated expenditure and leave over a small balance after levying the same rate as last year-41. in the £. The total amount which would thus be raised on the townships would be £276 10,. id—Mr. W. flocosrrox moved, and Mr. MATHER seconded, and it Wad carried, that the esti- mates be accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>130 \TVS TO THE CLERK.</head>
        <p>A 130 \TVS TO THE CLERK.</p>
        <p>A recommendation from the Streets Committee was read to the Council to the effect that during the past five years, a large amount of work had devolved upon the Clerk in connection with the street making in Little Sankey, and that VO was a reasonable sum to be al- lowed him for his services. This sum was less than 2 per cent, upon the money collected by him.—Mr. W. HOUGHTON moved the confirmation of the recommenda- tion. lie sold that the whole of the Streets Committee were present when the recommendation was passed.and they were unanimously of the opinion that the sum was a fair amount to be given.—Mr. T. HALTON seconded, and the motion was carried unanimously. - so RVEYOR'S REPORT. _</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM OWEN reported that during the month inspection had been made of Mr. Dumbill's and Mr. R. Hughes houses at Penketh, Mr. Kilshaw's house*, Burtonwood, Messrs. Kennon and Bibbv's houses, Poul- ton, Mr. W. Si[cock's, Mr. Ezard's, and Mr. Matthew's houses, Padgate. Certificate, of habitation had losers granted to Mr. H. Ezard, for one House,padgate ; to Mr. W. Silcock for two houses, Padgate ; to Mr. H. Hughes. for two houses, Penketh ; to Messrs. Kennon and Bibby, for two houses, Poulton ; and to Mr. Joseph Webb, for houses at Burtonwood. The following plans were sub- mitted and being in accordance with the bye-lawsonight he snoroved. Mrs. Hall, addition to farm, Pearnhead- lane, P+dgate ; Messrs. Mortimer and Co., drying sheet Orford Tannery. The Surveyor further reported as fol- lows :—Re Penketh drain : As directed at your last meeting 1 have looked this matter up and find that the estimate of Mr. Williom Heaton, of Warrington, dated' 14th August, 1893, :ind amounting to £B7, was accepted by me according to instructions given, but on the con- dition that the work was not to be proceeded with until the question of wayleave had been settled with the pro- prietor.—Re Burtonwond drainage : This work is now completed and is now in working order with the excep- tion of the iron cage for the alumino ferric. This cage has had to be specially made and the makers promised that it should leave their works yesterday. The whole of the branch works are now connected and the work is all in order. He took it that the Parish Council now takes work over.—The report was adopted unani- mously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>"DID SHE ? "</head>
        <p>"DID SHE ? "</p>
        <p>" Yes. she went back home to him from her mother's that very night, for he just sent her a little paltet o' tea and wrote on Come back, Huth. and I'll stick this.' And that bit o' Brooke, Bond's tea-wrapper has looked out of the corner of the old clock's lace ever since, for six een years, reminding 'em every time they want to see What's o'clock. Yes, Roth's husband did stick to his promise, and for stxteett years they've been the Si pplest couple in the county, and, every time they see a Br oke. Bond packet, they just bless it as the good old peace- maker. Now I don't mind telling yo.i as that young man was me : and Ruth, bless her good heart, has gone rou• d with our two younrst kids, to the Agent's for the usual quarter-pound. This little bit of real life tells its own tale and von will pick up its moral when you buy your next packet o' BROOKH, BOND'?. Our Agents are everywhere and sell the sot Ruth likes best at la 81. a lb., but we have other sorts from is. to 2s. Bd., and so we suit everybody's purse.</p>
