        <p>THE EXA:'il INER, SATURDAY SEPTEMBER 21, 1895.</p>
        <p>SAVE 25 PER CENT. FURNISH AT WORSLEY'S, THB PANTHEON. GAYTHORN, MANCHESTER. ESTABLISHED 54 YEARS. SHOWROOMS COVERING NEARLY ONE MILLION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>SQUARH FEST.</head>
        <p>SQUARH FEST.</p>
        <p>THE MOST COLOSSAL FURNISHING ESTABLISHMENT IN THE NORTH. SPEC IALITB: HIGH CLASS SECOND-HAND FURNITURE. -</p>
        <p>Fllll IT URB by JAMBS LAMBTHairti-05.671R.N and SONS, GOODALLS, GILLOWS of Lancaster, GARNETTS of Warrington, CUTTER of Liverpool, SHOOLBEBD of London, STARKEY &amp; MBI D of Altrlncham, CHAMBERLAIN of Birmingham, and other High-cl .ss Houses. TERMS CASH OR DEFERRED PAYMENTS. All goods are advertised at cutting prices, being In most cases 50 percent. below ordinary city prices for cash. If by deferred payments, no differ nee Is made in the invoice price. but a slight per centage is charged according to the length of credit desired As compared with the hire system this saves from 10) to 2 0 percent.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY CUAT.—SISCOND-BANO FURNITURE.— Y Benjamin Franklin said a penny saved is a i enny made. This is an obvious truism, and ought to be borne in mind by all, rich and pour. Bat at "The Pantheon" it is not only a question of saving pennies, but • f pounds. House Furnishing is one of the important ohs in every family, well deserving careful thought. If you can economise with- out detriment to qu ,lity and stability, then by all means de so, and you cannot practice thi, theory better than by paying a visit to '• The Pantheon." which will cost nothing, but profit you much. One of the most interesting sights in tea Fur- nishing World is • The Panthe m." a busy hive of indastry, containing without fear of contradiction, th ee to four times the stock of any two of the largest furnishing houses in Man- chester. The bulk of this stock is High-class Second-hand Fern iture by high-class makers, at quarter the price of the poor miserable, veneered orange box furniture from the East End of London, now so largely retailed by drapers. Beware of purchasing London furniture ; it lacks stability and finish. A Good Second-band Article is vvirth a hundred of spurious imitations of furniture made in East London. Wursley's can supply you with all you require in furnishing, by sueh makers as Lambs. Gillows, or Garnet, at prices simply pul- verising competition. For instance, as a simple bargsin you ran obtain a Fine Sideboard by povesitons which cost h 85, for £l6; what chance has the competition of new furniture against bargains like these I. If you go to an auction room and buy you take the risk yourself as to whether your pur- chases are sound and durable, but at Worsley's you have no risk, Worsley's ab•olutely taking all risk, guarantee every article for ten years. and will take any article or articles back nut giving satisfaction. It will cost you nothing to call at "The Pantheon,"and see for yourselves ; you can inspect without feeling compelled to purchase; they will be delighted to show you round whether you buy or not. Under theta circumstances you will be guilty of criminal, wilful neglig•, erica of your own interests if you neglect visiting Worsley's before deciding where ti Furnish.</p>
        <p>ONLY ONE WORSLAYE IN THE WORLD. GREAT BARGAINS IN COMPLETE ROUSES OF FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>THE GIOIATEST BARGAINS IN THE WORLD,</p>
        <p>COMPLETE HOUSES OF FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>Lot 1. Splendid House of Furniture, complete, the most substantial lot in this city, and containing more for the money. The Dining-room has a heavy Dining-room Suite, complete, in hard grain, Levant leather, the frames beauti- fully carved in bas relief; has comfortable couch and luxuri- ously upholstered easy chairs, a heavy dining table to watch. extends to dine 10, with incised early English legs n patent castors; a handsome Per fan bordered carpet and rug to match, solid brass bar fender,flre brasses and dogs complete; cornice, with rings, ends and brackets ; Notting am lace curtains ; and lasi, whieh is thrown in gratis and is in addi- tion to what any competitor could do, simply for an adver- tisement, a handsome American walnut sideboard, in the Renaissance style, to mach the dining-room suite. The Bedroom Suite is solid Cypru- wood and is a suite which can be highly recommended, guaranteed for 10 years, being charged in some establishments as much a. we ask for thet whoie of this house of furniture; a handsome solid brass and euamelled bedstead, with complete &amp;Mega of mattress and bedding; there are the usual accessories for bedroom—carpets. curtains, fender, chamber ware, tfc. Also Kitchen Furni- ture, consisting of the usual chairs, h, avy white top table, fender, ashpan. and top bar, floorcloth for kitchen, landing. and hall. I will pick and deliver the lot for 21i guineas, 10 percent. extra if by deferred payments. Call and inspect this lot, it pulverises all competition ; If you approve buy, if you don't there is no harm done.—W ORSLEY 'S.</p>
        <p>thogq</p>
        <p>Lot 2. A Splendid House of Furniture for £4O. solid oak and walnut, heavy massive furniture throughout, in capital condition, worth £BO, dining-room, two bedrooms, kitchen. hall stairs, and lauding ; a great bargain. Lot 3. A workingman's House of Furniture for £l5 ; a splendid bargain. There is better stronger, and more furni- ture in this lot than many competitors offer for £3O. Lot 4. The beet House of Furniture for £6O ever offered Same quantity as Lot 2, but higher class. except that we ar-. prepared, as an advertisement, to present a pianoforte with this lot or its value. This offer holds good for 14 days only. Lot 5. And last, but not I east, we have a magnificent House of Furniture, depusited by the executors of a deceased gentleman. of Alcierley, with us for immediate disposal. Price for the lot. £2OO. Contains superb furniture for draw- ing, dining, sitting, and three bedrooms; kitchen. Will, stairs, landing; besides sundries ; honestly worth £560. Full and complete particulars on application.</p>
        <p>GREAT BARGAINS IN DINING-ROOM FURNITURE</p>
        <p>LOT 113. A remarkably handvome carved oak Dining- room Suite, in morocco, hair stuffed, bold, massive, and ha Asorne ; couch, two easys, six chairs, six feet sideboard, eight-feet dining table, fine bold overrnantel, coal cat.inet, the lot complete for £45; worth £2OO. —WO itSLEY'S. LOT 212, A capital solid American Walnut Dining-room Suite, in best English Morocco, hair stuffed throughout, the framework Is h, avy, magnificently car,,r,d) In bas relief, consists of broad coinfortaNe couch, pair of luxuriously up- holstered easy chairs, a set of six high-padded bat* standard single chairs, all upholstered in a rich wine coloured turkey grain Morocco, beautifully banded and studded, cost £7O; price £l5 155., guaranteed for 10 years. The sideboard is of mob e proportions, of modern design, well equipped with every molern convenience, early English design, fitted with broad deeply-cut bevelled edge mirrors of great lustre and brilliancy, cost £65, for 16 guineas. A massive handsome Dining Table, £3 10s. ' cost £l2 10s„ and Overmautel, which matches the sideboard, for £5 10s.. worth £25. All the above in splendid conditi</p>
        <p>Lot 251. A heavy handsomely-carved Dining Room Suite in Oriental saddle bags, a full suite of nine pieces, t ,gether with raagnificent couch and easyehsirs ; honestly worth £45, for 14 guineas.—WoltSLEY'd. Lot 261. Excellent Spanish Mahogany and Morocco Dining Room Suite, nine pieces complete; cost 2N% for 8 guineas.— oßmArs.</p>
        <p>Over 200 Diaing-room Suites on view, from £2 to £SOO ; over 200 Sideboards from 355. to 100 guineas; over 300 Dining Tables, from 15s. to £2O ; over 460 Overmantels, all shapes, styles,and sizes, from Bs. 6d. to £3O.—WOItSLEY'S. Inspection invited.</p>
