      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Op – Qeect</head>
        <p>Op - Qeect</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>HARVEST THANKSGIVING SERVICES. There were large congregations at the harvest thanks- giving services held in Holy Trinity Church on Sunday. The Rev. Dr. Preston occupied the pulpit at the morn- ing service, and the Rev. W. Stanton Jones in the even- ing. The anthem, " The eyes of the Lord wait upon Thee," was sung by the choir, the solo parts being well sung by Messrs. F. Bradshaw, and C. Atherton. The organis , was Mr. William Cooke. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF M. DYER. The death occurred on Monday night, somewhat sud- denly, of Mr. Dyer of the Waterloo Hotel, Top Locks, Runcorn. For three years he had been a sufferer from diabetes, but there was apparently nothing to indicate that the end was so near for on the night previous to his death he was to be seen outside his hotel. He was 45 years of age,and for 16 years he had had charge of the Waterloo Hotel, the present structure superseding one of smaller dimensions,* few years ago. He leaves behind him two sons and three daughters to mourn his loss. He was greatly esteemed and respected by all who knew him. Drs. Robinson and Mcli.,nzie were his me Heal attendants. The funeral took place on Thursday after- noon in Runcorn Cemetery in the presence of a numerous attendance.</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING CONCHRT. In Runcorn Public Hall on Wednesday evening, the Volunteer Band (2nd Earl of Chester), assisted by local artistes, will give a grand benefit concert in aid of Mr. James Harrison, of Runcorn, whoa met with an accident 3i years ago which rendered him incapable of earning any means Of livelihood. Mr. S. Taylor, J P., C.C.,will occupy the chair, and the band will be under the con- ductorship of Sergeant Outhwaite. EYRNING CONTINUATION SCHOOL. As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, the evening continuation school inaugurated by the Runcorn School Board, will be re-opened on Mon- day night next. For the young men's classes, Mr. J. F. Moulton will be the head teacher, and Miss Kilburn will be in charge of the girls' department. Prizes are to be distributed to the successful students of last year on Monday evening in Greenway-road school, where the classes are to be held. Intending pupils are requested to be present on the opening night in order to facilitate the formation of classes in different subjects. _ _</p>
        <p>TONIC SOLFA PRIZR CHOIR, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In connection with this choir, a gentleman's " At Home "was held in Ellesmers-st. schoolroom,on Wednes- day evening. The arrangements were carried out by the gentlemen with great taste and judgment. The room had been tastefully decorated for the occasion with plants, flowers, &amp;c. Over 70 members and friends sat down to the abundant repast provided. An attractive programme of games, songs, recitations, &amp;c., was gone through. After the removal of the cloth " Off to Pnila-1 delphia " was well sung by Mr. Harrison, and Mr. I Pendlebury's quartette contributed " On the banks of Allan Waters." The song " Beautiful Spring Onions" was nicely rendered by Mr. Arnold Povah, and an appreciated Lancashire reading was given by Mr. March An overture which had been specially composed for the occasion, was rendered by an orchestral band, under the , conductorship of Mr. Brown. Refreshments were after- ' wards handed round, and games followed until eleven o'clock, when the proceedings terminated. During the '1 latter part of the evening, a violin solo was contributed by Mr. Arnold Povah. Mr. Harrop creditably sang " Doodah." The pianist was Mr. William Cooke. For ' the decoration of the room, plants were kindly lent by Mr. Philip Speakman J.P., C.C. glassware by Mr. El- ward Dobson, Bridge-street, anddrapery by Mr. Thomas Leach, Church-street. Three concerts have been arranged for the forthcoming season. PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH—HARVEST FESTIVAL. Harvest thanksgiving services in celebration of the ingathering of the harvest were held in the Primitive Methodist Church, Greenway-road, an Sunday. There were large congregations at both services. The Rev. T. Braman, superintendent minister, preached at the morn- ing service,and the Rev. W. Moore, of Frodsham, in the evening. The edifice had been taste- , fully decorated for the occasion by the lady members of the Christian Endeavour class, the choir and the Sewing meeting. On a crimson cloth attached to the pulpit was displayed the words "Praise ye the I Lord of the harvest.' Around the pulpit there was a large quantity of fruit and vegetables, relieved with bouquets and plants. The pillars were decorated with 1 ivy leaves, and the window sills covered with various vegetables. The choir sittings were also decorated with evergreens, interspersed with fruit and flowers. Two anthems were sung by the choir at each service. In the anthem, "The Lord is my Shepherd," the principal parts were taken by Miss Hillidge and Master R. iffillidge. Mr. Gittins presided at the organ with his usual ability. Satisfactory collections on behalf of the Circuit fund were realised. There was a tea meeting and sale of fruit in the schoolroom on Monday night. About 70 persons sat down to tea at tables presided over by the Misses Hillidge,Shingler, Stubbs, and Lunt. The fruit, flowers, and vegetables, which had been used in decoration were disposed of at good prices. DEATH OF AN OLD BUNCORNIAN. On Saturday, there passed away at the age of 68 years Mr. James Williamson, of 15 Greek-street Runcorn. The deceased who was an old Runcornian, was well-known and highly respected. During his life-time he had been associated with the chemical industries, and at one time was employed at the Weston works, but at the request of the late Mr. C. Hazlehurst, he took charge of the vitriol department in the firm of Hazlehurst and Son, which has recently been transferred to the United Alkali Com- pany. At these works he performed his duties for about 40 years. He was of a quiet disposition, and a consis- tent member of the Wesleyan denomination, his connec- tion being of a lengthened period. He was a firm teeto- taler, and to this he attributed his robust health. It was not until twelve months ago that his health began to fail, he having enjoyed almost total immunity from ill- ness throughout his life. He stuck to his work until Whitsuntide when he relinquished it on account of his health. He was medically attended by Dr. Robinson, who also called in Dr: Larkin, of Liverpool, but their efforts were unavailing and he died as stated. The funeral took place on Tuesday afternoon, when the re- mains were interred in Runcorn Cemetery. Amongst those present were Mr. S. Pope,Mr. J. Holford (members of the Rose of Sharon Lodge of the United Order of Oddfellows,to which he belonged), Mr. Barnes, and Mr. J. Leathwood. Messrs. T. Booth, Bizley,Badge, and E. Jones were the bearers, and amongst those in the pro- cession were : Messrs. Joseph Williams,William William- son, J. Pickthall, W. A. Lightfoot, Isaac Nicholas, J. Riley, gen., J. Riley, jun., Kinsey, S. Boyers, Brock- bank, D. H. Jones and A. E. Jones. A number of floral tributes were on the coffin, contributed by the following: Mr. Joseph Williamson (chemist at Messrs. Gossages, Widnes), Mrs. Pickthall, Runcorn, Mrs. Isaac Ninholas, Weaverham, Mrs. S. Boyers and Mrs. D. H. Jones. Messrs. D. H. Jones and Sons carried out the funeral arrangements. SHOCKING BURNING FATALITY AT ItUNCORN.—THE INQUEST. At Runcorn Police Court on Monday afternoon, Mr. J. E Worsley held an inquest touchinv the death of a child, 12 years of age, named Mary *Ann O'Niel, who died on Saturday. The first witness called was Mary Hushin, wife of Thomas Hushin, a labourer, residing in ' Water-st., who deposed that the dece-ased, Mary Ann O'Niel, lived with her. She was 12 years of age. On the 2nd inst. the deceased got up about 8.30 for the purpose of making the fire. About half an hour afterwards wit- ness heard screams. She ran downstairs, and saw the deceased ablaze. A neighbour put an apron around the girl. Witness at once sent for Dr. illbinson who at- tended up to her death on Saturday. Before deceased died she told witness how she was burnt. She had made the fire and put a paper across the grate in order to make the fire draw. She then went to the door to a school mate,and when she returned the paper was on fire. She pulled it away and tried to stamp the fire out, but in do- ing so her petticoats caught fire. There was no one with her when the accident happened.—Mary Rooney, of ' Water-st., steed that she saw the deceased coming out of her mother's house on Monday, the 2nd September, in flames. Witnesa went to her and put a coarse apron round her. Dr. Robinson was called in, and eveything necessary was done for the deceased .—Dr. Robinson said on receiving information of the accidant he sent a mes- senger for the district nurse. When he first saw the de- ceased the nurse had dressed the wounds and made the girl as comfortable as possible. He found she was badly burnt on the front and lower part of the body. He hardly thought she would have recovered from the first shock. The account given of the accident was exactly consistent with the injuries received. The deceased was well looked aPer by two nurses. Tile parish nurse was very attentive to the girl and there was also a skilled nurse attending her during the first week,named Nurse Lawrenson. She went there voluntarily and did many kindnesses for the girl. The deceased died on Saturday night from shock, the result of severe burning—P.C. Richardson stated that he had seen the District Nurse who had told him that the other nurse attended of her own free will.—The Ceroner in summing up the evidence, said Nurse Lawrenson de- served very high commendation for what she had done. —The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and the foreman said the jury wished to record their appreciation of the services of the nurses—The Coroner said it showed how useful such institutions were. The number of kind actions done by them was beyond des- cription.</p>
        <p>The trial of the boy Coombes,aged 13, for the murder of his mother by stabbing her as she lay asleep in bed, and of a sailor named Fox,charged with being an acces- sory after the fact, came to an end at the Old Bailey, London, on Tuesday. Very little additional light was thrown on the tragedy. The evidence as to the boy's mental condition, and his own conduct in the dock and in prison, led the jury to find that he was guilty of the murder, but that he was not accountable for his actions at the time. They acquitted Fox, who was discharged. The usual order for the detention of the boy Coombes in an asylum " during her Majesty's pleasure" was made by Mr. Justice Kennedy. Seven of the leaders in the Kucheng massacres were executed on Tuesday in the presence of the foreign Con- suls forming the Commission of Inquiry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER, SAT-UR I )A. Y, SEPTEMBER 21, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SAT-UR I )A. Y, SEPTEMBER 21, 1895.</p>
        <p>salary, though he though the duties deserved it,he would revrve to himself the right of again referring to the sub- ject and proposing an increase of salary, if it turned out that the duties were more numerous.—Mr. Woodward seconded the proposition, which was carried unan- imously. Tnis was all the business of public importance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon a meeting of the above-named council was held at Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Mclntyr J.P.,occupied the chair, and there were also present: Ad- miral Parry,Messrs. J. Brandretti,ll. E. Haywood,W.M.e Williams, S. Woodward, T. Lewis, E. Aaron, J. Weir, W. Pickering, J. Knowles, J. Darbyshire, J. Stokes, T. Wright, C. Hoylance, W. Holt, J. B. Chadwick, E. Bretherton, A. Grreenway, E. Wallington, C. Mounfield, Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer), Mr. Farrington (nuisance inspector), Messrs. Diggle and Swinton (surveyors). THE BONE NUISANCE AT FRODSHA.M.</p>
        <p>Mr. Williams stated that with regard to the complaint of the nuisance caused by standing trucks of bones at Frodsham, Mrs. Linaker, in the absence of her husband, had told him that morning, that the nuisance still existed. There was a number of trucks standing there that morning. He understood that the matter was receiving the attention of the Council.—TheClerk stated that he had an appointment with the railway company the following morning.—Mr. Aaron wished to know what was going to be done for the workpeople if the works closed. If people could not stand the smell let them cle ir out. There would be no money without smell—Mr. Woodward : Who is to pay the rates ?—Mr. Brandreth stated that he had received complaints of the trucks having been kept standing at Helsby.—The matter then dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>DANGEROUS ROAD AL LUMB BROOK..</head>
        <p>THE DANGEROUS ROAD AL LUMB BROOK..</p>
        <p>A letter was ;sad by the Cnairman from Mr. J. Charl- ton Parr, of Grappenhall Hayes, Warringtonottating that he was sorry to trouble the Council again, but the road under Lomb Brook Bridge was now so bad as to be al- most dangerous for horses and carriages and nothing appeared to be done or doing. If an acc:deut occurred it would have to be ascertained with whom the respon- sibility rested.—Mr. Swinton stated that nothing had been done in the matter by the Ship Canal Company, but they had promised him that the road should be remedied. He thought they would attend to the matter. —Mr. Derbyshire said the road needed immediate atten- tion. It was not the first time Mr. Parr had had to call attention to the state of the road. It was very dan- gerous.and it was quite time that it was attended ta.— Mr. Brandreth moved that the Surveyor write the Ship , Canal Company informing them that unless they per- formed the work the Council would execute what work was necessary and charge them with the costs. DAIRIES, COWSHEDS, &amp;c., AGAIN. The Local Government Board wrote with reference to the Dairies, Cowsheds, and Milkshops Order, 1895, and said they had again considered the question referred to , in the Clerk's letter, but they must adhere to their ob- jection to a regulation dealing with the air space to he provided for each cow, which would require only 450 cubic feet of air space, and would be limited in its operation to dairies and cowsheds newly erected after the regulation came into force. Under these circum- stances the Board could only suggest that the paragraph' might be entirely omitted.—Mr. Heywood made a pro- position that the by-laws be not adopted.—Mr. Wood ward, seconded, stating that it seemed they could not have what they knew to be necessary.—Mr. Derbyshire did not think it would be wise to entirely disregard the re- commendations of the Local Government Board. They must act consistently with their requests if possible.— Mr. Brandreth moved an amendment that the matter be referred back to the committee.—Mr.Williams seconded, and on Mr. Heywood withdrawing his proposition, the amendment was agreed to. RIPER POLLUTION AT WARRINGTON. With regard to rivers' pollution, Mr. Potts, clerk of the Cheshire County Council, asked whether the Council accepted the proposal embodied in the letter ef the Town Clerk of Warrington, or what action they purposed tak- ing to remedy the nuisance occasioned by the Stockton Heath sewage entering the river at the point in question. —Mr. Darbyshire said the pullution wai having a very bad effect upon the houses. There was a large number empty, the assessments were lessened and the contribu- tions to the rates were consequently lower. It was quite time they were up and doing something to allay the objectionable state of things.—The Chairman said he would like to know whether it was entirely owing to the bad smells that there were so many houses empty at Wilderspool and Stockton Heath. He understood one cause was that some of the houses were jerry Knowles said some of the houses which had been erected had never been tenanted.—Mr. Heywood said it was brought before them that morning at the meeting of the Assessment Committee that too many houses had been erected. He did not hear the word smell mentioned.— Mr. Aaron thought that by the agreement between the Canal Company and themselves the Company could be held responsible for the offensive smells.—The Clerk stated that at the previous meeting he was directed to press the Town Clerk of Warrington to insist on the opening of the sluices. The County Council seemed to be pressing them (Runcorn Council) to carry out a re- commendation made by the Town Clerk of Warrington with reference to joining with them in the construction of a sewer. The Town Clerk suggested that that should be done now instead of waiting for the passing of the Warringten Extension Act, which it was not expected would be passed until November, 1896. He thought it was a matter for the Appleton Committee to consider.— It was decided that the matter be referred to that Corn. unittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>HEISBY WATER SUPPLY,</head>
        <p>HEISBY WATER SUPPLY,</p>
        <p>Writing on behalf of the He!shy and ;District Water Company, Limited, Messrs. Potts, Netts, and Gardner, stated that a part of their scheme was to supply Alvan- ley with water,it would be spoilt unless the Council allowed them to lay their pipes along the length ot road, in respect of which permission had already been pro- mised to Mr. Brandreth. They did not wish to interfere in any way with Mr. Brandreth's pipes. They trusted the additional permission they were asking for, would on reconsideration be granted.—Mr.Brandreth promised that the matter be referred to the Parish ;Council of Bele- by for their consideration.—Mr. Roylance seconded. An amendment was proposed that the permission asked for be granted. On being put to the vote there was a tie, seven voting for each.—The Chairman gave his casting vote in favour of the proposition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>W-ESTON ROADS.</head>
        <p>W-ESTON ROADS.</p>
        <p>The committee appointed to consider upon the South. road and Post Office-road, Weston, reported that the Clerk had, in accordance with instructions, written to Captain Clarke explaining that the District Council con- templated in South-road, Weston Point,running past his cottages, and were informed that the gardens in front of such were an encroachment, and rtquesting him to take down the wall enclosing the gardens. in order that they might again form part of the road. Captain Clarke had called upon him and produced his title deeds, from which it appeared, one portion of the land on which the cottages were erected was freehold ,and was conveyed to him by a deed dated December 7, 1864, and the other portion was leasehold for 99 years. and was leased to him by deed dated June 18th, 1880. Both deeds pre- scribed the building line as that which formed the front of the cottages,and provided for the land which formed the gardens being left open and unbuilt upon. Captain Clarke, however, contended that the gardens never had formed part of the street, and that as he had had pos- session of one portion of the land over 30 years, and of the other portion over 15 years, he should, if he allowed the gardens to be pat to the Street, claim to be com- pensated to some extent, but that as he agreed with the suggested improvements, he would be willing to allow the garden wall to be taken down and the gardens put to the street on payment of £2o,he having the rails and gates, but the authority taking the stone and other materials. After some discussion it was resolved that the Clerk and Surveyor be authorised to enter into negotiations as to terms with Mr. Clarke. It was also resolved that application he made to the Local Govern- ment Board that section 154 of the Public Health Act, 1875, be in force in Weston, investing the Rural District Council with the powers thus given. It ws.s further decided that a 15 inch sewer be constructed in Siuth- road, and that application be made to the Local Govern- ment Board for sanction to a loan of £l5O for the pur- pose.—The report was adopted on the motion of Mr. Wright, seconded by Mr. Chadwick.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT. In his report, Dr. Adams mentioned the outbreak of diphtheria at Newton-by-Frodsham, and stated that three fresh cases had since occurred. A case of scarlet fever had occurred at Norley,and one at Moore. A case of typhoid fever was reported frenn Frndsham. In ac- cordance with instructions he had visited a brink built single storey at Weston Point, occupied by William Thomas Meakin, and also a wooden structure on the north side of the canal, occupied by Reuben Pickering, also at Weston Point, and he certified that they were unfit for the habitation of man. He had also inspected five offensive and objectionable privies behind the Waterman's Arms, Weston Point, and certified that they constituted a nuisance, dangerous and injurious to health.—Mr. Aaron commented upon the unsatisfactory condition of the property mentioned.—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>In submitting the report of the Finance Committee, Mr. Brandreth said there was an account from Mr. Thomas Silcock for £3l 10s, for making out plans for Appleton. The only ether account was a small one from Appleton. The balance in the treasurer's hands was £2,897 3s. 4d.—This report was adapted. NUISANCE INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrington submitted an analyais of the water supply of Halton village, and as such was not SAtilfan- tory, it was decided that two other samples be sub- mitted, one of which should be taken previous to the water entering the Halton reservoir.—An order was made for a sample of water to be submitted for analysis from cottages belonging to the Ship Canal Company at Norton on the recommendation of the Inspector. A certi- ficate of occupation was granted to Mr. William Griffiths for a new house at Helsby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>REAPPOINTMENT OF THE INSPECTOR.</head>
        <p>REAPPOINTMENT OF THE INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Williams moved that the Inspector of Nuisances and the Inspector of the Canal boats, be reappointed at the same salary he is at present receiving. He had learned that Mr. Farrington would have some further duties added in connection with the Factories and Work Shops Act. Generally speaking it would entail the inspection of one or two hundred work-shops in the Union, and he would have to prepare sundry reports for the authorities in London. This would involve more work, and although he did not now propose an addition a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>LATE MR. THOMAS HANDLEY, J.P.,</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. THOMAS HANDLEY, J.P.,</p>
        <p>OF IRIINCO RN.</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE AT BETHESDA CHURCH. Special reference was made to the death ef Mr. Thomas Handley, J.P., of Higher Runcorn, in Bethesda , Congregational Church, by the Rev. J. Simpson, pastor, on Sunday evening. A large congregation was present. The preacher based his remarks on the text :—" Father into Thy hands I commend ins' spirit," Luke xxiii., 46. Referring to the late Mr. Handley, he said that night they were feeling intensely the loss of a dear brother. Across the horizon of their life had passed one whom to know was to love. Some tnen's 'characters fell under one of two classes—Merl to be loved or men to be ad- mired. There were some characters which though you admired, you could not 'cling to them, and others to which you could cling, yet you know them to be weak. A special feature of their beloved friend's character was a combination of gentleness and power. Never did a more tender heart throb beneath the human breast than his. Re had the power, possessed by few men, of invok- ing and ineoiring the trust and affection of those who came in contact with him. This was because of his sympathy and tenderness. Their departed brother qiickly responded to the cry of suffering and need. Need he say anything about the work the deceased did in connection with that church ? it was almost pre- sumptuous to name it. The church was dear to him because of his father's and mother's connection with it, and because he was a thorough Congregational- ist at heart. But he was no narrow sectarian. Be loved all God's people, and was ready to serve them when opportunity was given him. His loss would be felt beyond the bounds of their charch, and that day there were many who were sharers of their sorrow. The town was all the poorer beeause there heti passed From it one of the gentlest and most beautiful spirits. Their friend was God's true gentleman. He possessed tenderness, delicacy of feeling, a keen sense of honour,a quick perceptive in a great degree. He was considerate, patient, and tender. Be subordinated his own feelings for the interests of his fellow men. The truest, sweetest spot on earth their friend had was his own home. He was busily engaged in building a larger one for his growing family when death took him. Their hearts went out to the bereaved family—the aged mother who was lying upon a sick bed waiting patiently to hear the summons of God: the widow, the fatherless children, and to all the bereaved ones their sympathy flawed. Might God grant them all the strength and comfort they might need. Might the burden be not too great for them to bear.—During the service appropriate hymns were sung, and the choir impressively sang" Tne Vital Spark." The Dead Mare.' in "Saul" was played by Mr. C. Le Couteur, the organist, at the close of both morning, arid evening services. , VOTE OF CONDOLENCE FROM THE MUSICAL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The members of the Runcorn Musical Society met for their first rehearsal in Bethesda L-et tire Hall on Tuesday evening, when there was a good attendance. Ten new members were enrolled. The Secretary read &amp;communi- cation from Mr. Brundrit, the president of the society, who expressed his regret at enforced absence, especially on account of it having been his intention at their first ga'hering to propose that a vote of condolence be for- warded to the family of the late Mr. Thomas Handley, who had keenly interested himself in musical matters and had been an earnest and energetic worker in connection with the societv.—Mr. Scott proposed that the secretary write to the widow and express the sympathy of the members with her and the family in their severe domes- tic a ffiction.—Mr. Birkett seconded the proposition in appropriate terms, and the motion on being put to the meeting was unanimously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Knowles presided at the fortnightly meeting of this Board on Tuesday afternoon, held at Dutton Work- house. Amongst those in attendance in addition to the members of thr Rural District Council, there were, the Rev. Canon Wood, Rev. D. Gastaidi and Mr. Yarwood, with Mr. Aghton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), and Mr. Cuonah (workhouse master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>THU LATE MR. THOMAS HANDLEY, J.P.</head>
        <p>THU LATE MR. THOMAS HANDLEY, J.P.</p>
        <p>Before proceeding to the b-u-sinesa of the meeting, the Chairman said he thought they were all aware of the somewhat sudden and unexpected death of a member of the Board, namely Mr. Thomas Handley, who had passed away since they last met. Be would like to propose that the Clerk be asked to forward a letter of condolence to the wife and family. Be felt sure they had the sympathy of every member of the Board in the unex- pected way he had been taken away.—Mr. Brandreth seconded the proposition, and in doing so, said Mr. Handley had been a member of the Board for several years and was very much respected.—The motion was carried unanimougly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>NORLEY AND THE RUNCORN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORLEY AND THE RUNCORN UNION.</p>
        <p>A memorial from the parish meeting of the township of Norlev 'was submitted by the Local Government Board. It set forth the inconveniences which the in- habitants of the township were put to through being connected with the Runcorn Union, and petitioned that the townships might be severed from Runcorn and added o Northwich Union.—Mr. Pickering thought it would be a very good thing for Norley if they were severed from the Runcorn Union. The resolution submitted was passed unanimously at a well attended parish meet- ing.—Nir. Woodward said there was another view of the question which they should consider. They had to look to the interests of the Union generally.—Dr. Gastaldi said it wag a very important matter and it was to the interest of the people of Norley that something should be done. He proposed that a committee be formed to consider the matter —Mr. Darhyshire seconded the pro- position, and it woe carried. FINANCF._ . - -</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Finance Committee (Mr. Hey- wood) submitted the report and estimate of the probable balance in favour of or against each township in the Union at the end of the current half year, and the con- tribution required from the overseers to meet the ex- penditure for the ensuing half year ending Lady Day, 1896. The basis of contributions was calculated on the rateable value as per valuation list in force. The total central for the current half year ending Michaelmas, 1895, amounted to £10,949. and the net probable expen- diture was estimated at £8,228, which showed a bal. ance at Michaelmas of £2,723. The probable expenditure during the ensuing half year ending Lady Dar, 1896, was: common charges, £3,000 ; county rate. £4,177; and balance and contingency fund, £3,000 ; total, £10,177. After deducting the probable hiltnce in favour of the townships at Michaelmas, 1895, the amount required br contribution orders from the overseers was £7,451. The rateable value of the Union,as per valuatson lists in force was £240,551,being an increase of £9,316 compared with the corresponding period last year. A rate of 70. in the £ would produce £7,517, and it was recommended that a call for £7,454 bc. made upon the overseers in accordance with detailed estimates. The calculation was subject to alteration made in valuation lists, previous to the contribution orders being signed, as there were several new and sup- plemental valuation lists to be received. Continuing,he said that the only amounts for cheques were for out- relief, namely £9O for Runcorn, £24 for Frodsham, and £2O for Bud worth. The following townships had not paid their calls i—Alyanley, Antrobus, Budworth, Clif- ton, Hatton, Riagsley, Latchford Without, and Whitley Superior. He recommended that the Clerk write to the townships in question.—The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>THE LADIES' COMMITTEE. Canon Wood introduced the subject of the Ladies' Committee, and stated that the ladies were never made aware of what became of their suggestions to the House Committee. He would move a resolution "that the Clerk be instructed to reply to the communications from time to time of the Ladies' Committee, and that such reply shall be written in the minute book of the said committee."—Admiral Parry seconded the proposition. —Dr. Gastaldi supported.—Mr. Heywood moved an amendment "that the House Committee deal with the ladies' report and enter it in their minute book."—Mr. Yarwried seconded the amendment. —A long discussion ensued, and Mr. Woodward also moved a further amend- ment, hut ultimately on the motion of Mr. Darbyshire, seconded by Mr. Aaron, it was decided that the matter be deferred by 14 votes to 6. This was all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW INDUSTRIES</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW INDUSTRIES</p>
        <p>FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>With regard to the contemplated erection of works by the Aluminium Company and Salt Union Limited, at Weston, there is nothing further to add to what was stated in our previous issue. But we believe that the dirgctors of the United Alkali Com- pany have decided to erect at the works known as Wigg's works, a new and somewhat extensive plant for the conversion of" blue billy" into blocks. In this form we understand that there is an in- creasing demand for it in the manufacture of iron and steel. There is a very large stock of iron ore at these works and if it is the intention of the Com- pany to convert the whole of this into the new form, it seems that a considerable amount of work will be provided for some years to come. News of this kind will prove very acceptable after the severe depression which has existed in the town.</p>
        <p>It is stated that a prolonged strike in the Belfast ship- building yards, affecting 12,000 men, is probable. The notices of the men for an increase of wages will termin- ate on the 26th inst., and the masters have decided to close the yards for an indefinite period rather than grant the demand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN CO-OPERA.TIVE SOCIETY,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERA.TIVE SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>LIMITED.</p>
        <p>RE-ELECTION OF A COMMITTEE MAN..</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the members of the Runcorn Co- operative Industrial and Provident Society, Limited,was held in the Co-operative Hall, Church-st., Runcorn, on Tuesday evening, for the purpose of deciding upon the position of Mr. Riley on the committee. Mr. Riley was elected to that post at the annual meeting in August. Mr. Dyer occupied the chair,and there were also present Messrs. Dudley, Aston, Sayles, Rogerson, and W. Upton (secretary). The Chairman, in opening the proceedings said Mr. Riley's eligibility to serve on the committee was ques- tioned because he was acting as agent for an Insurance Company,and the Society also carried on the same busi- ness. The Secretary had written ta a barrister, taking his advice on the subject, and he had replied that Mr. Riley was disqualified at the thrie of the election, but as he had since given up the insurance business, he was eligible for re-election. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Riley said he had been an agent for some time. rhe society had insured with him, and he had been a member of the committee at that time. He could not see what harm he had done, and nothing had been said against his eligibility previously. Mr. Kniveton said as Mr. Riley had now given up the business of insurance agent he was eligible, and he knew the m .tives which prompted the persons to start this movement against Mr. Riley's position, and sug- gested that he be unanimously re-elected. Mr. W. Millington stated that Mr. Riley had never carried on the same business as the society which was defined in rule one, and he proposed an amendment, " that in the opinion of the meeting Mr. Riley was not disqualified from sitting on the committee." Mr. Robinson snpported Mr. Millington's amendment. Be contended that Mr. Riley was legally elected on the committee as no objection was raised at the annual meeting. He considered that the society had been sub- jected to an attempt by a few persons to override the majority. (Hear, hear.) Mr. G. Millington said he understood from what Mr. Riley had said that he intended to carry on the business of insurance agent again. If he did, be would be dis- qualified.. . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Cozier wished to know by what authority the society were insurance agents? Rule one stated that the society should carry on the trades of dealers in pro- visions, clothing, firing, and other necessaries. Be did not think that insurance was a necessary. After a prolonged discussion, Mr. Millington withdrew his amendment, and Mr. Riley was unanimously re- elected on the committee.</p>
        <p>The adjourned meeting for the revision of rules was then commenced. After various rules had been pasQed under review and adopted, it was agreeJ, Dn the motion of Mr. Dudley, seconded by Mr. Stubbs, that a com- mittee consisting of Messrs. Sayles,Dudley,G. Millington and W, Mdlington, be appointed to examine the rules before being sent to the registrar.—The meeting termin- ated with a vote of thanks to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN CYCLING PARADE AND FLOWER CARNIVAL.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN CYCLING PARADE AND FLOWER CARNIVAL.</p>
        <p>. Mr. Harry Wright (hon. treasurer) writes :—May I trouble you to insert the enclosed copy letters in your next issue and so enable me to sh^w that the funds col- lected have been handed over to tho institutions proposed to he benefited." One of the letters referred to is trom Mr. W. R. Smith, assistant secretary of the Royal Life- boat Institution,in wnich he acknowledges the reciept of " a welcome cheque" for £2O 15s 51. He adds " I en- close a receipt and can assure you that the committee are very grateful to you for your continued valuable co- operation on behalf of the Life Boat cause,which is most opportune at the present time, as the society is much in need of the aid stall its friends. You will, lam sure, be glad to hear that we have this year eranted rewards for the saving of 327 lives." The other letter is from Mr. S. Taylor, hon. treasurer of the District Norte Fund, in which he also acknowleges the receipt of a cheque for £2O 151. 51. Mr. Taylor adds: —.• I am pleased that the Parade was so successful both as regards the splendid procession and also that the col- lection was so large. lam sure your committee must have worked hard to make it such a success, and my committee are exceeding grateful for the help you have given us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ANOTHER THEFT FROM THE LONDON AND NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY COMPANY'S SIDING. , ~. •</head>
        <p>ANOTHER THEFT FROM THE LONDON AND NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY COMPANY'S SIDING. , ~. •</p>
        <p>PROSECUTION OF AN OLD EMPLOYE.</p>
        <p>Oa Wednesday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court, Al, /cinder Clare. Wellfield-st., a brakesman who had been in the employ of the company for 26 year; was charged with having on Sunday, 15th moat, stolen from a waggoa at Bank Quay Station 75 eggs and four tins of condensed milk, the property of the London and INorth-Western Railway Company. Mr. Browne repre- sented the defendant, and Mr. Lambert prosecuted on behalf of the Railway Company. The latter gentleman, in opening the case,said he was sorry to say that the goods yard at Bank Quay had been, during the last few months,the scene of a large number of robberies which had occurred repeatedly. A good deal of these robberies had been confined to the goods traffic passing through the town. In consequence of this a watch had been kept upon the place for some time and officers not known in the locality had searched the yards. On Saturday night Sergeant Kidd, of Man- chester, was, in company with P.C. Barlow of the Borough Police, concealed in the siding. Shortly after 5 o'clock in the morning they saw Clare come to the yard with a lamp and go to a part of the yard where some waggons were stationed, and soon after heard the sound of boxes being broken open. Hiving visited three waggoes he was leaving the yard by the Froghall siding when he was met by a man named Taylor —a fellow workmen—who would be charged subsequently. The officers then closed up and asked Clare if he had any- thing in his basket which did not belong to him and he replied that he had not,hut on being told that he would be taken hack ha said there were a few eggs in the basket. He was then with the other man taken to the Police Station, on the way to which he offered a sovereign to the officers to say nothing about the matter, and afterwards increased his bribe to £5. When searched, his basket was found to contain 75 eggs and 5 tins of condensed milk. Prisoner subsequently pointed out the place where he got them from. Mr. Lambert understood the facts would not be disputed, and it remained for them to deal with the prisoner. He was instructed to point out that it was an exceedingly bad case, because the prisoner had had warning by another case of the same kind, which was brought before the court not long ago. Fie was rather surprised to find a man of such long service and appar- ent good character doing this sort of thing. The annoy- ance and loss to the company were most considerable, claims having come in by the score for goods lost in that particular yard, and under those circu-nstances he was bound to ask that their Worships would make a severe example in that case, so that such proceedings might be put a stop to. Mr. Bowne said that his client had practieally ad- mitted to the officers that he had stolen the goods. What he said was, however, that he he had only taken 14 eggs, but as such a contention was not of much moment he would plead guilty to the charge of 75 eggs and 5 canisters of milk. The prisoner,Mr. Browne added,had never taken a single thing in his life before, although he had 26 years with the company, and had taken some of the most important trips that had left the town as ilbrakesman. On the night in question he had just come II home by the 330 train from Liverpool and booked off duty. In order not to disturb his wife and family by returning home unexpectedly he asked for the van to be shunted in the northern portion of the siding so that he could sleep there. This was done and he went to sleep. On awaking he noticed a wagon near him open, and unfortunately he was tempted and fell. Mr. Browne asked the Bench to remember that the prisoner was a man with a good character and nothing known against him previously. The charge deprived him of his situation and he had now no living in his hands. Under these circumstances, Mr. Browne ventured to think that they would take into consideration the fact that he had been locked up from Sunday morning—a degradation in itself, and he thought the case would be amply met if they inflicted a fine upon the prisoner. Prisoner was a lifelong teetotaler and had lived in War- rington all his life.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said they thought it was a serious case but as the prisoner had previously borne a good char- acter and that was his first appearance in the dock,they would, instead of imprisonment, fine him £5 including costs, or in default of payment one month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>John Taylor was charged with stealing seven eggs, on the same occasion. Mr. Lambert again prosecuted, and pointed out that although Taylor said he had taken the eggs out of Clare's basket by way of devilment, he was found by the officers in possession of stolen property of which he did not give a satisfactory account. Mr. For=haw defended, and after testifying to his client's good character of 16 years standing with the company, said that he had stepped into Clare's van to see him. The latter was not there and defendant notic- ing some eggs in a basket took out seven for a lark and without the slightest idea that Clare had stolen the eggs, as it was customary for brakesmen going to Holyhead to bring hack eggs with them as they got them cheap. Taylor afterwards met Clare as he was going home. Mr. Forshaw submitted that there was no felonious intention on the defendant's part and he applied for a dismissal. The magistrates considered that there was considerable doubt in the case and riving Taylor the benefit of it they would dismiss the charge.</p>
        <p>It is announced from St. Petersburg that the German Government has decided to j in Russia and France in putting pressure on Japan with a view to bringing about the evacuation of the Liaotung Peninsula.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WA.RRENGTON TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>WA.RRENGTON TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT THE CONTINUATION SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual distribution of prizes and scholarships in connection with the Continuation School took place at the People's College. The large school was filled with young people of both sexes, and there were present, representing the Technical School Commit ee—Mr. F. Monks (the chairman), Councillors F. W. Wood and Shaw-Green. The Mayoress attended to distribut the prizes, and was supported by Miss Fair- dough and Mrs. Madeley. The chair was occupied by Mr. Monks, who opened the proceedings by calling upon Mr. Madeley, the secretary to the committee, to read the report, which was as follows : CONTINUATION SCHOOL REPORT, 1894-5.</p>
        <p>It is pleasant in the first place to be able to report that we had last session a much larger number of pupils in the Continuation School than in the previous year. The toful number who joined-in our first year was 146, in the second 197, and last year it was 388. This great increase obliged us to divide our classes in the more popu- lar subjects. We thus had three or tour, and even (in arithmetic) five separate clas3es in a single subject. . . . . . .</p>
        <p>The amount of work done in the schools and the bene- fits to be derived from it depend not only on the number of those whojoin, but even more upon the regularity with which they attend the classes. The school was openel 83 nights during the session, and the committee offered a prize of a certain value to each student who at- tended for two hours on fifty evenings, that is to say on two nights a week regularly, and a prize of a less value to those who attended 35 evenings,which would be equal to on' night a week and about ten nights over. Out of the 368 students 44 earned the higher prize and 35 the lower one. The general body of the students attended fairly well, but there was, as usual, a great falling off after Christmas. The most popmar subjects were, as in previous years, the most generally useful ones, English composition and arithmetic, and the other subjects followed in this order: drawing,tnodern history, shorthand, needlework, chemistry, cookery, mechanics, English grammar. .</p>
        <p>With regard t) the instruction given, it may be said that the school was visited several times by her Majesty's Inspectors, who examined the wark going on in every class, with the result that the highest grant allowable was given for each subject, and the Education Department wrote at the end of the session" A varied and well-considered syllabus of work has been efficiently carried out." For this gratifying, result the committee is indebted to the efforts of Mr. Hall (the superintendent) and the other teachers of the school.</p>
        <p>At the examinations held in the spring, under the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes, 119 papers in various subjects were worked by students of the C3O- - School. Sitting at these examinations is not compulsory, but upon them chiefly depend the award of the scholarships offered by the committee. The classes which came out best at the examinations were the arith- metic, in which only one-fourth failed,and the drawing, cookery, and needlework, in none of which were there any failures.. _ . . .</p>
        <p>- On the result of these and other examinations, the committing has granted 23 scholarships, which are intended to pay the class fees for those who gain them for the next session (i.e., the one now commenc- mg). The following is a list of the scholarships riven Two (value 103. each, which will pay for School of Art or for three subjects in the School of Science) to Esther M'Garty and Joseph Tilling. Five (7s. 6d. each, equal to two classes in School of ' Science) : 'Robert Carpenter, \Marshall Gandy, Stanley ' Harnilpon, KAU° Miller, Wilfrid Rigby. Three (ss. each, equal to one class in School of Science): Mary J. Carpenter, John M. Shaw, Harold Sumner.</p>
        <p>Thirteen (ss. to pay. Continuation School fee for another session) : Alfred Arnold,Frederick Baker,William Carpenter, Emily Houghton, Mary A. Lea, James Magrath, Charles Mason, Allen Parr, John Vernon,Eliza- beth Walker, John H. Wildman.</p>
        <p>• The importance of regular attendance is shown by this fact, that more than three-quarters of the certificates, hence all the scholarships except one, are taken by pupils who also receive prizes for attendance. For the present session very much the same arrange- ments have been made as last year, nearly the same classes have been provided, the same rewards promised, and if the students endeavour by regular attendance and work to earn them, next year there will be a still longer list of prizes, certificates and scholarships to give away. At the conclusion of the report the MAYORESS said she was very glad indeed to be there and to distribute the prizes, for she took an interest in these classes, but more especially those in cookery and needlework. She ad- vised the girls to attend these particularly, so as to be- come more useful and if possible learn to make nine- pence do the work of a shilling, for that was what they would have to do. She was very pleased to hear of the increased attendance, and hoped that the usefulness and prosperity of the classes might contiaue. The Mayoress then distributed the prizes and certifi- cates, and at the close The CHAIRMAN said be had a duty to perform in which he was sure all would join, and that was to thank the Mayoress for her attendance that night—(cheers)--but before he did that he would like to say how pleased he was to see so large a number of young men and women present. It was very gratifying to him to see so many taking advantage of the school. He was old enough to remember the days when there was nothing except the classes in connection with the Mechanics' Institute for the young men who wish-d to continue their education, and the young people of the present day had no idea of the difficulties that had to be overcome at the time of which he was speaking. Everyone of them should have an object in attending the classes, and of this he was sure, that in years to come they would be greatly bene- fited by the intellectual efforts they were making that day. Some of them probably would be the future manu- facturers of Warrington. The advantages offered by these classes were such as to make theru fit for any posi- tion in life, and by their attendance whilst young they were laying the foundation for future successes. They might take it as the result of an experience of over fifty Wars that the way to get on was to improve every faculty, and to make the most of the opplrtunities they had. The committee was pleased to help and encourage them in every way. He then formally proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayoress,which was seconded by Coun- cillor SH&amp;W GREEN, and carried unanimously. At the close a lantern lecture upon the Geography of the British Empire was given by Mr. Hall, the lantern being manipulated by Mr. D. Stone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>PADGATE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services in connection with Christ Church, Padgate, which commenced on Thursday, were continued on Sunday. The sermon was preached in the morning by the Vicar and the choir sang the anthem commencing "Thou shalt show us beautiful things," Master Burrell taking the treble solo. In the evening the church was filled. The sermon was preached by the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, M.A., vicar of Runcorn. The evensong was fully choral, end was accompanied on the cornet by Mr. Thomas Armstrong, of Warrington, in an efficient manner. The anthem was "I will feed My filelc," Mr. William Fryar singing the tenor solo in good style. Mr. Frank Blackwell was at the organ. Offer- tories were taken in aid of the Sunday schools, and amounted to £lO 12s. sd. PADGATE WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services in connec- tion with the above church were held on Sunday. The interior presented a very charming appearance, as the various parts of the church had been decorated in a most tasteful manner, by young ladies who attend the church. Plants had been kindly lent by Messrs. George Andrews, E. Richardson, Joseph Bennett, and Joseph Andrews. The pulpit was occupied at the morning service by the Rev. W. G. Triggs. ths newly appointed minister to the Warrington circuit, who preached from the words " Honour the Lord with thy substance, and with the first fruits of all thine increase. So shall thy barns be , filled with plenty, and thy presses shall burst out with new wine." A successful service was also held in the afternoon. There was a large congregation at the even- ing service, when the Rev. G. D. Mason preached. Special hymns were sung, and at the evening service the choir sang the anthem "I will rejoice in the Lord," Miss Mary Bennett and Mr. James Bennett singing the solos effectively. Special collections were made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thankqgiving services were con- tinued on Sunday in Winwick Church, when Holy Sac- rament was administered at eight o'clock in:the morning and at half-past ten a morning service was held, when the Rector, preached an appropriate sermon. In the afternoon a sermon was preaehed by the Rev. J. Canon, IM.A., rector of Wargrave, Newton-le-Willows. Mr. E. IWeeks, the organist and choirmaster, presided at the organ. The collections during the day were aid of the expenses of the church, and the total amount received at the services on Thursday and Sunday was £l6 14s. Bd. The church had been very artistically and tastefully de- corated with choice plants, cut flowers, fruit, grain, vegetables, Ste. DARESBITRY.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services were held in the Parish Church on Sunday, when two sermons were preached by the Rev. H. Wynue Jones, vicar of Carnar- von. The services commenced with an early celebration of the Holy Communion at eight a.m., followed by morn- ing service at 10.30, and evensong at 6.30. Unusually large congregations attended each service. The sacred edifice was neatly adorned with fruit, corn, and flowers, far surpassing the work of former years. The decoration of the chanrel was undertaken by Mrs. and Miss Whitley and Miss Miller ; the pulpit by Mr. Calder; the chancel screen by Mrs. and Miss Litton ; the font by Miss Black. lock; the belfry screen by Messrs. E. de Prez, P. Hamblett, and William Oulton ; and the pillars and windows, Sz^.. by Misses Darlington, Horrabin, Mrs. Bi,lington. Mrs. Wright, Messrs. Billingtnn, Hayward, Warburton, Sanderson, and Houghton. The offertories were devoted to the fund for the support of the Sunday schools in the parish and realised nearly £l5.</p>
