      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN 'RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN 'RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUE.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the members of the Runcorn Club was held on Friday evening for the purpose of considering the action of the committee in joining the Northern Union. The chair was occupied by Mr. P. Speakman, j.p., C.C., and amongst those present were Messrs. Longshaw (secretary),J. Ellison (assistant secretary).W. Carroll (chairman of committee), W. Jones (vice-chair- man), Henry Cawley, J. Fraser, G. Robinson,:S.Abrams arid J. Lightfoot (committee), and Dr. McKenzie. Mr. Carroll, at the reque4 of the Chairman, explained the action of the committee. He said they all knew that some months ago a number of Yorkshire and Lancashire organizations had disagreements with the county autho- ritiei, with the result that the formation of a Northern Union was proposed. They were invited to take part in . the preliminaries, but feeling that they were purely a Cheshire club,and therefore quite outside the two coun- ties mentioned,they thought it well to watch the course of events and remain quiet. The next thing they heard was that a Northern Unien had been formed, and in it were the cream of the clubs with whom Runcorn had fixtures. The subject was then carefully considered,and it being thought advisable to j dn it,steps were taken for that purpose. He (Mr. Carroll) and the Secretary went to Manchesterond met the te gentlemen managing the Northern Union, and with very little trouble they were admitted. It was for that meeting to say whether the action taken met with their approval. He did not think they would be acting fairly unless they told them that the great objection raised was on the score of expense. He might say they had a team as anxious as the com- mittee for the club to be free from debt. (Hear, hear.) The players had met them in a right loyal fashion. They ' said if they were paid a day's wages for visits to York-. . shire, and received an increase of 61. in hotel expenses for Lancashire, they would abide toy ally by the committee, and thus try and clear off the ha- lailities of the club. (Applause.) The only thing they had to fear was on the score of expense, and he did not think that joining the Union would make a difference of more than £4O in tiaat direction, and they had every reason to believe that far more than that would be re- ceived from the extra gates. The committee had thought the matter well over,and done what they considered best for the interests of the eub. (Applause.) Dr. McKmzie said he had studied the matter very closely, and his fear waethat they would not'be allowed ' to join the Northern Union. This would have meant - being reduced to play fourth or fifth-rate organizations, such as Frodsham, Iletahy, &amp;c., (laughter)—and their ' playing members would have been induced to leave Run- corn for better spheres. In his opinion the committee had acted very wisely,- and no one was more proud of this than himself, He moved that they endorse the ac- tion of the committee in joining the Northern Union. (Applause ) Mr. T. Watson seconded the proposition. He did BO becau4e he could not see how, under the circumstances, they could have adopted a different course.</p>
        <p>The Chairman was pleased that Dr. McKenzie had moved and Mr. Watson had sPeonded the prepositionin such able terms. The action of the committee entirely met his views. He had had a great deal of conversation,. with Mr. Carroll, and had als consulted their Secretary. "1 and as a consequence he found that they had only One object in view, and that was the success of the Runcorn Football Club. He did not k that be had ever been better plPased that he was OFI Saturday, when he wit- nessed the first match under the new regulations. It was a pleasant one in every respect. In the first place their players did so nobly, and in the second Widnes were their opponents. In other seasons there had been a feeling among local people, but he trusted in the future that nothing but harmony would prevail between the two organizations. ne was delighted to hear Mr. Carroll ' state that the teams were willing to help the committee out of their little difficulty. With regard to the extra gate, he believed the spirit with which they had started had given them an impetus,and that the talent they had in their midst would maintain that supremacy hitherto held, and would keep the club up to high water mark. The motion was then put and unanimously carried. Mr. Longshaw explained that that was the first meet- ing of the members since the honour of baronetcy was conferred upon their member by her Majesty,and there- fore he had great pleasure in proposing that they present their Congratulations to him and Lady Brunner. He , alluded to the handsome way in which Sir John had treated the organisation' and made reference to the valuable assistance he had rendered to the town. He thought they must all feel very grateful to their Parlia- mentary representative.—Mr. Walter Jones cordially seconded the motion, which was unanimously passed. The Chairman explained that they were exceedingly sorry that their captain, Mr. William Faulkner, was absent,and especially on account of the cause, namely a death in his family. He thought they ought not to part without forwarding a votes! condolence to their captain in his bereavement. He moved a proposition to that effect.—Mr. Arthur Howard seconded the motion which was adopted. Mr. Moulton proposed a vote of thanks to the com- mittee for having so successfully guided them through a difficult crisis. He trusted the public would support the club to the best of their ability.—Mr. Craig seconded the pmposition.which was adopted.—Mr. Car- roll, in responding, said they were a happy family, and be could assure them with such a committee the fortunes of the club would not suffer.—Mr. Longshaw also re- plied, after which the meeting closed with a vote of thanks to the Chairman,</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL UNION ANNUAL MEETING. THE NEW PROFESSIONAL RULES. The annual gederal meeting of the Rugby Football Union was held on Thursday night at the Cannon-street Hotel, London. A huge agenda was down for discussion, consisting principally of the proposed rules against ro- fessionalism in the game. At one time it was anticipated that these proposals would give rise to considerable discussion,but the withdrawal of the extreme opponents to such ascheme as was put forth and the formation of a Northern Union had the effect of de,troying almost all opposition. The result was that little interest was shown in the proceedings comparod with the last two or three years, and there were not a hundred delegates present at sevenlo'clock.when the meeting was timed to commence. Shortly after that hour Mr. R. Walker, the retiring presi- dent, took the chair. He was supported by Mr. G. Row- land Hill (hon. secretary), Mr. W. Call (Northumberland) Mr. A. Budd (Blackheath), Mr E. T. Holmes (Midland Counties), Mr. H. W. T. Garnett (Yorkshire), Mr. G. H. Harnett (Kent),Mr. H. Vassal! (Oxford and Cambridge), the Rev. F. Marshall (Yorkshire), Mr. A. F. Birnie (Surrey), Mr. G. H. Miller (Yorkshire),Mr. E. T. Gurdon (vice-president), Mr. J. MacLaren (Lancashire), Mr. R. S. Whalley, Mr. J. W. 'Thorpe. Mr. Cail, the treasurer, presented the accounts, remarking that they had a bal- ance of £2ll at the commencement of the year, and this year, after giving £lOO more to charities that they were compelled to do by their laws, they had a balance of £it43 odd, besides which they had £2,000 invested in Consols.</p>
        <p>In the course of the meeting, the Chairman made a reference to the movement in the north in favour of pay- ment for broken time, which had resulted in the resigna- tion of about twenty-two clubs in Lancashire, Yorkshire and Cheshire. The Committee, he said, bad had to deal with that matter, and Mr. Rowland Hill would report the result of their deliberations.—The Hon. Secretary (Mr. Rowland Hill) said that the Committee had declared that the members of the Northern Union Clubs were. under the Rugby Union rules, professionals. (Cheers.) They decided that that ruling should not include those members of clubs which belong to the Northern Union who, by November 1 neat, did resign their membership of their club, or in case of amalgamated clubs resign their connection with the Rugby section thereof, and at once report such resignation to their respective county committees. No Rugby Union club or player might play on the ground of a professional Rugby club. No profes- sional Rugby club might play on the ground of a Rugby Union club. No Rugby Union club might employ as referee or touch judge any member of a professional RugUy club. No member of a Rugby Union club might act as referee or touch judge in any game in which a professional player took part. (Cheers.)—On the motion of Mr. Davies (Essex), seconded by the R.v. F. Marshall, the Committee were heartily thanked for their efforts to keep the Rugby Union purely and solely amatsur,—All the opinions expressed were unanimously in favour of the continuation of amateurism.</p>
        <p>Where to Go for my Holidays? This is a question which is nose occupying the a tten ' Lion of hundreds of our readers. Now the various health resorts of thY British Islands, as well as the Con- nent, will be shortly flocked with visitors. Second only in importance to la here to go to ? If, indeed, second te that is its natural corollary : How shall I de- rive the most benefit from my holiday ? Now, any exp rienced mJical man will tell you tha a course of some suitable tonic medicine taken during the change will double the beneficial effects of the holi- day. The appetite for food will be sharpened, the as- similating powers of the stomach will be increased, the nervous system will be strengthened, the blood will be. purified, and the indispensable "tone "regained by the system, mucn more certainly and eftectually by suchla course of medicine taken during the holiday alone or by the medicine alone. Each helps the other, and the ex- perienced in these matters always take both together. I Great care, however, mast be exercised in the choice of your tonic. Many so-called remedies are absolutely injurious. There, is, however, one which long and uni- versal experience proves to be absolutely trustworthy, that can do no harm to the most delicate constitution, and has practically in every instance where it has been tried, even proved to be most beneficial, and, indeed, the most suitable tonic medicine for all such cases. This is the well-known preparation known as G wilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. TIIE VEGESABLE TONIC. WELT &amp;RE ITS EFFECTS? 1. It assists and prom Ate Digestion. 2. It strengthens the Nerves and Muscles. 3. It Cleanses and Puriftes the Blood. 4. It Enlivens the Spirits. 5. It removes all Obstructions and Impurities from the human body. 6. It gives Tone to the whole system. Sold in Bottles at is. li., 24. 91, and 4e. 6d. each. Beware of Imitations! See the name of.. " Gwilym Evans" on Label. Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors : QUININE Burl EHS MA.NI3FACTURING CO., LIMITED. LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE NEXGISTRATES. TER DIFFERENCE BETWEEN DRITNIENNESS AND INEBRIATION. At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor and Mr. F. Monks,Wilmot Webster, 28, Buttermarket-street, was charged with being drunk in Buttermarket.street,while in charge of a horse and trap, on September sth. In reply to the charge, the defend- ant replied very positively, " Not guilty my lords." P.C. J. Cooper and P.C. Williamson said that at twenty minutes to ten on the above date, they saw the defend- ant in charge of a trap in Buttermarket-street, and he was very drunk at the time. He also used some strong language to them when they took his name and address. As he had not been before the magistrates pre- viously, they allowed him to go home.—The defendant then prefaced his defence by addressing the Bench as "My Lords." Mr. Monks, however, informed him that neither he nor the Mayor were lords,and defendant then corrected himself by saying," Your Worships then." He went on to say that he was not drunk; he was only a little bit inebriated, and knew what he was doing.—Mr. Monks : What is the difference between " drunk " and " inebriated ? "—Defendant : Oh, a great difference. "Drunk " means when a man is Pupposed to be unable to take care of himself, while " inebriated" means being a bit jally." (Laughter.") He emphatically denied the statements of the officers that he was drunk. —The Mayor said that they believed he was" drunk," and he might have killed some onelf the officers had not stepped him. They would be lenient with him on that occasion as it was his first offence, and they would fine him ss. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT. Faruev.—Before Alderman Wright (chairman), Council- lor Wherts, anti Mr. J. H. Fleming. DRUM( &amp;ND DISORDERLY CONDUCT. —John Thomas Robinson, 4, Mill-Bt., was fined 5. and costs or in de- fault seven dare hard labour for being guilty of drunk- anness.coupled with disorderly conduet,in Scotland-road at 11.5 on Thursday night, STEALING POTATOES.—A FARMER'S CeniEvescs.—Ernest Garner, a boy, 6,David-st., was charged with having on the 7th September stolen potatoes to the value of Bd., the property of Messrs. Bennett Bros.—P.C. Turner said that on Saturday morning last at 6 45 he saw the defend- ant coming from the direction of Bennett's potato field, Folly-lane, carrying potatoes. When questioned he said where he got the vegetables, and took witness to the spot. Defendant further added that his Tne- ther sent him for them, but afterwards with- drew this statement.—Mr. Bennett, the protimutor, said he was sorry to have to bring a case up but his crops had suffered such tremendous damage by theft that he was obliged to resort to such measures.—As the lad appeared repentant and bore a former good charac- ter the bench dismiased him after administering a severe caution and ordering payment of the costs. These latter were paid by a neig,hbour of the defendant's mother,yrno testified to the poor circumstances of the family, and added that the lad had done several odd jobs for her and always appeared honest and truthful. litotamix.—Befnre the Mayor (Councillor James Fair- slough), and Mr. F. Monks. OBSTRUCTION OF TTIE ROAD—William Bailey, Golden Grove-et., was fined ss. including costs for causing an obstruction of the way in Scetland-rsad,on August 31st. P.C. Clough, who proved the case' said that the defend- ant left a truck for an hour and ten minutes between Walker's Vaults and the theatre in Scotland-road, on the night in question. Defendant said hia truck was painted exactly similar to some others, and that he was blamed for them. The officer, however, asserted that his truck remained in the same place the whole of the</p>
        <p>time. FIGHTING —Matthias Wends, 17 Orford-t. was bound over to keep the peace for six months and also ordered to pay the costs for fighting in Golden Grove-street, on September 7th.—A warrant was issued against William Connor, 20, Dolman's lane, with whom it was alleged Woods was fighting with. PUIOUS DRITINCI.—CharIPR Blackshaw, farmer, of Hatton, Cheshire,was charged with furiously driving his horse in Wildersponl Causeway, on September 12th.— P.C. Evans and P.C. Turner proved the case, and de- fendant was fined as 61 including costs. AN Vt./LICENSED DRITEII —Andrew Clark, 13, Bennett. it., was charged with being the driver of a hackney coach without having a license, and Peter Magi= was Charged with causing him to so work it.—P.S. Taylor proved the case,and a representative of Mr. Maginn ex- plained that the defendant Clark was sent out because Mr. Maginn was rather pressed for work at the time.— Maginn was fined ss. and Clark 2s. 6d. Tugsmiy,—Bonre Mr. E J. Guest (chairman), Mr. R. Garnett, and Dr. T. Starkey Smith. lissmuya.—Mark and Michael Burke, father and son, were charged with begging in Bold-st., on the previous night.—P.C. Bell said that he saw the prisoners begging in Bold-st., and presently he saw Michael in Dr. Sher- ratt's garden. When witness went to him, the defend- ant said that he wanted to sea Dr. Sherratt to find out what was the matter with his brother who had died some time ago.—Dr. Voisey gave evidence of the pri- soners begging, and the Bench decided to send theta to gaol for 14 days. DRUNMENNESS.—The following were summoned for drunkenness: Mary Hill and Catherine .Hughes, ordered to leave the town ; Thomas Lawrenson, discharged with • caution ; and Benjamin Roberts, and David Petree fined ss. including cost. SFXDssanay.—Before M. John Fairclough (chairman), and Mr. J. Geddes. A CHEWS 'MAN n/ Tammuz —Charles Browne, a mar- ried man, hailing from Crewe, was charged with assault- ing his son William Browne, in Scotland-road on Tuesday morning. He was further charged with being drunt and disorderly in Portland-st. on the same day.—He pleaded guilty to both charges, and the Bench fined him ss. and costs in each case. Drama AND DISORDERLY.—Mary Hill, a dissipated looking person, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly on Tuesday afternoon at 2.30. From the evi- -dence adduced it appeared that she had been charged with a similar offence the morning before and ordered to leave the town. She was now fined 10s. and costa or in default 14 days' hard labour ; James Williams for the Same offence OD Tuesday night, was fined Se.; Daniel Crosby, for similar behaviour in Bridge-st.. was fined ss. and costs, and William Jackson was fined ss. DRUNI AND ASSAULTING THE FOLICH.—Ann Hickey and Sarah Hickey were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-st.,at 4.30 en Tuesday after- noon • Sarah Hickey was further charged with assault- ing P.C. David Jones whilst in the executiou af his duty at the same time and place.—P.C. Jones stated that at the time in question he was at Market Gate,and hearing a disturbance down Buttermarket-st.,be proceeded there and found the two prisoners drunk and fighting. He took Sarah Hickey into custody, but as she became very violent and struck him several times in the face, he had to get the assistance of P.C. Barnett to take her into eustody.—Mr. Hunt explained that Ann Hickey was the mother of Sarah and had come from London to take her borne, She had not been up before whereas her daughter had.—The magistrates fined Sarah Hickey 103. and costs or If days.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNIODAL—Betore Captain Withington and Mr. John White.</p>
        <p>Iwriudrau.swcz.—Samuel Jmes, Sankey, was fined 23. Bd. and costs for being drunk and disorderly on the Bth Sept.—Martin Flanaghan, Riaton, was fined ss. in- cluding costs for the same offence. Tins Ray. A. M MITCHELL AND HIS DRAINAGE.—The Rev. A. M. Mitchell, vicar of Burtonwpod, was charged that on the 17i h July last, he failed to divert from the highway sewage from his residence, as required by the Local Authority.—The defendant did not appear.—Mr. F. Kerfoot,nuisance inspector,said the proceedings were taken in reference to a nuisance at the New Vicarage, Burtonwood. On the 9:h April a notice was served upon Mr. Mitchell to abate a nuisance caused by the drainage from his house such as wash closets and slop- stone refuse being turned into the highway. He was asked to put down a cesspool and turn the drainage therein. Up to the present nothing had been done and proceedings had th•refore to be taken. At a previous hearing arbitration had been resorted to and the de- fendant was asked to put down a tank, but as he had not done so, Mr. Kerfoot asked for an order with costs. The order was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RUNCORN PE I' r Y SESSIONS Mosnkr.—Before Mr ; ohn Brimdrit (chairman), and __ _ _ _ .</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PE I' r Y SESSIONS Mosnkr.—Before Mr ; ohn Brimdrit (chairman), and __ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. G. L. Davies. A NOTED Cireascree.—inn Corcoran, of Salkeld- square, who has frequently been charged at Runcorn Police Court, again appeared to answer two charges of being drunk and disorderly, one of the Bth September. and the other of the 15th inst., in Mersey-st.—P.o Ric- hardson proved the first charge, and S-Tgeant Gill the second.—lnspector Hicks said the prisoner's conduct was unbearable.—She was committed to gaol for 14 days on each charge. DISTREMED RATEPAYERS.—For non-payment of the poor rate seven persons were summoned, and eight bad been summoned for the nou-payment of the water rate, and seven for the District rate. In cases where the money had not already been paid, orders were made for the payment of the rate. THE MAINTENANCE OF a BLIND GlBL.—James Roo/bot- tom was summoned at the instance of Mr. Bowyer for Runcorn School Board, who claimed the sum of £2 43. for the maintenance of his (defendant's) step-child in accordance with an order made by the magistrates On the Bth April, 1895.—Mr. Bowyer stated that on April Bth, the magistrates made an order for the defendant I to contribute the sum of 2s. per week towards the main- tenance of his step-child. who was attending the school for the blind at Liverpool. Since the order had been made there had rot been paid a single penny—Defendant stated that they took the girl away out of the house without his permission. He had not been getting money enough to contribute the money.—Mr. Bowyer informed the bench that the defendant had stated that he did not intend to pay a penny. He (Mr. Bowyer) had been down to a brickyard where the defendant worked, and he found that he was receiving ed. per hour,and his wages during the past six weeks had. averaged 253. a week. The Board were paving £2l a year or 84. a week for the child's maintenance.—The Chairman said the defendant was bound to take over his wife's liabilities. It would cost him more than 2s. a week to maintain the child. The magistrates would make an order for the money to be paid within seven days.—Defendant : Well I shan't pay it. SCHOOL ATTNNDANCE CASES.—For neglecting to cause their children to attend school regularly the following persons wire each fried ed. and 43. CM 'costs : Thomas Cawley, boatman ; Job Nield, Handley-street ; Thomas Jones, Surrey-st., William Marshall, Gilbert-st. ; Anni - Okell, Nelson-st. ; and William Ellis, Surrey-st. Mr. Bowyer, school attendance officer, prosecuted.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS. SATURDAY.—Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, Nlr. R. Stone,and Mr. C. B. F. Berron. DRUNICENNESS.—The following werr fined for drunken- ness :—Jonathan Gleave, and Joseph Lawrenson, Hay- dock ; John Flaherty and William Rona.yne. Newton ; Patrick McAllen and Peter Travis, Golborne ; James Harrison, Jonathan Brown, W. H. Hutchinson, VVilliam Barber, John Mack, Thomas Hetherington, Esther Mackie and Ellen Withington, Haydock. Doe AT LARGE.—Shadrack Webber, Golborne, was fined ss. Bd. for this offence. DAMAGING GARDEN PEAS.—John Cotton was fined 10s. and costs for damaging some garden peas to the extent of ed., the property of Edward List, at Golborne, en August 10. ALLEGED DAMAGE TO A RAILWAY EMBANKMENT.— Winstantley and John Asninall were each charged that at Haydock, on August 18, they committed damage to the embankment of the Liverpool, St. Helens awl South Lancashire Railwey Company to the extent of is. Mr. J. W. Whitworth of Sheffield,. who repre- sented the Company, said that the two men were walk- ing along the embankment on the ab we data, and while doing so, they plucked some herbs which had been sown for the purpose of strengthening the embankment. Considerable damaen of this description was continually being done. The defendants said that all they did was to pull up a bit of yarrow. Thes were not trespassing, —The Bench inflicted a penally of 8,1. and costs. SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST VIRE&amp; Boys—Samuel Stevenson, Walter Myerscough, and Edwin Hart, were each charged on August 16th, at Ashton, for feloni- ously placing a wooden spout upon the rails of the Liverpool, St. Helens, and South Lancashire Railway Comoany.—Mr Whitworth on behalf of the Company said that shortly after five o'ctock a goods train was travelling between Sl...Helens and Lowton S. Mary'e,and after rounding a curve at Aehton Station, the fireman noticed an obstruction on the rails about 100 yards in front. He also noticed the three defendants down the side of the embankment,watching the train. The engine was stopped about 20 yards from the obj •ct, which con- sisted of a long wooden ?Tout peeked up with bricks, and the fireman then left the engine and followed the lads, who had run away when the locemetive was brought to a standstill. Ba caught Stevensen, and after- wards got the names of the other two defendants. IF the eneine had left the rails the driver, fireman, and guard might have been killed —William Arstall,fireman. stated that he WAS on the locomotive when be perceived the obstruction near Ashton. Be caught one of the tnys and asked him the reason why he hid laid the spout on the rails,and he replied "The ether two hors deLl it." The obstruction stood about 18 inches above the rails, and if the wheels of the engine had mounted this object the result might have been the death of these in charge of the train. They were travelling at the rare Of about. 25 miles an hour, and only had bre waggons attached.— Edward Thomas Norman, enee'e guard on the Manches- ter, Sheffield and Lincolnshire R ilwav, corroborated.— Mr. Stuart said that the case was a very serious one.and the boys ought to be punished in some way for their mischief. The line in the particular spot in question was unprotected, and he admitted that the boys had placed the snout on the line, but nut with any intent to wreck the train. There Was a water tank near the spot where the obstruction wla laid on the metals, and the boys had placed the spout in that plac,. in order that the water mieht run from the tank through the spout, and over on to the other side of the rail crey. Although they had trespassed and done such a silly act, it. would be ridiculous for anyone to think that. lade of their age had intended to endanger the lives of those in charge of the train.—After a short consultation the Bench fined Stevenson 10s. and costa, and ordered the other two defendants to each receive six strokes of the birch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
