        <p>Total • Decre.se.</p>
        <p>THE COTTON TRADE ND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The strenuous efforts put forth to secure a portion of the c.tton traffic between America and Liverpool for Manchester are apparently about to bear good fruit, as already several large steamers have been fixed from the American cotton ports direct to Manchester. It is stated on good authority that a company in Manchester have arranged to place a number of vessels en the berths at the cotton ports for Manchester during the season. Four vessels have already been fixed far Manchester. The Joseph John will clear from Galveston on the I.sth prox., and a vessel will at the same time receive cotton at New Orleans for this port. The steamers Teutonic and Dar- lington have also been fixed with cotton direct to Man- chester. On Monday the steamer Riela sailed for Glas- gow, la here she will receive a quantity of cargo, after which she will return to receive For the Brazils. The steamer Micmas is now receiving cargo in the Salford docks for New York. The Clan M'Kay is being discharged in the Salford docks. The total amount of tea which will be discharged in Manchester by the vessel exceeds 2,600 packages.</p>
