      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>THE Fa 4iNl INFP,. rI'ITT;TI SPPTEMPF,R, 21 1Q(15</p>
        <p>and Buckley, forwards. Hull: Johnson, full back ; rhomption, lampriere, ,J. Townsend, Mahoney, three quarters; C. Townsend and Barker, half backs ; Hamer, tteetham, Mermen, Jacketts, Mansell, Holmes, Booth, and Spenceley, forwards. The game was commenced iialf an hour late,owing to the Hull team waiting for a new player they expected by a later train. He did not arrive however, until about ten minutes after the game had commenced, and the reserve man Mennell, was played in his place. There were between 4,000 and 5,000 spectators, when Barber having won the toss,Turner kicked off towards the pavi- lion goal. Townsend teturned and followed up so smartly that he placed Hull onside. Sure tackling by Warring- ton 12.-wever prevented further progress,and a scrimmage took place in the visitors' quarters. A free kick fell to Bar ber,and Bate attempting to prevent the return was so much injured on the forehead that he was compelled to leave the field for about fifteen minutes,and hie place at half back was taken by Berry. With 14 men, War- rington were unable to keep Hull in check, and Thomp- son by a timely run and punt put his side on the ag- gressive. J. Townsend still further increased the advan- tage and Boscow was repeatedly called upon. At length from a forward rush,Feetham wriggled over near the goal posts,and Thompson had no difficulty with the easy place kick. Hull were now five points ahead and the game only five minutes old. Turner restarted the game, and Dakin followed with a rush and some spirited play took place. Berry smartly securing the ball passed to O'Brien. The latter in turn passed to Barber, and the latter was well on his way to tha line when he unfoitu- nately stepped into touch. From the line out, Buckley secured, but kicking too hard the ball went dead. A scrimmage ensued after the kick out, atid C. Townsend cleverly tricked Berry and Foden and got clear away, but the pass to Lamphire was knocked forward, and a good chance was lost. At this stage Bate took his posi- tion again at half back. His re-appearance for a time did not improve matters, and Warrington were hard pressed, Broady especially coming in for plenty of work. On one occasion he almost let Hull in by missing his mark, hut he atoned for this by pluckily picking up the ball and sending in touch. The ball was at length got over the Warrington lines,but Boacow touched down and thus staved off the danger. Bate followed the kick out, and securing, passed to Broady, the latter finding touch at the Hull quarter flag From thr ensuing line out and scrimmage, a grand bout of passing between Bate, Turner, Broady, Burton, 0 Brien, and Barber ended in the latter being pushed into touch just on the line. Tie Warrington men how- ever were not to be denied, and from the line out, which enSued, Foden received the ball, and diving through tne :legs of his opponents scored at the corner, this being his second try this season. The place was a very difEcult one, and Burton failed to put Warrington on equal terms . with their opponents. Some dogged play ensued from tbe kick out, for no fewer than six successive scrim- mages ensued in the visitors lines. Just as this was get- ting monotonous Bate giit away and was only just col- lared by Johnson in time. I free kick was now conceded to Warrington, and Dakin almost got over by quick fol- lowing up. Hull now had a turn of pressing,ana BOtICOW was repeatedly called upon, his and Broady's kicks into I touch being particularly well timed. An anxious mom- ent was experienced when Burton had his kick charged, but Bate, to whom the ball rebounded, passed back to Boscow, who found touch at half way, and thus staved off danger. From now to half-time, each side pressed in Sum, and Burton and Barber experienced some very ill-luck. Half-tivne arrived with the score —Hull, one goal ; Warrington, one try. The first half had been fairly even, and the game in comparison with the second half was fairly fought. In the second half, however, the Hull men commenced to go for men as well as the ball, and a most unsatisfactory game was witnessed. The Warrington team also fell away, and the result was that while Hull further augumented their score, Warrington failed to do so. However. Hamer kicked off,and Hull being conceded a penalty kick, Barker almost landed a goal, the ball just tailing under the goal posts. Warrington tailed to get it away, and a scrimmage was ordered. A sharp pass trom Barker to rhompson enabled the latter to drop a goal from close in, and Hull were thus six points ahead. Passing between Broady, O'Brien, Burton and Barber, ended in the latter being tackled and hurt so that he had to retire for a few minutes. Monotonous scrimmages now ensued, and this continued for almost 20 minutes, neither side being really dangerous, although Hull did . the majority of the pressing. Hepes arose on the War- rington side at length when Broady suddenly darted from the thick of his opponents,but just as be got past the full back, be was collared by C. Townsend,aad no one being by to receive the pass, a splendid opportunity was lost. The game now resumed the old order of scrimmages, and free kicks from marks. From one of these free kicks a very regrettable incident occurred, and which called forth loud exclamations of indignation. Barber was watching the ball high in the air, when although he was nowhere near the ball, be was suddenly sent full length with great force upon the ground by Jacketts,aud the game was delayed for a few minutes in consequence. Barber kept on the field, but it gwas evident tnat he had been severely hurt. Burton took the free kick which was granted from this accident, but Hull still kept up the pressure. The Warrington forwards did not exhibit the same dash as Hull, though Turner,Taylor and Dakin worked extremely bard. No more scoring ensued, and Hull were declared victors of the game by two goals (9 points) to one try (3 pints). RUNCORN v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Runcorn suffered defeat at the hands of St. Helens on Saturday in weir secoad mach in the new union com- petition. W.Faulkner and Jolley were absent from the Runcorn team. The sides were—St. Helens : Foulkes, back ; Dougherty, Appleton, Allen, and Wniteley, three- quarters : Cross and Ate Lees, half backs; Gladwin, Winstanley, Rimmer, Ashcroft, McKay, Briers, and Rey- nolds,forwards. Runcorn: Butterworth,back ; Warder, Myers, Itohne.oe, and Dunbavand, three-quarters ; Faulkner and Penulebury,hall backs; Langley, Farmer, Gayter, Moores, Pendlebury, Lightfoot, Johnson, and Walker, forwards. Farmer started at 3 30 and Walker,by smart following up, bowled Ashcroft over,ball in hands. Cross next got the ball from his partner, and ran right across to no ad- vantage, screwing the ball down to Butterworth, who with a strong kick sent the ball well into touch. Soon ' the Runcorn tbreequartes began to work. Robinson and Myers exchanged cleverly, the latter finishing by a long kick to the line, and thus gaining ground. Soon Appleton tried Butterworth, but be safely cleared his charge. Play was getting rather warm. There appeared to be no quarter asked or given, and strong rushes by the lorwarde gave the half backs on either side plenty of defending. One preminent rush was made by Walker, who aso made aeme excellent tackles tat McLees, and Cross, thus very safely preventing any passing amongst the home backs. The spectators were hereabouts treated to some exciting manceuvres. A series ef long kicking by Farmer, Butter- worth, and Foulkes was followed by smart passing on the part of Robinson and Myers. A rush back by Ash- croft and Reynolds gave Butterworth another chance tri,, show his ability. Matters for Runcorn looked bad until ' W. Pendlebury came through the thick and got the ball to centre, where Myers took up the running, and dashed off finely to Foulkes, and rightly passing to Dunbavin on the wing, the latter tailed to take the chance, much to the disgust of the Runcoun players. Foulkes getting up, all chance of scoring was lost. Still Runcorn kept up a very fierce attack, Farmer going straight for the line, and tossing to Walker, a try was scored. An appeal was made by St. Helens that the ball had been passed for- ward, and they got the referee's verdict. A scrimmage was fermed about 10 yards from the home line, and for some minutes Cross, Appleton, and Doughterty, had any amount of saving to do to keep the Cheshire team from wring. As the result of some long kicking by Apple. ton the ball got transferred to neutral ground, and PrlcLees gave Dougherty a chance which he took, and going right for the Runcorn line shook off all oppositi...n, and got in near the posts, Allen kicking an easy goal. This reverse roused up the Runcornians, who quickly kicked off from the centreline, play being again located ,in the St. Helens quarters. When half-time arrived the score was St. Helens one goal • Runcorn nil. Cross restarted the game after the interval, Warder responding, and for a little time play was in the Runcorn half. By a strong forward effort mid-field was reached, when Cross made a rough tackle of Walker, who was getting wal under way. The advantage gained was kept up by J. Faulkner, who dribbled grandly, and easily bowled over Allen in his attempt to neutralize,and for some minutes St. Helens had a warm time. Runcorn front rank forged their way into the home quarters, and for the remainder of the game St. Helens could not drive I them hack. Butterworth made a splendid shot at goal, I , the ball going a few inches wide—a very near thing. The attack was still kept up, first by Robinson and Myers, and next byea very determined rush by Faulkner, which some half-dozen St. Helens men stopped just a few yards from the line. The game closed in a very lucky win for St. Helens by ems goal to nothing. Winsis v. LEEDS. —This match on the Lowerhouee- lane enclosure, on Saturuay, attracted upwards of 3,000 spectators. The hostilities were cemmenced at a late hour, Widnes having agreed for the kick-off to take place at 4.30. Seven minutes after the advertised time the teams laced each other as follows: Widnes. Brennan, full back ; Archer, Rispan, Nevitt and Clark, three- quarters ; Drummond and Barber, half backs ; Whelan, Foster, Grace, Emery, Handley, Fraser; Peters, and J,,linson, forwards. Leeds,Walton, full back ; Clarkson, Close,G. W. Flainstock, and H. Hainstock, three-trier- tees ; Bestow and Midgley, half backs ; Pickles, Riley, Gregg, Armstrong, Fletcher, Parfit, Wray aud Har- greaves, forwards. Mr. Dillon, of Warrington, acted as referee. Widnes won the toss, and elected to play with the sun behind them. Pickles started the ball for Leeds. In the first few minutes' play, neither side could claim an advantage, but Rispan by a grand kick transferred operation to the Leeds 25, and Widnes showing great smartness on the ball field the upper hand. Barber had free kick.which was charged down by Hargreaves. From a scrimmage, Drummond obtained possession, passed to Barber, who gave the ball to Archer, who ran to the line but was pulled up by Walton. Widnes kept up an aggressive attitude, and severely tested the de_ fence of the visitors. Widnes, however, were not to he denied, and keeping up a continual bombardment wer,, rewarded after a quarter of an boar's play by Archeu</p>
        <p>scoring from a grand dribble by Barber. Leeds strove Mr. Payne, in returning thanks for his election, re- hard to neutralise the efforts of Widnes,but the home ; ferred to the split in the English Rugby Union caused team kept the ball in the Leeds territory, playing ;by the secession of the clubs forming theNortnern Union. a magnificent game. Drummond by a long pass gave The battle that had bet n waged was a battle of politics, the ball to Clarke, who rushed over and added a second en] be felt convinced that the colleagues who had left try, Barber again failing at gal. Up to this stage them had been sincere in their objects,and thought they Widnes had played a winning game losing no °poor- were doing their best for Rugby football generally. He , tanities, their quickness on the ball, their smart passing hoped they would succeed or fail according as their policy and excellent combination altogether disorganizing.their was right or wrong. opponents. An incursion was made in the direction of The Chairman, who is also President of the English the Widnes goal, Barstow getting over. Walton made a Rugby Union, spoke on the same subject. Be said that poor attempt at goal. This reverse sharpened Widnes if he could have thought that payment for e' broken up, and Clarke rushed half the length of the field, but time" could be properly instituted and carried out, he was pulled up by Walton. Play of a tolerably even would have been most decidedly in favour of its adop- character ensued, and Hainstock obtaining possession tion. made a good run, but found Brennan prepared for him. As the game advanced Leeds showed improved form, keeping play to a great extent in Widnes quarters. The home team, however,defended with great determination and although Close had a free kick no substantial advantage accrued. F.st and vigorous play followed, both teams putting in excellent work, the result being that the spectators were treated to a capital exhibition of football. Half-time—Widnes, two tries; Leeds, one</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>try. The second half was continued with the same spirit, but for sometime neither side could gain an advantage. I Ultimately Leeds, by good passing, enabled Hainstock to cross the Widnes lines,and thus score a most meritorious try, the major points being added by Walton. The home team then put on increased pressure, which was soon rewarded by another try being obtained, which was converted into a goal. Spurred on by this success they continued to press, but the defence of the Headingly brigade,was so strong that further invasion was render. d impossible. They continued to hold the upper liana up to the close of hostilities,and during the last few minutes they were constantly in the visitor's 25. The game, which throughout had been fast and exciting, and which was generally acknowledged to he the finest one yet seen on the Lower-house enclosure, ended in a victory for the Lancastrians, the result being. Widnes one goal, two tries; Leeds one goal, one try.</p>
        <p>North Division: J. Caleart (Lancaster) and J. M. Mawson (Barrow). North-east : E. Healey (Littleborough), W. Lees (Mos- ley), and J. S. Turnbull (Blackley). South : S. E. Wilson (Liverpool),J. Higson (Salford), E. Lines (Manchester), and T. Hunter (Blackley and Free Wanderers). North-west : .1 Mills (Swinton), J, Robertson (Brough- ton), and J. C. Poyser (Broughton Park). The following were appointed the Referees' Commit- tee :—Messrs. F. A. Grover, A. M. Crook, J. FL Payne, T Hunter, J. Higson, H. Williamson, and S. Spencer. CHESHIRE RUGBY UNION: ANNUAL MEETING. The annual meeting of the Cheshire Rugby Union was held at the Crown Hotel,in Manchester on Monday even- ing. Mr. W. H. Thorp, president, occupied the chair, and amongst those present were Messrs. H. J. Ryalls and N. A G. Dowie (Birkenhead Park), F. Drinkwater and C. C. Harvay (Sale), J.Love (Birkenhead Wanderers), H. 0. Davies (New Brighton), and W. Bailey and G. Gatley (Altrincham). As usual the meeting was private,but at , the termination of the deliberations it was stated that the general feeling was that the split of the clubs forming the Northern Rugby Union from the parent body was most unfortunate. The resignation of the Runcorn club was formally accepted, but as the Stockport organisa- tion had not thought fit to communicate their severance from the County Union no action could be taken with regard to them. The election of officers for the ensuing season resulted as follow :—Mr. J. W. H. Thorp presid- ent, Messrs. H. J. Ryalls and H. B. Browning, vice pre- sidents, Mr. W. H. Wallace hon, treasurer, and Mr. C. C. Harvey hon. secretary. The balance-sheet, the adoption of which was proposed by Mr. Gatley, and seconded by Drinkwater, showed cash owing to the treasurer £45 118. 3d., the principal item of receipt being -£75 16, Id., the amount of profit shown on the match against Lancashire. The total travelling expenses amounted to £B5 4s. 2d., the other items being of an incidental character.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON LEAGUE. RUNCORN V. WIDNES Penna.—These two rival organ- isations met at Runcorn on Saturday to play off their first league fixture, before a goodly number of specta- tors. The respective teams faced each other as follows: —Runcorn : Goal, Hough ; full backs. Ledsham and Carter; half backs, Py e,Paton, and Holiday; forwards, Baylie, Jones, Walker, Burrows,and Peacock. Parish : Goal, O'Bierne ; full backs, White and Ramsay ; half backs, Duffy, Clibborn, and Maddison ; forwards, Jones, Risley, Stewart, Mellonship, and Davies. Peacock WOO the toss. Stewart gave the initial kick, and Runcorn checking took play to the Widnes quarters, and experi- enced hard luck in not scoring. From the goal kick Widnes got possession and forced play to the Runcorn goal, and Millonship shot, but nothing resulted,it going wide. From the goal kick some midfield play ensued, when the whistle sounded half time with no score. Walker re-started, and Runcorn still kept up the pres- sure,and from a scrimmage in goal Jones got possession and scored for the homesters. Stewart re-started, and Widnes then took play to the other end, and from a bully in goal points were registered for the visitors. Walker re-started, and Runcorn still pressing had again hard luck in not increasing their score, the ball going wide. From the goal kick Widnes got possession and took play to the Runcorn territory, and a further goal was obtained by the visitors. Walker re-started, and Widnes checked and forced play in front of the Runcorn goal,and the homesters' custodian in making a save got hurt, and play was stopped for some time, he being obliged to leave the field. On resuming, Runcorn only having ten men, Carter having gone goal, Ledsham be- ing the only full back, even play ensued, and when the whistle sounded the final score stood—Widnes, two: Runcorn, one. GRAPPENHALL V.WARRINGTON RESERVE.—Grappenhall played their opening game at home on Saturday there being 200 present. The visitors began well after several exchanges Reid shooting a grand goal in the first fifteen minutes. The game at this point livened up and after some good passing on both sides, Wilson for the visitors just managed to beat Dean. Half time being—Reserve 2, Grappenhall 0. On the restart the homesters had the benefit of the breeze and after several exchanges Free- man at half got the ball nicely away to James Burrows who passed his opponents and shot a splendid goal. The homesters were now playing a grand game, and W. Carter getting possession from J. Carter soon put the second goal into the net. Bate sending across to Grounds that player scored the third goal, Still not to be denied the visitors played up E. Wilson, Sutton and Latham making a good bid for goal but a long shot from J.Carter which rebounded from the posts, Duckett for the borne- eters put the finishing touch on for the fourth goal. The result being Grappenhall 4 goals, Warrington Reserve 2 goals. STOCKTON HEATH V. FRODSHAM.—This match, the opening one of this season, was played at Fairfield-road, on Saturday. Hallows started for Stockton Heath. The game was keenly contested for the first quarter of an hour, neither side having any distinct advantage. Frod- sham paid a brief visit to the home goal, but the defence was sound, and the home forwards getting possession, Johnson put the finishing touch to a neat passing move- ment by shooting a goal, Kimberley shortly afterwards put his side still further ahead with a low shot, with which Fletcher had little chance. Half time arrived with the score in favour of Stockton Heath by two goals to nil. After the restart the Heathens,. following up their late successes still further increased the score, Webbe (two), and Hallows, each invading the Frodsham citadel. This cow luded the scoring, the pleasantly con- tested game ending in a victory for the home team by five goals to nil. Mr. J. Mather (Warrington District Association) officiated as referee.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. A meeting of the emergency Committee of the North- ern Rugby Union was held at the Spread Eagle Hotel, in Manchester on Tuesday evening. Mr. H. H. Waller (Brighouse) presided, and a full attendance of represen- tatives included Messrs. H. Sewell (Leeds), J. Clifford (Huddersfield), J. Nichol (Halifax), A. Fattorini (Man- ningham),J. E.Warren (Warrington),E. Gresty (Brough- ton Rangers), J. Quirk (Leigh), ,J. H. Smith (Widnes), and J. Platt (Oldham), hon. secretary. As on former occasions,the deliberations were private. The press re-' nresentatives were informed that the meeting was occu- pied a considerable time in the consideration of the by- laws, which were completed, and the formation of the rules were also entered up m. These will probably be completed at a meeting to be held at Huddersfield next Tuesday, at which gathering a scheme of insurance of players will probably be submitted. The referees to officiate in the various matches on the 28th were agreed upon, and the names will he published next Tuesday. It was decided that the fees tor referees and touch judges should be the same in the three counties, viz. 10s. (id. with second-class railway fares for referees and ss. and second-class fares for touch judges. County matches between the various clubs were arranged as follows : October 21 : Lancashire v. Cheshire, at Stockport. November 25 : Yorkshire v. Cheshire, at Leeds.</p>
        <p>December 7 : Lancashire v. Yorkshire, at Oldham.</p>
        <p>The official times for the starting of matches were fixed as follows :—During September and October, 3 30 p m. ; November,three o'clock ; December and January, 2 45 ; February, three o'clock; and March and April, 3 30. Providing all the rules are completed on Tuesday next it is the intention to call a general meeting at an early date, at which the rules and by-laws, together with the scheme relative to the insurance of players, will be submitted for approval. There was some dis- cussion as to the proposed adoption of certain changes in the game, principally in the reduction of the number of forwards to six. Nothing official was done in this direction, but an exhibition match is to be played at Manningham on October 1 between that club and Hali- fax, and it will be left to those two organisations to de- cide what innovations should be introduced into the game. Of course it does not follow that whatever is done at Manningham will be taken as the basis of sug- gested reforms. In all probability,however, the line-out I will be done away with and thirteen played on each Bide. LANCASHIRE COUNTY FOOTBALL CLUB. THE PRESIDENT OF TFIE RUGBY UNION AND THE SPLIT. The annual meeting of the Lancashire County Football Club was held at the Grand Mite], in Manchester, on Tuesday evening. Mr. Roger Walker, president, was in the chair,and a large attendance included Messrs. F. A. Grover, J. A. MacLaren, A. M. Crook, J. H. Payne, J. HigBol3, J. Robertson, S. E. Wilson, J. M. Mawson, J. Mills, J. E. Foyser, and E. Healey. In submitting the annual report Mr. A. M. Crook re. ferring to the formation of the Northern Rugby Union, said that what they had to do now WRS to pull them- selves together, and work for the honour of the county. (Hear, hear.) It was a good opportunity for some of the young clubs to come to the front, and he hoped the chance would not be neglected. (Applause.) Mr. Payne moved the adoption of the balance sheet. He said the funds had been administered in a splendid manner by the committee, and the season was com- menced with no less a sum than £BOO in hand. The accounts for the past season showed a total income of £1,775 16s. Bd., and an expenditure of £1,155 18e. 6d., leaving a balance of £619 18s. 2d., an increase of close uptin £BOO as compared with the previous year. The most noteworthy items in the receipts were £1,506 £l6s. 31. in " gate " money. £lO 17e.entrance fees to the championship competition, and subscriptions £29 183. The chief reason for such a large amount being taken in gate "money lay in the fact that one of the matches was played at Oldham, namely. Lancashire v. Westmor- land. On the expenses side £37 16s. 9d. was spent in travelling, £59 Is. id. in hotel expenses. £5B 3s. Bd. in subscriptions to the Rugby Union, £29 18s. committee men's expenses attending committee meetings and matches, medals, &amp;c., £6O Sc,, and £l2B 4s. for the use of grounds. Both the report and the balance sheet were unanim- ously adopted. The election of officers was then proceeded with. Mr. A. N. Hornby was unanimously re-elected president, be- ing described as one of the best sportsmen Lancashire had ever produced. Messrs. Grover and Walker were re-elected vice presidents, Mr. J. H. Payne hon. ;tree., and Mr. A. M. Crook hon. sec.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>FULL OF ALL DEOtta.</head>
        <p>FULL OF ALL DEOtta.</p>
        <p>NAPOLEON I. died of cancer of the stomach developed from acute inflammatory dyspepsia—of which he had, In his latter years, frequent attacks. Yet he was not a gourmand nor a drunkard. From this illustration— which is but one out of thousands—the casual reader may judge of what great oaks of suffering and death may grow from the little acorns of indigestion, which most people think are merely matters of a day or so. A prominent physician says : " A near relative of my own has for the past fifteen years suffered from dyspepia, complicated with pseudo-phthisis (false con- sumption), and over and over again the best of doctors have predicted his early death. He has spent a small fortune at various resorts tor consumptives. but is at present perfectly well, and has not the smallest sign of present or past disease of the lungs. Cases such as these I am constantly alining across." Mrs. Ann Lancaster, of Bradford, a few years ago used to have attacks in which she would fall to the ground insensible. Her nerves were so disordered that she habitually trembled from head to foot. Sometimes her nerves were so uncontrollable that the bed shock under her. Her sleep was, of course, disturbed and broken, and she became so weak that she could walk only a few yards at a time. The malady first showed itself in the summer of 1867.1 She felt strangely tired and languid, and was not able to obtain rest, either by the use of medicines or change of , bodily positions. Her mouth was filled with a thick, disgusting slime which clung to her teeth so strongly that she was compelled to wipe it off with a cloth or a handkerchief. Her mouth tasted so badly that she fancied that such a taste might accompany certain poisons. She could eat but very little on account not merely of want of appetite, but because of a resulting pain in the chest and sides. In October of that same year she went to the Bradford Infirmary, but received no benefit from the treatment at that institution, the physicians not seeming to know what her ailment really was, and finally she returned home discouraged and very feeble. Her mind suffered from that form of melancholia which the ancients cot,-</p>
        <p>sidered to be due to the circulation of bile through the body. These facts thus hastily and crudely stated, will indicate in some measure her forlorn situation when— But let her tell the rest herself. She says : "One day a book was left at my house describing the nature and success of a preparation calkd Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. I procured a supply of it from Mr. Dobson. Bolton-road,and after a faithful use of it for a compara- tively short time I felt better, and sot long subsequently I was completely restored to health by it. Yours truly, (Sigaed) (Mrs.) Ann Lancaster, 16, Back Spinkwell Terrace, Bolton-road, Bradford, December 15th, 1892." The leading, and indeed the only point we desire to make on these cases is this : Indigestion and dyspeps la —the real complaint in all of them—is the most aggres- sive dangerons, and diceptibe of all the ills we are heist'</p>
        <p>to. It killed Napoleon the Great by producing cancer, an out-growth of blood poisoning ; it deluded the doctors, who attended the relative of a doctor, and in Mrs. Lancaster's case it imitated epilepsy. _ .</p>
        <p>Moral : Watch its first syinptons keenly, and keep Mother Seigel's Curative S)rup within easy reach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. BROORLANDS.—The concluding match of the Warrington season ended disastrously for the local team, Brooklauds winning the game by six wickets. The Warringtonians lacked the services of Mr. J.J. Crosfiwld, but Brook:ands had made a special effort for this their initial match at Warrington, and the Cheshire combina- tion included several players of note, who, although affiliated with the Brooklarida Club, place their services more regularly at the disposal of the Manchester Cricket Club. 'lns Warriugtonians won the toss and batted first. Score and b3svllng analysis : WARKINGTOa. BROOILLANDS. B Smith e Pagden b H T P Bei:nouse b Connor 66 Tindail B hi Uriffen c ft Lomas b 11 Lomas b Connor 39 1 Ctay 3 BM. Tindal aotout lB B Connor c A LOMAS b A Hartley C Clark b Clay 24 Coo uor 0 D elaraer b -t. 'Tindall Bates o Bellhoue b II n</p>
        <p>Tindall W X Pvdmore c Hdrnoud- sou b H Ticd3.ll B le Oakley o 11 Lomas b Clay 0 G Clarke 0 and b Hartley 47 Clark it A Lomas uHartiey 16 B hingland c and b Varian 18 611 cock not out 0 Extras 6 ixtras ..................14</p>
