      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUGBY. THE NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. HULL.</p>
        <p>The team which Warrington met on Saturday was from the East Yoikshire port of Hull. This team made a very poor show in last season's competition in the Yorkshire Htnior League. Tdoush performing very poorly against teams of their own county, the Hull team almost invariably "go for a Lancashire team,and this was the case on Saturlac ,for they " went " for the Warrington man in a manner which was the reverse of pleasa-•t. The game was not a pltasant one for there were Lir too many stoppages owing to the vigftrous play of the Hull team. It is ten years since Warrington played Hull previously, so that the teams were not very well acquainted with each oth,r, there being only one man in each tear* who took part in it. Mr. J. Boydell of Leigh officiated as whistle blower on Saturday, and the following players represented each team. Warrin g- ton : J. Boscow, full back ; Barber O'Brien, Burton, and Broady, threequarters ; Bate and Foden,half backs ; Turner, Taylor, Dakin, Berry, Sankey, Saunders, Swift,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>CUTTING AND AND WOUNDING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CUTTING AND AND WOUNDING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PRISONER COMMITTED TO THE ASSIZES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning before the Mayor and Mr. S' Chant!ley, at the Warrington Borough Police Court, George Flanaghan,a young man living in Antrobus-street was charged with cutting and wounding Walter Hum- phries, in Autrobus-street, on Monday, the 16th lust, with intent to do him greviously bodily harm. Walter Humphries, the complainant stated that on Monday night the 16th lost, he met the prisoner in the Market Vaults about 7 o'clock and they had a drink to- getter and then went home to Mr. Joseph Pilling's house Ant robus•street. There, complainant went out to shut the hack door and when he returned the prisoner was fryil.g some bacon. Complainant sat down and prisoner suddi My struck him across the fingers with a carving knife and afterwards attempted to drag him into the bed- room. He threw him ution some harness and threatened to take his life. Complainant got away and ran out side while prisoner threw the knife to Pilling. Corn. plainant then ran out and returned to see why Planagban nad injured him. Prisoner then knocked him down and kicked him while on the floor until some women dragged him away. Prisoner had a spite against him because he had got a job with Mr. Pilling which prisoner formerly had. Henry Langdale junior house surgeon at the Infirm- ary, said the complainant was brought to him on Mon- day about 9 o'clock and be found him suffering from a clean cot wound on the inside of his fingers the tendons on the 3rd and 4th fingers being divided. In all proba- bility the fingers would be stiff when healed. The carv- ing knife produced was an instrument likely to cause . the wound. It had apparently been drawn across the Angers. Samuel Warburton, 7, Antrobus-st.. said that on the night in question he heard a disturbance in Pilling's house across the street. This continued for about half an hour when Humphries came running out with his Angers hanging and bleeding badly. When Flanaghan came out he knocked complainant down, and the latter , crying out that he was being kicked, some women dragged prisoner away. Witness afterwards took com- plainant to the Infirmary. Martha Warburton. 7, Antrobus-st., gave similar evi- dence to that of the last witness, and added that it was she who put prisoner in Pilling's yard. Detective-Sergeant Monaghan said that from what Humphries told him he accompanied the complainant to Pilling's house where he noticed a quantity of blood on the kitchen floor. He found prisoner in bed and witnna told him to get up as he wanted him for wounding Humphries with a knife. He went with them into the kitchen and picked up a broken tea cup, alleging that Hunphreys had cut himself with it. Pilling was present, drunk, and witness asked him where his carving knife was and Pilling replied that they had none. It was, however, afterwards found under some sacking on the sofa. On the way to the Police Station the prisoner eaid to the complainant, "If you're going to lock me up for this I'll cut your other hand when I come out." When he was formally charged at the Station the prisoner re- plied, " It is not true ; he did it himself with the cup." The prisoner reserved his defence,and the magistrates committed him for trial to the Liverpool Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LATE MRS. SCRWABE.—The remains of Mrs. Eleanor bchwatse, whose death took place at her residence, Dingle Brow House, Lymm,on Thursday, wefe interred in the graveyard of the Parish Church, Lymm on Saturday. She was 52 years of age, ond much respected in the village. Numerous wreaths were forwarded to the the undertaker, Mr. A. B. Ferris, of Lyrom,and plaeed on the coffin. The Rev. G. W. Guest officiated. . . . . .</p>
        <p>• . . THE REVISION COURT.—The revision of the voters' lists for the Lymm district of the Altrincham Parlia- mentary Division was held at the Lymm Police Court, on Saturday moaning, when the Revising Barrister, Mr. William Trevor Parkins sat from 10.30 until two o'clock in the afternoon. Mr. F. R. B. Lindsell represented the Conservatives, and Mr. G. L. Welford, the Liberals. The proceedings were principally of a routine character.but , a case from High Leigh was brought under the notice ' of the Revising Barrister. The Liberals objected to the vote of a man named Samuel Bendall, Colonel Legh's valet, on the ground that he went way with Colonel Legh, and so he broke his 'tenancy. Bendel!, however, ' appeared and proved his claim, and was granted 2s. fid. for expenses. ALLEGED THREATENING BY FARM LABOURERS.—Patrick McElugh, farm labourer, in the employ of Mr. Alexander Mclntyre, of Lymm, was charged at the Police Court on Saturday before Dr. Brabazon (in the chair), and Mr. J. Gregson, with threatening to assault Alexander Mc Intyre, jun., on the sth inst.—The evidence of the com- plainant showed that without provocation the prisoner threatened to " knife him " and otherwise illtreat him. In consequence of the threats of the prisoner and other labourers he was very much afraid, and was kept on the top of a cern stock for fully an hour and a half. The prisoner and the others were waiting at the foot of the stack with nikels.—Mr. Alexander Mclntyre, father of the complaihant gave corroborative evidence.—Sergeant Garner asked the magistrates to remand the prisoner to the Petty Sessions, which would take place on Friday. The interval would allow further investigations to be made.—Tbe application was granted.—Prisoner applied for bail and it wasgranted.</p>
        <p>A shocking affair is reported from Paisley, where a large number of rats entered a romm in which three children named Weaver were sleeping, and attacked them. The mother, attracted by the children's screams, went to the rescue, but was in turn attacked, the rats being eventually driven off with the assistance of neigh- bours.</p>
        <p>The suspension is announced of Messrs. Fox, Clinch and Co., of Gloucester, a well-known firm in the grain trade. The liabilities are said to amount to nearly £200,000.</p>
        <p>1116L_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
