      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>“THE RANSoM oF ROME.”</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>TEMPORALITLES FOR THE POPE.</head>
        <p>TEMPORALITLES FOR THE POPE.</p>
        <p>The "Daily Telegraph" publishes a proposal that " tract of Italian territory and a free Roman seaport should be made over as inalienable temporalities to the Holy Bee, under the guarantee of Italy and the other Powers, in consideration of £200,000 000, which would redeem the Italian Exchequer from impending bank- ruptcy and ransom Papal Rome and her new posses- sions." This proposal, says the "Telegraph," reaches it from abroad " through a source amply guaranteeing its serious inspiration and practical purpose." The writer says Twenty-five years have passed since the enthronement of the House of Savoy in the Palace of the Quirinal after the bombardment of Rime and the breach of Porta Pia. The Italian Government has determined to make the twenty-fifth anniversary as brilliant a festival as possible, and has induced the Legislature to raise this festival to the rank of a 'civil holiday.' But it would seem, In spite of this, that the unification of Italy is still tar from its perfect accom- plishment. Its people are divided into two camps, thoroughly hostile to each other. In every remote village and commune, and in most cf the municipalities, the project of this national holiday to celebrate the over- throw of the Papacy in encountering opposition. The Liberal press is bearing ample witness to the general revival of Catholic life and activity, and there can be no doubt that the Catholic party in Italy, so far from being weaker, less numerous, and less active than it was, 25 years ago, is gathering strength and courage year by year. That is attributable not only to the training and practice which have consolidated the Catholic party and given it an opportunity to feel its strength, but to cir- cumstances altogether disconnected with the Pope and followers. The ruinous expenditure insisted upon in order that Italy might form part of the Triple Alliance, the consequent crushing of the population under an ex- traordinary burden of taxation, the impoverishment of the landowner and the upper classes, the confiscation of the smaller holdings on aacount of inability to meet the taxes and financial imposts, the disappointment of the middle and commercial classes, which had looked upon the unification of Italy as a guarantee for a more successful and extended commerce with enhanced pros fits, the misery of the working classes, which have been seeking to fly the country, or have b-en 'quietly sinking into starvation, disease and d, ath—all these are circumstances which have been fighting for the Pope and the Catholic party. The impasse becomes more and more difficult and hopeless. The kingdom cannot afford to declare itself bankrupt and to compound with its creditors ; and, on the other hand, the taxation can scarcely be increased to meet the growing deficencies. The problem is to find a solution and a remedy. There are not wanting well-wishers of the kingdom of Italy and of the Papacy who profess to see in the present deadlock the elements of a final solution of this, compli- cated question so thorough and complete as, if rightly used, to promise a new peint of happy departure in the history of the peninsula. The kingdom is stifled under the double incubus of her financial difficulties and the ever-threatening Roman question. Rome demands cer- tain temporal guarantees as essential to the life and ac- tion of her unique religious institution, whose influence and authority are recognised from Berlin to:Washington. from Lisbon to Japan, and to Egypt. What if a scheme could be devised,by means of which each should supply to the other what is lacking ? Such a hypos thesis may at first sight appear extravagant and impos- sible. As a matter of fact it is under serious considera- tion. Nothing less is proposed than that the Catholic countries and peoples of the world should combine to ransom Rome. To those familiar with the organisation and working of the Roman Catholic communion, and with the intensity of the desire which animates the Catholic Church to secure the perfect independence of . its spiritual head, it would not be difficult to believe I that, the scheme once sec on foot, a large sum of money may be collected and placed in the hands of Leo XIII. The Pontiff, with .£200,000,000 at his disposal, would ' have it in his power to deal with the distracted and bankrupt Government of the Italian King more effec- tively than at present. It would rest with the Pope to meet the Government with proposals that might save the Italian Kingdom, reinstate it exchequer, restore peace, unity, and prosperity to Italy. And if it shoq,ld I turn out that, by some singular obliquity of mental vision the Italian Government should fail to come to fair ' and honourable ter ms with the Pontiff, the latter might feel justified in wit hdra.wing from Rome and establish- . ing himself for a period elsewhere, in a position of vant- age other than the Vatican ; while there would be with- drawn from Rome at the same time that immense sum which is annually brought by the Catholics of the world to the Eternal City and is there expended." The writer declares, in conclusion, his belief that Leo XIII, and Signor Crispi are statesmen enough to bring about la solution of the question.</p>
        <p>The Dublin Corporation dicided on Monday to attend officially a demonstration to he held on October 6th in commemoration of the patriotic services rendered to Ireland by Mr. Parnell.</p>
