      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <p>• RUNCORN FREE LIBRA.RY.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Runcorn Free Library Committee WM held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall,Etus- Cote on Tuesday evening. Mr. William Handley occu- pied the chair, and there were also present—the Rev. John Simpson, Messrs. H. J. Once, R. H. Posnett, S. fope, W. J. Cunningham, W. Hapkinson, and Mr. J. D. Jones (librarian). THE CHAIRMAN'S BEREAVEMENT. Mr. Pope said he wished to express the sympathy of the committee with their chairman in his bereavement, under, he might say, almost tragic circumstances.—Mr. Once said he quite appreciated the reference that Mr. Pope had made to the bereavement their chairman had had in the loss of his brother. Mr. Thomas Handley was a gentleman they had known throughout their lives,and they all sympathised with the relatives in their bereave- soent.—The Chairman said he could scarcely trust him- self to speak of the subject at present. He was sure that on behalf of himself and their family, he could say that they appreciated the kindly expressions of sym- pathy with them.</p>
        <p>THE MONTHLY REPORT. The Librarian in his report for the month of August stated that the number of books issued was 1,572, vi hich was a decrease of 136 in the lending department, and„26 in the reference department. The books were taken out as follows: Theology 22, philosophy 4, science 52, history Jac., 92, poetry 9, fiction 1,227, miscellaneous 129,French literature 0, music 12. Reterence department 25. THE LIBRARIAN'S ATTENDANCE AT CARDIFF. A letter was read from Mr. Marshall, secretary to the Urban Council, in which he stated that the recommenda- tion of the committee, that the Librarian attend the annual meeting of the Library Association at Cardiff, and that his expenses not to exceed £5 be paid, had not been accepted by the Council. A discussion ensued upon the matter in which the opinion prevailed that it was owing to a misapprehension of the whole circumstances, that the recommendation was not accepted. It was decided on the motion of Mr. Hankinson, seconded by Mr. Pope, and supp irted by Mr. Grice, that Mr. Philip Speakman be thanked for generously paying the expenses Of the Librarian. THE LIBRARIAN'S REPORT OF THE CONFERENCE. Mr. Jones submitted a report to the members, of his visit to the annual meeting of the Library Association of the United Kingdom, held at Cardiff, quite recently. He referred to the opening address of the Chairman, Lord Windsor, and also gave interesting summaries of the various papers read at the Conference. In conclusion he said that the opportunity afforded him of coming in- to contact with the managers of other libraries, and giv- ing him the benefit of hearing discussi ins enlivened and enriched by the varied experiences of library work under different conditions, must have a stimulating effect,and make his work more pleasant to himself and beneficial to the public whose interests he sought to promote.--. Several members expressed their satisfaction at the report. 7 The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NEW BOOKSIM A.GAZINES,ETC.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKSIM A.GAZINES,ETC.</p>
        <p>MACMILLAN AND CO.—The latest of the Illus- trated Standard Novels is another of Marryat's— and one of the most animated and amusing too— namely, " Jacob Faithful," which has been before the public in one form or another for the last 60 years. Numerous and varied as have been the editions of it, we may safely say that the one to band, representing the above series, is superior in all points to its predecessors. Like the remainder of I. S. N's it is well printed and bound, and also has its value considerably enhanced by illus- trations from the pencil of Mr. H. M. Brock, a favourite in his line. Mr. James flannay contri- butes an interesting introduction which comprises a skilful criticism of the various odd characters in</p>
        <p>• the book. In the Kingsley seri,s, " Westward Ho !" is the last edition and comprises two volumes of equal size, the first containing the adventures occurring in England and the second commencing with the voyage of the good ship" Rose." The majority of people are aware of the intense interest and excite- ment attached to a perusal of this book, but to those who are not we can only point out that there' is now afforded an excellent opportunity for be- coming so, and at the same time the possessor of! two of the neatest of its many editions. ARCHIBALD CONSTABLE AND Co.—" My Japan- , ese Wife," a Japanese layll in a Japanese cover, is a short dreamlike story, by Clive Holland. There is no plot in it, no incident, in fact scarcely anything -beyond the fact that an Englishmen in Japan mar- riea a small, doll-like native of that country, and yet the reader is quietly interested. 'I he book is almost exclusively descriptive of something or other, generally the "wife," whose husband is an everlasting but kindly critic of her peculiarities. Of course there is some conversation in the book. but it is throughout common-place, though amus- ing—of which latter character the Englishman's frequent soliloquies possess most. It is the oddity of the idyll that entertains the reader. Volume 6 of the Acme Library. from the same publishers, is entitled " The Red Spell," by Francis Gribble. This novel is founded on the Parisian struggle between the Commune and the Versaillais. Its hero is one Ernest Durand, who is held firmly by the spell of Communic patriotism. Actuated by its influence he fights and ultimately dies for the Commune, notwithstanding the earnest pleadings of his sweetheart, a noble, gentle girl. When she thought she had drawn him from the vortex of strife and overcome his scruples, the Spell re- turns, and without her knowledge Durand goes out and sacrifices himself to what he then knew was a hopeless cause. Ass literary work, the book ranks low, though no serious failing is apparent. CAseaLL AND Co.—Some new magazine is almost always being issued from the establishment of the above firm. The last we have received has been the first number of "Heroes of Britain in Peace and War," which the publishers have determined to place within the means of all by the issue of this work in 22 monthly numbers at Id. each. In them British pluck is chronicled in almost every form and place—in the mine,in the prison, on tho battle- field and on the sea : v,ilorous perseverence in ex- ploration and the combating of abuse by heroes of the gospel. Every female householder andeoek will welcome " Cassell's New Universal Cookery Book," which, to be completed in ten parts, will contain 12 coloured plates and other illustrations. Instructions and re- ceipts are given in quantity and value,suffieient to endow the student with culinary knowledge of both plain and fancy order, illimitable When (complete, no better reference book or teacher will 'be extant.—Price,6d, per part. Under the title of "The Queen's London," a sup- erb album of views of London and its environs is t.to hand. The work, by permission, has been dedi- cated to H.M. the queen, and is of somewhat less ' size that the majority of like productions and • v therefore handier and more suitable for its in- tended position as a table-book. All manner of scenes are depicted in the' 32 illustrations in each 6d. part, and a further recommendation lies in the k fact that photographs are reproduced of the in- teriors of important and interesting buildings, not accessible to the general public. Letterpress and paper are of the highes% order and the whole a creditably neat production. Ozonan NEWNES AND CO.—The publishers of the " Strand " are to be congratulated on the more than usually excellent number of that magazine for sop- ktember. In addition to the exciting series "Gleams from the Dark Continent,' "Adventures or Bri- gadier Gerard," and " Stories from the Diary of a Doctor," there are other short stories of equal at- Is-traction, together with an instructive article on .."War Ballooning" and another entitled ". ;14 Artists." showing an 4 describing work by Sir r Richard Temple, Bart., Colonel Saundertion, and Mr. Curzon. "The Sportfolio" is a most welcome production, giving as it does, portraits and biographies of the . shining lights of sport and pastime. Prominent Sc . cricketers, golfers, cyclista,runners, e are held up to the admiring public gaze which it cer- tain will rapidly become extensive, as to hal:oe hand all that is necessary to see and know eabout privilege never before the athletes of the world is a accorded the public. Prominent in the "Strand Musical Mazazine," for this month is an interview with NI. Paderewski, who relates several anecdotes about his care..r, and one of whicb,having something local in it,will bear repetition—. It would seem that when travelling in England the famous pianist received a number of letters from a circus proprietor exhorting him to fulfil his engagement to appear at Warrington in thf“ gentleman's circus, with e performing bear. He added that the Warrington public were greatly disappointed. One letter enclosed a printed form of contract in which it was set in writing that " Paderewski is engaged to appear with, a dancing bear for .£lO a week. No play, no pay:' Subsequ- ently it transpired that there was a circus per- former in existence styling himself Padere.wski,and who when asked why he had assumed that name 'Enid he had the right to assume any nagie he Pleased, and added to his interrogator, M. Paderewski's secretary, " It is'nt w irth making a fuss about, I shall be a good advertisement to M. Paderewski."</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.</head>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.</p>
        <p>At the Barlestown Police Station on Monday I morning, before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, John Kelly,tor being drunk and disorderly in Queen-et., Males- town, on Sunday morning, was fined ss. and costs. —James Henry Bell, for being drunk and disorderly in Cross-lane, on Saturday night, was fined 2s. 6d. and costs.—Sarah Hayes, for a like offence in Park- road, Newton, on Saturday, was fined ss. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Viaduct Institute Bowling Club played off their annual handicap for the Wellington prize, the gift of Mr. J. W. Thorpe. The afternoon being fine and the green in good condition, there was a good number ot entries, 36 competing for the prize. Owing to a late start being made, the final heat bad to be made in semi-dark- ness. The result of the handicap was as follows: —lst prize, Mr. J. R. Allan ; 2nd prize,brass kettle, Mr. W. Grimshaw ; 3rd prize, set of carvers, Mr. James Bedworth. A vote of thanks at the close was accorded Mr. Thorpe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>EABLEEITOWN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>EABLEEITOWN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>This society, which was formed last year heid its first practice meeting on Tuesday night in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown. There was a fair attendance,and several new members were enrolled. Owing to Mr. C. Pryor having left the town, Mies Fludworth was appointed pianist in his place. It is intended to produce at Christmas "The Messiah," and the society has under contemplation the pro- duction at some later date, of another important work, probably Gaul's "Holy City." Mr. E. Ley- land still officiates as conductor, and Mr. J. H. Profit is the general secretary. DEPARTURE OF SERGE A.NT-I NSTRUCTOR BIRD FROM NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Expressions of regret are heard on all bands amongst the local Volunteers at the doparture cf Sergeant-Instructor Bird, who for the past two or three years has held the post of Instructor to the "F" (Newton) Company of the 6th Kings L.R.V. Mr. Bird by his straightforwardness and impartia- lity, together with a kindly bearing towards the men, had earned their esteem and respect, and he leaves the neighbourhood, carrying with him the best wishes of all those with whom became in con- tact. He is joining the Ist. Wiltshire Regiment which left Southampton for India on Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held on Monday , evening in the Co-operative Hall. Bro. J. Hatton presided and there was an excellent attendance of members, there being also present Bros. Sergeant Roberts, of Warrington; T. Thompson, St. Helens; Bro. and Sister Singleton, and others. Four can- didates were initiated, and several others proposed. Bro. Newman gave a report of the Convention which was adopted. Miss E. Blair was appointed pianist for the present quarter, with Bros. G. Clarke, J. T. Holliday, and J. Anderson as a musi- cal committee. Thanks were tendered to Mr. E. Leyland and the members of the Wargrave Church choir for the excellent service rendered the previ- ous Saturday evening. A short address was given by Bro. Thompson, of St. Helens. It was arranged for an open meeting on Monday evening next. The programme of the evening was sing, recite or pay, and an excellent programme of songs and recita- tions was rendered by various members, but a num- her preferred to pay forfeit.</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING MATCH—NEWTON V. DOUGLAS. The return shooting match between the Newton and Isle of Man detachments of the 6th V.B. Kings Liverpool Regiment took place on Monday at the Hightleld Moss Range. The Manxmen arrived at Newton on Saturday. On Sunday morning they visited and descended the Wood Pit ot Messrs. R. Evans and Co.,Haydock, and in the afternoon drove to Latchford where they inspected the Ship Canal, afterwards proceeding to Lymm where they had tea. On Monday morning they visited Messrs. Mc- Corquodale and Co's printing works. and Messrs. Gillespie and Mason's paper worka. In the after- noon the shooting contest, took place, the light being unfavourable. On the whole however the shooting was good ,and at the close Newton had won easily by a margin of 146. The Newton Majority at Douglas was 25. After the match a smoking con- cert was held at the Pied Bull Hotel, at which the Manx Volunteers were profuse in their expressions of praise of the hospitality accorded by the local detachment. A very pleasant evening was spent, but much regret was expressed at the departure of Sergeant Instructor Bird. THE SALVATION ARMY.</p>
        <p>In connection with the local corps No. 1253 of the Salvation Army, harvest thanksgiving services extending over four days were commenced on Friday last. For this special occasion Captain. Raper and Lieutenant Mutant, who were stationed at Earlestown when the corps was originated, were invited,and they conducted the services all through. The barracks had been profusely though neatly decorated with flowers, fruit, vegetables, and earn, which had been given by members and sympath- isers of the army, the decorations being carried out under the supervision of Captain Thomas and Lieu- tenant Morgan. The meeting on Friday night par- took of a welcome to Captain Raper and Lieutenant Murant, at which a large number of old friends were present, and it was of a most hearty and enthusiastic character. On Saturday evening there was an open-air meeting, and afterwards in the barracks a service of song entitled "God never makes mistakes," was given by the soldiers, con- ducted by Sergeant Major P. Evans, the connective readings being given by Mr. Glover. The band connected with the corps officiated, and all the members wore large straw hats decorated with Iflowers and corn, whilst the sisters ware white laprons. On Sunday morning the members assem- bled at 630 and led by the band had a march out. At 7 o'clock there was knee drill in the barracks, vsomething like 60 persons being present. At 10 o'clock there was an open-air meeting and at 11 o'clock a holiness meeting in the barracks. At 2 o'clock a junior company meeting was conducted in the barracks by Lientenent Mutant, Captain Raper at the same time I conducting an open-air meeting, afterwards march- ing to the barracks where Lieutenant Morgan re- cited " Sowing the wind, or the story of the back- slider." At 5.45 they again assembled on the Mar- ket ground, where an impressive service was held. :subsequently at the barracks a 'large crowd mus- tered, and at the close of the service two persons were led to the penitent form. On Monday after-, noon at. three o'clock a mother's meeting was held at the barracks, which was well attended. In the evening the local contingent was reinforced by a number of soldiers from Warrington, and a success- ful service was held on the Market ground. A band festival was afterwards held in the barracks room, several of toe Warrington contingent render- ing vocal pieces, whilst the band gave instrumental selections. Lieutenant Morgan recited, " Wanted a Minister." A sale of fruit and vegetables was afterwards held, in aid of the funds of the corps, and on Tuesday night there was a farewell meet- ing to Captain Raper and Lieutenant Murant,when three persons were led to the penitent form. Al- together about 1,200 persons attended the services, and the result financially and spiritually has been beyond expectations.</p>
        <p>NEWTON VOLUNTEER PRIZE MEETING. The annual prize meeting in connection with " G " (Newton) Company of the Ist V. B. Prince of Wales' Volunteers, S. L. Regt., was held on the High field Moss range on Saturday. The range was in charge of Sergt. Instructor Wooding, and the weather being in every way favourable, there was some good shooting. There were two competitions, one for the challenge cup, presented by Lieut. T. Hamer, and the other for recruits. The cup was won outright by Sergt. J. Tunstall. with the excel- lent total of 67 out of a possible 70, this being the second time in succession the, he has won it. The following are the results of the shooting : General competition : Sergt. J. Tunstall (cup and .£2 55.) 67; Sergt. James Tunstall (..£2) 65; Colour- Sergt. Dolan (XI 17s. 61.) 54; Private G. Atkinson , (.£1 155.), 51; Sergt. Instructor Woorling 124.). 49; Private J. Wickham (1 73. 61.), 47 : Corporal J. Atkinson (.21 55.), 47; Corporal S. Roberts (CO', 46; Privste Joseph Roberts (17s. 61.). 42; Private Goldsack (155.), 39 : Drummer Mullins (125.), 38 ; Private Marsh (10s. 61.). 36 ; Lance Corporal Nealer (103. 61.), 36; Private J. Kynaston (103. 61.‘„ 36 Private J. Atkinson (84.), 35 ; Private Onions: (74. 6(1.), 31; Private Large (7s. 641.), 33. There were several smaller prize which were won by .Privates, Melslip,. Holmes, Cooper, Roulson. A. Williaire, Lawson, Dutton, T.Creed,Taberder,Lloyd, Holland and J. Roberts. Reunite competition : Privates J. Dutton (104.) : A. Holmes, 83. ' • P.Fahey, 73. 61. i C. Crouthley, 64.; E. J. Wjlliams,s3. ; G.E. Barton, 53. ;H. Talhor, 40. 614 G.' Breverton, 33.6 d.; and A. Houghton, 3s. - - -</p>
        <p>WRINGING MA.CHINai. Gancana, ironmonger Warrington.</p>
        <p>1.0.G.T, EMILESTOWN EdENEZER LODGE, No, 752.</p>
        <p>This loklo held its usual weeKly session' on Tues- day, Bro. W. Hatton p esiding. After the usual business had been gone through three new members were initiated. The programme, single members , being responsible, was then proceeded with, songs, recitations, &amp;c., being given by the following : Bros. J. Junes, W Brown, A. Morris, and others. The Lodge was zlosed in the usual way, and the members afterwards partook of supper, which was provided by the single members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 2l, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 2l, 1895.</p>
        <p>„</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>VIADUCT INSTITUTE TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CLASSES.</head>
        <p>VIADUCT INSTITUTE TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The session in connection with these dlasses com- mences next week, and the syllabus which has been issued is a remarkable volume of information. The fullest particulars are given embracing exhaustive descriptions of the various subjects to be taught, together with the places of teaching, and the times at which the classes in the different courses are held. Besides the various arts and sciences, classes in domestic econony are to be held for ladies, in- cluding cookery, laundry, practical dressmaking, needlework, and sick nursing, awl efforts are being made to extend the classes to all parts of the district covered by the Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WARGRAVE CHURCH HARVEST FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>WARGRAVE CHURCH HARVEST FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>The St octal services of thank-giving for the in- gathering of the harvest, were commenced in the above church on Thursday evening. The church was neatly dicorated with fruit, fhwers, vegetables and corn, the decorations having been carried out by Mrs. Carson, the Misses Carson, Mrs. Coiling- wood, Mrs. Norman, Mrs. Thornpon, Miss Ratcliffe, Miss Laithwaite, Mr. R. Cbandley, and others. There was not a very large congregation, the pulpit being occupied by the Rev. J. Bridger vicar of Bamford, wig° in the course of his sermon, dwelt upon educational matters,and the voluntary system of education. At the close of the service Mr. J. Burman, who some years ago was organist at St. John's Church, Earlestown. played one or two selections on the organ, and also played for the c'ioir practice. The collections were in aid of the Wargrave day and Sunday Schools.</p>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE FROX DnowNiNG AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>1 On Tuesday afternoon the infant daughter of Mr. William Moss, living in Peel-street, Earlestown,had a narrow escape from being drowned in a dolly-tub. ale mother it appears was washing in the back kitchen,and went outside for a few minutes leaving the child which is only 15 months old playing about. When she returned she found the child had lallen head first into the dolly-tub which was about half full of water. She took it out, and sent for Dr. Valentine, who on arrival found it unconscious and at once commenced to restore animation, his efforts after a time proving succestful, and the youngster is now on the road to recovery.</p>
        <p>ATAL FALL DOWNSTAIRS AT THE VULCAN. On Wednesday, John Houghton,late of 16, Derby- row, Vulcan Cottages, Earlestown, died at the Warrington Infirmary from the effects of falling downstairs. On Saturday night, the 18th August, the deceased came home between 10 and 11 o'clock under the influence of drink and Went' to bed. About one o'clock next morning one of his eons heard him moving, and hearing an usual noise he went to ascertain the cause and found his father lying at the bottom of the stairs. The defieased said be got up for a cup of tea and had slipped down the stairs, hurting the back of his head, and his back. Dr. Mouncey was sent for, who ordered his removal to Warrington Infirmary, where he died, as stated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>"THE RETURN FROM CALVARY."</head>
        <p>"THE RETURN FROM CALVARY."</p>
        <p>SERMON BY THE REV. JOHN YONG&amp;</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the Rev. John Yonge delivered a special sermon in Wycliffe Church, Warrington, upon the subject afforded by the exhibition in the town of the beautiful picture by Schmalz, ontitled " The Return from Calvary." There was a large congregation. The rev. gentleman took for bis text John 19, verses 25 to 27 : "Now there stood by the Cross of Jesus, His mother and His mother's sister—Mary, the wife of Cleophas—and Mary Magdalena. When Jesus therefore saw His mother and the disciple standing by whom RP loved, He saith unto His mother, ' Woman ! behold thy Son.' Then saitb He to the disciple, ' Behold thy ' mother !' and from that hour that disciple took her unto his own home."</p>
        <p>_ _ Mr.Yosos commenced by saying that the remarkable and beautiful painting that was being exhibited in the town had been suggested to the artist by the words just quoted. Many of them, probably, would have already seen the picture, and would be mare or less susceptible to the significance and suggestiveness of a striking pic- ture. They learnt sometimes quicker and better through the eye than through the ear. He thought they owed a peculiar indebtedness to the great artists who had devoted their genius to the delineation of sacred scenes. The historic personages and scenes of Scripture, and supremely the person of Christ. and the events at his in- comparable life and death, had been the subjects of the highest art in all ages; and it was an incontestible proof that, at the end of this, the 19th century, the interest and faith and hope of men centre more than ever ar.und Cal- vary and its cross,and that our greatest modern artists were still intent upon the realization mart of the history of our Saviour's passion? The remarkable fact that up- wards of half a million persons had already stood before that one striking picture of " The Return from Calvary" spoke significantly of the undiminished, or rather increasing hold which Calvary had upon the imagination and faith of men. The artist told them that the subject had been growing 141 his mind for some years although for long he did not attempt to embody it even in a sketch, hut one day, while at work, he sud- denly realised the entire scene and therefore at once, he roughly outlined it. He Said further, that although after the viqion cf it, considerable time elapsed before RS exo, Caton, still the picture stood completed on the canvas, almost upon the identical outline of the first sketch. For the sake of those who had not seen it the rev, gentle- man gave a brief description of the picture, as follow,: 6, It is entitled *The Return from Calvary,' and deals with the pathetic incident of St. John's taking to his his own home the m-ther of Jesus after receiving from the lips of the dying master upon the cross, the sacred charge with which he cammitted His mother to his care. Properly, however, to comprehend the scene presented in the picture, would need a`most to take ourselves first of all in imagination to Calvery itself, to recall the spec- tacle of that most pathetic of all the groups which gathered there. 'There stood by the Cross of Jesus, His mother, and His Mother's sister—Mary, the wife of Cleonhas, and Mary Magdalene.' Now this ve.se may read in two ways—either as specifying three persons or four, as pres-nt in this group of women. The artist for the purpose of his picture, has concluded thit four and not three are specified—and that one of the four, the sister of the Virgia Marv, is left unnamed. If his read ing is correct, the probability, judging from othe names of women present at the Cross and given by other evangeliats is that the Virgin's sistcr, whose name is not inserted In this verse would be Selnme, the mother of James and John." . _ .</p>
        <p>Following this description, the rev. gentlensan in a mery eloquent manner proceeded to deal separately and at length with the feelings and motives of those present at the scene depicted. Then,he says,below the first three mentioned " we see spanning the city a triumphal arch set no by the Romans and through it is massing a mixed and motley crowd who have all been to Calvarr,and who seem the advanced ranks of a great procession which the eye can clearly trace all thr augh the city thoroughfare to the slopes of Calvary itself. On the tops of the houses also, as far as the eye can reach, are groups and clusters of figures, scanning the excited crowds below, and all strangely testifying that the Execution upon Calvary was of unrivalled interest and that the city had been s'irred their by. Farthest of allrawav on the hack. ground at the right of the picture stands the Bill of Calvary, bathed for the moment in a .trangels, lucid' light, and on the hill the three crosses, and in the midst the cross which had drawn with its mysterious magnet- ism, the city to its feet—and which from that day onwards until now has never ceased to attract and hold and rule and save the world."</p>
        <p>There were several lessons which the picture should convey, sad of which the rev.gentleman said they might profitabfy specify two. These were "The beautiful and Christian ministry of sympathy and succour for the help- less and the sad," and a different but equally suggestive lesson " The crowds of people that flocked to the Crucifixion were impressed, but they were saved." Never had a crucifixion wrought such a public feeling as that had. The feeling of a boundless significance was in the air, and the whole multitude was under its; spell. From the crowds which had viewed the picture the preacher erg •ed that the Cross was still magnetic. Christ still attracted the multitude. Men went with peculiar feeling to see art illustrating the Cross. The people stood beholding, and some of them, perhaps even many more than they imagieen,returned with christened thought, and even penitent and trustful faith. A.collection was taken during the evening on behalf of the church funds and this, together with one in the morning, realized over £l4.</p>
        <p>There is living in the almhouses at Leathlay, in Yo,k- shire, a, old lady, Elizabeth Watson, who has just cele- brated her hundredth birthday. She comes of a stock remark.tle for its Inbgevity. Her father, who was for nearly forty years coschman in the family of the Rev. George A Mt, rector of South Kirkby, died. it is said at the age Of 110, while his brother, a soldier who fought at Waterloo. died at the age of 115. Mrs. Watson's husband claimed to have a grandmother 108 yearn of age. She can thread a needle without the aid of spectacles, and is often out early in the morning gathering mushrooms, which she subsequently takes to market. Heavy mortality from cholera is reported in the Russian provinces of Volbynis and Podolia.</p>
        <p>L_LA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARS CON VENTION.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARS CON VENTION.</p>
        <p>The monthly session of No. 1 Convention 8. W. Lan- cashire of blood Templans Order was held in the Co- operative Hall, Earlestown, no Saturday,when there was a Mir attendance of representatives and visitors from various lodges in the Convention area. The Convention was preceded by a special session of the district lodge when the district degree was conferred upon lour mem- bers by Bro. A. Newell, D. Company, of Liverpool, as- sisted by other officers. The Convention was called to order at 3.45 by Bro. Quazterniaster-Sergeant Roberts, e.C.T., ot Warrington, after which Bro. Newell, D Corn- , panv was invited to preside. The usual press and credential committees having been appointed,the reports of officers were received. Bros. Roberts, C.C. F., and Newman, C. Sec., gave interesting reports on the posi- tion and prospects of the various lodges in the Ginivtii- tion area, several of which had shown considerable acti- vity in holding open air and indoor public meetings with g results. A similar course wss urged upon all as a means of improving, the order in the district. Bro. J. C.E.S., of Prescot, reported upon eit ctoral mat- ters,statin4 that though the recent general el ct.ou uatt not resulted as could nave been desired, yet, as temper- pi-rance workers, they must not lose Lea( ,but buckle on the armour afresh and keep pegging away, and in due time they would be successful. He I etefletl to cue agita- tion in various quarters against the tied house system, and showed also that in mauy recent licensing sessions there was a disposition to eards a furtuer reouction iu ties number of licenses. Bro. A. Francis, C.S.J.T., gave an excellent report on the juvenile section, giving an indication of more energetic efforts in prosecut.ng the work, but strongly urged a greater use ut the taacsuoard for inculcating temperauce prlueipl saw n..;st the young folks, and concluded by asking for a committee to be appointed to assist him towards more succ,•:-sful opera- tions. This was adopted aud a coininu,ee elected con- sistiug of Bros. Francis, Roberts, Flaherty, Sisters Mrs. Nurse, and daresnare. It was decided to liold the next meeting of the Convention at St. Helena in October. It was resolved to ask the district executivs to grant a special session to the district lodge on that occasion,and that Bro. Newell, D.C., be invited to read a paper for the good of the order. Thanks were tendered to the Hope of Earlestown for their arrangements for the session. Bro. Newman, C. Sec., next read a paper, as arranged, on " How to improve our lodge, and conven- tiun sessiou," and gave a number of useful and interest- ing ideas in promotion of the object indiceed,and at the close a general discussion followed, worKs beim; ac- corded Bro. Newman, and to Bro. Newell tor his visit, and the Convention was afterwards closed.</p>
        <p>I A public tea was subsequently held, and a large num- ber attended. At the close an entertainment was given in the large hall, before a good audience. Bro. J. Cord- ingly presided, and the following programme was sub- mitted :—Male glee, "The sailor's chorus," Messrs. Mawdsley, Kaye and Lea ; song, "The wolf," Mr. W. Kaye ; song, "Star of Bethlehem," Miss Roberts; song, " Anchored." Mr. C. H. Lea; humorous song, "Enoch Brown," Mr. J. Eluddlestou ; piano solo. "overture from 'Tancredy," Mr. E. Leyland ; song, "Three young maids of Leigh," Miss Roberts ; duet. "Excelsior," Messrs. Leyland and Kaye; song, " the BaLddlero," Mr. C. H. Lea; humorous song, "Never to return again," Mr. J. Huddleston ; song, "Thy sentinel am I," Mr. Kaye; song, " Twickenham Ferry ," Miss Roberts; male glee, "Comrades in arms," Messrs. Mawdsley, Huddleston, Lea and Kaye. An address was also given by Bro. A. Newell ot Liverpool, and thanks were tendered to the Chairman and speaker, WI the motion of Bro. G. Clarke and Bro. J. Hatton.</p>
      </div>
