      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>FRODSIIAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSIIAM.</p>
        <p>A FORGETFUL CLERGYMAN.—At Overton, Frodsham, on Monday afternoon, the relatives of the late Mr. William Saunders, age 79 years, of Bedford Leigh, Lancashire, were subject o a most unpleasant experience on the oc- casiou of the funeral. The time fixed for the interment was four o'clocktbut as the clergyman did not put in an appearance messengers were despatched in search of hen. They did not however succeed in their quest, and as it was now long alter the time for the appointed funeral, the Rev. F. F. Edmonds,Wesleyan minister,who resides in the locality was approactied,and he generously consented to perform the ceremony. Some time after- wards the missing clergyman put in an appearance at the churchyard and offered his apologies for having quite forgotten his engagement. WEDDANG.—it Overton I arish Church, on Wednesday afternoon, Miss Emily Mary Riley, second daughter of Mr. Thomas Riley, of Frodshaen, was married to Mr. Frank Arthur Dibbs, professor of music, and organist, only son of Mr. Edward Dibbs, of the Roiliest New Ferry. ehe Ito/. H. B. Blogg was the officiating clergyman. A large congregation assembled to witness the ceremory, the bride being exceedingly well known in Frodsham and district. Miss Eliza Davies was bridesmaid, and Mr. Price of New Ferry occupied the position of best man. As the party left the church, Mr. Charles R. Riley played Mendelasolm's well kuovin "Wedding March.", The newly-married couple were the recipients of numerous presents. PARISI! COUNCIL MEETINO.—A meeting of the Fred- sham Parish Council was held on Friday evening, under the presidency of Mr. Heywood, when the question of the boundaries between the township and lordship was considered. The Chairman read a communication from the Lordship Coancil, from which it appeared that that body refused to have anything to do with the alteration of the present boundaries. A letter was read froma re- sident at Saltport, calling the attention :of. the Council to the removal of planks which were put down by the Manchester Ship Canal Company some time ago for , carrying pedestrians over the ditches or dykes in the meadows leading to Saltport from Frodsham, when it was decided that notices be issued threatening proceed- , ings against anyone caught removing such planks in future. A small committee was appointed to look after the footpaths, and to do what was tummy in the mat- ter.—Tne minutes were confirmed at the meeting of the Rural Council on Tuesday. Falwell= Costaterree.—The monthly meeting of the Frt,dsham Parish Wuncil, acting as the Parochial Com- mittee, was held on Friday evening, at the Court-room, Frodsham. Mr. H. E. Heywood presided. Inspector Fdrnogton reported upon premises which were still ' without a water supply from the water main, and the , Clerk was instructed to write to the Misses Ashley that I a sampte of water would be taken for analysis. The Inspector was authorised to obtain tenders for, laying on water in connection with certain premises belonging to Mr. Thomas Davies and Mr. Gilbert Sharrock. A tender was submitted for needful work to premises belonging to Mr. Walker, when it was resolved that Mr. F. W. Spencer he instructed to proceed with the work.—A resolution was read from the Pariah Council of Frodsham Lordship requesting the Township Council to lay on water in the lordship, when it was decided that Messrs. Timmons, of Runceru, he requested to give an estimate of what the coat would be, and that a special meeting be. called to consider it.—A tender was accepted from the Frodsham Gas and Water .Company for the erection of nine addi- tional lamps, which were to be placed in Fluin-lane and Red-lane.—At a meeting of the Rural Council on Tues- day, the minute, were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NOTES ON ciAM.ES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON ciAM.ES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>FLIGHT FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Warrington club supporters were woefully dis- appointed on Saturday with the result of the Hull match. Instead of bagging a couple of points and improving their point averages, Warrington received a beating of nine points to three, and the point average is on the wrong side by eight for and 13 against. The only satis- factory thing about it is that Warrington were not beaten pointless.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team did not by any means play up to the form they displayed against Hunslet. If they had, the result would have been much different. The Hull team also were much too vigorous in their play, to say the least of it, and were too fond of going for the men instead of the ball. In fact in this respect they sur- prised some of their supporters who averred that they had never seen Hull play like that before. One outburst in particular was very reprehensible and that was when Jacketts sent Barber flying to the ground, although nei- ther of them were near the ball.</p>
        <p>I will, however, leave these unpleasant topics and get to the game. The department that Warrington were most decisively beaten in was the forward one. The Hull forwards are not very heavy, but they were mach smarter than their opponents,who seemed to lack the "go that was necessary. The committee have re- cognised this weakness of the team,and it will be noticed that some radical changes have been made.</p>
        <p>In other departments, Warrington were in no way be- hind band. Bate and Foden were equal to the other two, albeit C. Townsend required some watching. By the way, Foden is fast getting the reputation of a scorer. There have only been two tries scored yet by Warring- ti..n this season, and Foden has scored them both. It is to be hoped that he will oblige again to-day at Bradford. Two or three tries against the crack Yorkshire team will not be unwelcome. The try he scored on Saturday was obtained from a sudden dive from a line out, and he de- serves every credit for it.</p>
        <p>The three quarter line was very much handicapped by the beaten forwards, Barber and Burton played in their usual dashing style, but the former was very unlucky in the number of knocks he got. O'Brien would make a good centre if be were a little cooler. But perhaps he will get over this shortly.Broady made his first turn out for the season, and was of course not quite in his best form. He misjudged the ball once or twice, but he was in good kicking form, and the way he got touch was very welcome to the forwards. He is also a dangerous customer if he gets going as was shown by one sprint he made on Saturday. Boscow was all there as usual, and in fact never seems out of form. He payed a good game on Saturday and promises well for the future.</p>
        <p>In the Northern Union naatclaes on Saturday, there were six home teams victorious, and five defeated. Strange to say that out of the 22 matches already played there has not been one draw. In seven games Lancashire and Yorkshire clubs were pitted against each other, and in four of these the Yorkshire men were victorious, and in three, the Lancastrians. So far, the two counties have shown themselves remarkably even in the number of wins and losses.</p>
        <p>There were some very interesting—in fact astounding results in the eleven matches. Flunslet had to knuckle under to Warrington on the first Saturday of the season, and yet on the following Saturday, they actually beat Oldham by three goals and a try to a goal and a try. This is a bad start for the " Owdamers," and they will have to shape better than that. Tyldesley bad made short work of Manningham the previous Saturday, but Bradford the neighbours of the latter beat the "Bongers" by the goal kick. Brighouse Rangers, whom a good many fancy for the championship, and Wigan who also expect to have at least an outside chance, have still to have the first score made against them. In fact with the exception of Huddersfield who have only played one match, they are the only teams which have kept their lines intact.</p>
        <p>Poor old Wakefield Trinity are doing no better now than they have done in recent years, and while they have 21 points scored against them, they have not a single point to their credit. The only other club who are in this plight are, strange to say, Liversedge. This team was the champion team of Yorkshire last year, and is noted for its free open play.</p>
        <p>There are some het games to-day, and chief among these is the one between Oldham and Tyldesley at Old- ham. It would be extremely difficult to name the winner of this encounter, but Tyldesley have shown the best form thus tar. There will be a general exchange of Lancashire and Cheshire clubs with Yorkshire clubs to-day. Warrington, Wigan, Widnes and St. Helens, each go to Yorkshire to meet Bradford,Wskefield Trinity, Halifax and Batley respectively, while Broughton Rangers, Leigh and Stockport are" at home" to Hud- dersfield, Manningham and flunslet. Rochdale Hornets will go to Runcorn where they will most probably fail to get their first points in the table, while Leeds and Hull will meet Brighouse Rangers and Liversedge. The League table should therefore be very considerably altered to-day.</p>
        <p>Warrington of course go to Bradford, where they will make their debilt. The Bradford team is in good form and will take some beating. The following will repre- sent Warrington : Boscow, full-back; Barber, O'Brien, Burton and Broady, three-quarters; Bate and Foden, half backs; Dakin, Swift, Berry, Burrows, Callaghan, Nevins, Bailey and Buckley, forwards.</p>
        <p>The "A 'team make their bow to-day and will meet the Bradford " A "teat. It is reported that the mem- bers of the team are in capital form having been prac- tising much during the past week or two, and it is expected that they will give their opponents plenty of stepping about.</p>
        <p>There is a probability that in the near future, some important alterations will be made in the game of the Northern Union Clubs. The proposal to reduce the for- ' wards by two will, if adopted, make the game much more open and enjoyable from a spectator's point of view, but the players will have much more to do. It is also prop sed to abolish the line out, and this would be an unquestionable improvement. One alteration which has been mentioned will probably be looked at rather doubtfully by some old Rugby enthusiasts. This is the proposal to have a round ball instead of the present oval shaped one. There is no doubt that a round ball would give a better opportunity for dribbling with the feet,and thereby make the Rugby game a more open one.</p>
        <p>The table of the positone of the Northern League up to Friday of this week is as follows :</p>
