      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</p>
        <p>WEST AFRICAN ESTATES.</p>
        <p>A Company with the name of the West African Estates and Development Company, Limited, has been attracting some attention from speculative investors, owing to the price a the shares being only 35., while some very satis- factory items have oeesi gleaned respecting its condition. The property consists of about eight square miles, and is only some seven miles from the coast on the Ancobra River. It is said to be honey-combed with native shafts, which is taken as an indication that there is gold, as the natives never consider anything worth working that does not yield at least 3 oz. to the ton by their primitive method of hand-crushing. The quartz is of acrystalline nature, and is generally free milling. Deep level work- ing will prove ultimately the most profitable; but the surface work—say, to 30 feet—will be able to feed the mills with 25-thvt. stone for years to come. There is a creek running through the property which gives water carriage absolutely from Liverpool to the spot, an enor- mous advantage when the carriage of heavy machinery is considered. The mines working on the Gold Coast find the expenses of mining and milling amount to about 20s. per ton. This amount should be reduced in the case of the West African Estates to about half on account of the splendid facilities for carriage,there being practically no land transport necessary. _ . . .</p>
        <p>addition to these points, the Company possesses all rigits as to minerals and forestry; and as the property is drnsely covered with timber this is a point to bear in mini!. From the mahogany that is on the estate it is estirn tted there will be a profit of over £300,000 after Living all expenses, and taking the selling price at only 2s. 6d. per cubic foot, which is a low estimate, as the price occasionally reaches a higher level. rho shares as a speculative investment appear to promise fairly well.</p>
        <p>"The Bullionist," September 14th, 1895.</p>
        <p>BEACONSFIELD DIAMOND MINES.</p>
        <p>There is no reason why an investor should not prefer a share yielding over 25 per cent, in a thoroughly sound undertaking to a share of a larger corporation paying 10 per cent. De Beers diamond shares at the pros sent price do not yield 8 per cent. Beaconsfield share at existing quotations should produce more than 50 pea cent. Considering that reports lately received showed that the Company was winning diamonds at a rate suffi- cient to give a return of upwards of 60 per cent, on the paid up capital which is a return of over 30 per cent. based on the present price of £2 per share fully paid up. In four weeks the net profits were £4,290. Seeing that the output is increasing and the quality of the stones improving there is in store for, the Beaconsfield share- holders growing dividends with far higher prices in the immediate future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>JACKSON GOLDFIELDS.</head>
        <p>JACKSON GOLDFIELDS.</p>
        <p>The gold mines owned by the Jackson Company are surrounded by celebrated paying gold properties. They cover an area of 90 acres, have been developed by ex. tensive workings and at the 200 feet. level a new and Ilarge body of ore has b2en struck in going down on the lode. The gold is free milling and the ore easy of treat- ment. The plant already on the property is ample,eom- ,! prising aBO stamp mill and all necessary machinery 1 which is driven entirely by water power of which there is an unlimited supply. Few mines possess such aovan- tages as the Jackson Goldfields. The ore is reliable and abundant and the mines can be worked at great profit, as the cost of raising and treating the gold quartz is un- usually low and the organization set on foot has had due regard to large output and great economy. Jackson shares are now quoted on the London Stock Exchange at 3s. per share fully paid and are singularly cheap both for investment and speculation.</p>
        <p>-- Mr. Andrew Robert Hammond, the Managing Director of the Hammonds Matabele Gold Mines Development, sails for South Africa in the course of a few days. Mr. Hammond takes with him the necessary plant machinery and equipment for the purpose of opening up the exten- sive mining area belonging to the Company. This pro- perty consists of 150 claims situated on the North and South Banks of the Gwelo river comprising five distinct blocks containing 4i miles of reef. Assays and pan- flings have already been made on the line of reef from the ancient workings yielding 4 to 7 ounces .of gold per ton. Active dealings in these shares have taken place on the London Stock Exchange and there has been a strong demand for them, the latest quotation being 93. 3d. to 9s. 9d.</p>
        <p>CALEDONIAN GOLD MINING COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The White Feather district of Coolgardie Goldfield grows in favour as a rich mineral-producing ground, and the " Caledonian "is perhaps the gem of the place. The original reports were of a highly favourable character, and the developments which are taking place give ample confirmation to all that the experts reported. The manager announced fine specimens rich in gold in May last. A cable of July 24 stated that the main leader had been struck at a depth of 40 ft., and development was opening.out most satisfactorily. The ground on the north boundary was opening up splendidly. The mana- ger writes, July 30, that work has been pushed on vigor- ously at Shaft No. 4, and the reef had been definitely traced for a considerable distance. Excellent samples of quartz had been obtained showing coarse visible gold, estimated to run 6 oz. to the ton. The shaft on the northern boundary was to be pushed on, and if it realises the expectations of the manager the Caledonian is likely to be one of the richest properties in the neigh- bourhood, if not absolutely the richest. August 6: A cable message reports developments on north boundary opening up splendidly. September 6: A further message states that in shaft No. 1 northern boundary splendid samples have been obtained. In No. 4 shaft the quartz shows visible gold. The value of the mine is increasing every day, and with only a little more progress the shares will soon leave their present quotation of 4s. to 4s. 6d. far behind. They ought, in fact, to be double that before the present account runs out.—" Capitalist,' Sept. 14.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-09-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>MURCHISON DIAMOND MINES.</head>
        <p>MURCHISON DIAMOND MINES.</p>
        <p>The directors have received a cable message from the mine stating that No. 1 shaft is now in course of sink- ing wad development will proceed without delay. The manager is very much pleased with the favourable pros- pects, and intends to have a washing as early as pos- sible. fie states that there is every indication of a large yield of diamonds. This is a very satisfactory report, encouraging the belief in a bright future for the under- taking. The people of the locality have a profound faith in the value of the diamond field on which the Murchi- son leases are situated, and the proprietor of one well- known mine now in course of active development has set his heart upon relegating Kimberley from its high position as the greatest diamond producer of the world. Captain Rogers is certain that he can produce 8i carats of gems from each ton of wash dirt, while De Beers yield is abont or under 1 carat per load. The mine which Captain Rogers owns and is now developing is knc.wn as the Monte Christ°. If the ideas of the captain are realised we may find one fine day that the Diamond' King of South Africa has been deposed, and his king- dom has passed away. Then it will be New South Wales h 5 premier producer, and the Murchison and Monte Christ° running neck :and neck fcr the crown. Captain Rogers says the Murchison mine is ha identical ground with the Monte Christo. The shares have been actively dealt in during the week, and close at 4s. 3d. to 4s. 9d.—" Capitalist," Sept. 14.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Police Court, on Wednesday,Lawrence Cressy, licensee of the premises 16, and 18. Bevington- hill, was fined £lO and costs for permitting drunkenness, and his licence was ordered to be endorsed.</p>
        <p>Geoffrey Atherstein Perkins, described as an rAmerican barrister, was sentenced to ten years' penal servitude at the Old Bailey, London. on Wednesday, for attempting to blackmail the Rey. F. Williams,ot BettiscOmbe,Dorset. The prisoner had alleged that he had in his possession information as to the treatment of a young girl named Hsndsford by Mr. Williams' son •which would cause a great scandal if it were known, but that he was pre- pared to hush the matter up on payment of a certain sum.</p>
      </div>
